3 Annotate each step
For each step, note the definition, theorem, or algebraic rule that justifies it. A brief annotation to the right of the line is usually enough. Annotations make the proof checkable, and they teach the reader which tool to reach for next time.
For example, here is a derivation that the hat matrix \(\mathbf{H} = \mathbf{X}({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\) is idempotent, \(\mathbf{H}^2 = \mathbf{H}\), with each step annotated:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{H}^2
&= \mathbf{H}\mathbf{H}
&& \text{(definition of a matrix power)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X}({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\,\mathbf{X}({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{H} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X}({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}
&& \text{(regroup; matrix multiplication is associative)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X}\,\mathbf{I}_p\,({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}
&& \text{(a matrix inverse times the matrix is } \mathbf{I}_p \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X}({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}
&& \text{(the identity matrix changes nothing)}
\\
&= \mathbf{H}
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{H} \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
The Linear Algebra page uses this derivation to show that \(\mathbf{H}\) is a projection matrix. The probability notes derive the variance identity \({\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(X\right)\mathclose{}} = {\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X^2\right]\mathclose{}} - {\mathopen{}\left({\mathbb{E}\mathopen{}\left[X\right]\mathclose{}}\right)\mathclose{}}^{2}\) in the same annotated style.

8 Proof by induction
Many proofs in these notes show that a statement holds for every natural number \(n\), or for every integer \(n\) from some starting value on. Checking the statement one \(n\) at a time would never finish; induction proves it for all of them with two arguments.






DefDefinition 32 (Proof by induction)






Definition 32 (Proof by induction) Let \(n_0\) be an integer, and let \(P(n)\) be a predicate about the integers \(n \ge n_0\). A proof by induction [27] on \(n\) shows that \(P(n)\) holds for every integer \(n \ge n_0\) by showing two things:


Base case: \(P(n_0)\) holds.

Inductive step: for every integer \(n &gt; n_0\), if \(P(n - 1)\) holds, then \(P(n)\) holds.

In the inductive step, the assumption that \(P(n - 1)\) holds is called the induction hypothesis.










RemarkRemark 2 (Why the two steps suffice)






Remark 2 (Why the two steps suffice). The base case gives \(P(n_0)\). The inductive step with \(n = n_0 + 1\) then gives \(P(n_0 + 1)\), with \(n = n_0 + 2\) it gives \(P(n_0 + 2)\), and so on: each integer \(n \ge n_0\) is reached after \(n - n_0\) uses of the inductive step.
When \(P(n)\) is needed only for \(n_0 \le n \le r\), it is enough to show the inductive step for \(n_0 &lt; n \le r\); the same chain then stops at \(P(r)\).




The following example uses summation notation.






ExmExample 34 (Sum of the first \(n\) natural numbers)






Example 34 (Sum of the first \(n\) natural numbers) For every \(n \in \mathbb{N}\),
\[
\sum_{i=1}^ni = \frac{n(n+1)}{2}.
\]
For example, with \(n = 3\), \(1 + 2 + 3 = 6\) and \(\frac{3 \cdot 4}{2} = 6\). To prove the equation for every \(n\), let \(P(n)\) be the equation and use Definition 32 with \(n_0 = 1\).
Base case, \(n = 1\). The sum has one term:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{i=1}^{1} i
&= 1
&& \text{(definition of a sum)}
\end{aligned}
\]
and the right side is
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{1\,(1+1)}{2}
&= \frac{1 \cdot 2}{2}
&& \text{(} 1 + 1 = 2 \text{)}
\\
&= \frac{2}{2}
&& \text{(} 1 \cdot 2 = 2 \text{)}
\\
&= 1.
&& \text{(} \tfrac{2}{2} = 1 \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
So \(P(1)\) holds.
Inductive step. Let \(n \ge 2\), and suppose \(P(n - 1)\) holds:
\[
\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} i = \frac{(n-1)\,\mathopen{}\left((n-1)+1\right)\mathclose{}}{2}.
\]
Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{i=1}^ni
&= 1 + 2 + \cdots + (n-1) + n
&& \text{(definition of a sum)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left(1 + 2 + \cdots + (n-1)\right)\mathclose{} + n
&& \text{(associative law)}
\\
&= \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} i + n
&& \text{(definition of a sum)}
\\
&= \frac{(n-1)\,\mathopen{}\left((n-1)+1\right)\mathclose{}}{2} + n
&& \text{(induction hypothesis)}
\\
&= \frac{(n-1)\,n}{2} + n
&& \text{(} (n-1) + 1 = n \text{)}
\\
&= \frac{(n-1)\,n}{2} + \frac{2n}{2}
&& \text{(} n = \tfrac{2n}{2} \text{)}
\\
&= \frac{(n-1)\,n + 2n}{2}
&& \text{(common denominator)}
\\
&= \frac{\mathopen{}\left((n-1) + 2\right)\mathclose{}\,n}{2}
&& \text{(distributive law)}
\\
&= \frac{(n+1)\,n}{2}
&& \text{(} (n-1) + 2 = n + 1 \text{)}
\\
&= \frac{n(n+1)}{2},
&& \text{(commutative law)}
\end{aligned}
\]
which is \(P(n)\). By Definition 32, the equation holds for every \(n \in \mathbb{N}\).

Some special matrices appeared earlier:

the zero matrix (Definition 4 in Matrices), in the section on matrix addition
square matrices (Definition 8 in Matrices), in the section on square and identity matrices
the identity matrix (Definition 12 in Matrices), in that same section







DefDefinition 1 (Order of a square matrix)






Definition 1 (Order of a square matrix) The order of a square matrix (Definition 8 in Matrices) is its number of rows, which equals its number of columns.










ExmExample 1 (Orders of square matrices)






Example 1 (Orders of square matrices) \(\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{bmatrix}\) has order \(2\), and \([5]\) has order \(1\). A \(2 \times 3\) matrix has no order, since it is not square (Example 8 in Matrices).










DefDefinition 2 (Matrix power)






Definition 2 (Matrix power) For a square matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) of order \(p\) and a positive integer \(k\), the \(k\)-th power of \(\mathbf{A}\) is:
\[\mathbf{A}^k = \underbrace{\mathbf{A}\,\mathbf{A}\cdots\mathbf{A}}_{k \text{ copies}}\]
In particular, \(\mathbf{A}^2 = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{A}\).










ExmExample 2 (Powers of a \(2 \times 2\) matrix)






Example 2 (Powers of a \(2 \times 2\) matrix) Let \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}^2
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 0 & 1 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 1 \\ 0 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 0 & 0 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 1 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of matrix multiplication)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix},
&& \text{(multiply and add)}
\\
\mathbf{A}^3 = \mathbf{A}^2 \mathbf{A}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \cdot 1 + 2 \cdot 0 & 1 \cdot 1 + 2 \cdot 1 \\ 0 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 0 & 0 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 1 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of matrix multiplication)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 3 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}.
&& \text{(multiply and add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
A \(2 \times 3\) matrix \(\mathbf{B}\) is not square (Definition 8 in Matrices), so \(\mathbf{B}^2\) is not defined; indeed \(\mathbf{B} \mathbf{B}\) would multiply a matrix with \(3\) columns by one with \(2\) rows, which Definition 7 in Matrices does not allow.










DefDefinition 3 (Symmetric matrix)






Definition 3 (Symmetric matrix) A square matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) is symmetric [1] if \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top} = \mathbf{A}\), i.e., \(a_{ij} = a_{ji}\) for all \(i\) and \(j\).










RemarkRemark 1 (Symmetric matrices in statistics)






Remark 1 (Symmetric matrices in statistics). Covariance matrices are symmetric: entry \((i, j)\) of the covariance matrix of a random vector \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{Y}}\vphantom{Y}}\) is \({\operatorname{Cov}\mathopen{}\left(Y_i, Y_j\right)\mathclose{}}\), and \({\operatorname{Cov}\mathopen{}\left(Y_i, Y_j\right)\mathclose{}} = {\operatorname{Cov}\mathopen{}\left(Y_j, Y_i\right)\mathclose{}}\). For example, if \({\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(Y_1\right)\mathclose{}} = 4\), \({\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(Y_2\right)\mathclose{}} = 9\), and \({\operatorname{Cov}\mathopen{}\left(Y_1, Y_2\right)\mathclose{}} = 1\), the covariance matrix of \((Y_1, Y_2)\) is
\[
\begin{bmatrix} 4 & 1 \\ 1 & 9 \end{bmatrix}.
\]










ExmExample 3 (A matrix that is not symmetric)






Example 3 (A matrix that is not symmetric) \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{bmatrix}\) is not symmetric:
\[
\begin{aligned}
a_{12} &= 2 \\
&\ne 3 \\
&= a_{21}.
\end{aligned}
\]










DefDefinition 4 (Diagonal matrix)






Definition 4 (Diagonal matrix) A square matrix \(\mathbf{D}\) is a diagonal matrix [2] if all off-diagonal entries are zero: \(d_{ij} = 0\) whenever \(i \neq j\):
\[
\mathbf{D} =
\begin{bmatrix}
d_1    & 0      & \cdots & 0      \\
0      & d_2    & \cdots & 0      \\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
0      & 0      & \cdots & d_p
\end{bmatrix}
\]










RemarkRemark 2 (\(\operatorname{diag}\) notation)






Remark 2 (\(\operatorname{diag}\) notation). For numbers \(d_1, \ldots, d_p\), \(\operatorname{diag}(d_1, \ldots, d_p)\) is the \(p \times p\) diagonal matrix with diagonal entries \(d_1, \ldots, d_p\). For example,
\[
\operatorname{diag}(2, 5) = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\ 0 & 5 \end{bmatrix},
\]
and the \(p \times p\) identity matrix is \(\mathbf{I}_p = \operatorname{diag}(1, \ldots, 1)\).










ExmExample 4 (A matrix that is not diagonal)






Example 4 (A matrix that is not diagonal) \(\mathbf{D} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 0 & 5 \end{bmatrix}\) is not diagonal: its off-diagonal entry \(d_{12} = 1 \ne 0\).










DefDefinition 5 (Matrix inverse)






Definition 5 (Matrix inverse) For a square \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\), the inverse \(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\) (if it exists) is the unique matrix satisfying:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}\,\mathbf{A}^{-1}
&= \mathbf{A}^{-1}\,\mathbf{A} \\
&= \mathbf{I}_p
\end{aligned}
\]










DefDefinition 6 (Invertible matrix, non-singular matrix)






Definition 6 (Invertible matrix, non-singular matrix) A \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) is invertible [3] (or non-singular) if some \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{B}\) satisfies
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}
&= \mathbf{B}\mathbf{A} \\
&= \mathbf{I}_p,
\end{aligned}
\]
where \(\mathbf{I}_p\) is the identity matrix (Definition 12 in Matrices). A square matrix that is not invertible is singular.










ExmExample 5 (An invertible matrix and a singular one)






Example 5 (An invertible matrix and a singular one) The matrix \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) is invertible, with \(\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & -0.5 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\): multiplying out gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}\mathbf{A}^{-1} &= \mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{A} \\
&= \mathbf{I}_2.
\end{aligned}
\]
The matrix \(\mathbf{M} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) is singular: for any \(2 \times 2\) matrix \(\mathbf{C}\), the two rows of \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}\) are equal, so \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}\) can never be \(\mathbf{I}_2\), whose two rows differ.










RemarkRemark 3 (Invertible matrices and inverses)






Remark 3 (Invertible matrices and inverses). Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be a \(p \times p\) matrix, and suppose that two \(p \times p\) matrices \(\mathbf{B}\) and \(\mathbf{C}\) satisfy
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} &= \mathbf{B}\mathbf{A} \\
&= \mathbf{I}_p
\end{aligned}
\]
and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}\mathbf{C} &= \mathbf{C}\mathbf{A} \\
&= \mathbf{I}_p.
\end{aligned}
\]
Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{B}
&= \mathbf{B}\,\mathbf{I}_p
&& \text{(identity matrix)}
\\
&= \mathbf{B}(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{C})
&& \text{(} \mathbf{A}\mathbf{C} = \mathbf{I}_p \text{)}
\\
&= (\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A})\mathbf{C}
&& \text{(matrix multiplication is associative)}
\\
&= \mathbf{I}_p\,\mathbf{C}
&& \text{(} \mathbf{B}\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{I}_p \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{C}
&& \text{(identity matrix)}
\end{aligned}
\]
The steps use Theorem 5 in Matrices and Theorem 8 in Matrices. Since \(\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{C}\), at most one matrix satisfies these equations, which is the uniqueness that Definition 5 asserts. When \(\mathbf{A}\) is invertible, the matrix \(\mathbf{B}\) in Definition 6 is therefore the inverse \(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\) of \(\mathbf{A}\), and \(\mathbf{A}\) is invertible exactly when it has an inverse. For example, for \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) in Example 5, \(\begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & -0.5 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) is the only \(2 \times 2\) matrix \(\mathbf{B}\) with
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} &= \mathbf{B}\mathbf{A} \\
&= \mathbf{I}_2,
\end{aligned}
\]
so it is \(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\).










LemTheorem 1 (Inverse of a product)






Theorem 1 (Inverse of a product) If \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{B}\) are invertible \(p \times p\) matrices, then \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}\) is invertible, and
\[(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})^{-1} = \mathbf{B}^{-1}\mathbf{A}^{-1}\]










ProofProof






Proof. Multiply \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}\) by the candidate inverse on the right:
\[
\begin{aligned}
(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})(\mathbf{B}^{-1}\mathbf{A}^{-1})
&= \mathbf{A}(\mathbf{B}\mathbf{B}^{-1})\mathbf{A}^{-1}
&& \text{(matrix multiplication is associative)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}\,\mathbf{I}_p\,\mathbf{A}^{-1}
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{B}^{-1} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}\mathbf{A}^{-1}
&& \text{(identity matrix)}
\\
&= \mathbf{I}_p
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{A}^{-1} \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
and on the left:
\[
\begin{aligned}
(\mathbf{B}^{-1}\mathbf{A}^{-1})(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})
&= \mathbf{B}^{-1}(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{A})\mathbf{B}
&& \text{(matrix multiplication is associative)}
\\
&= \mathbf{B}^{-1}\,\mathbf{I}_p\,\mathbf{B}
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{A}^{-1} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{B}^{-1}\mathbf{B}
&& \text{(identity matrix)}
\\
&= \mathbf{I}_p
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{B}^{-1} \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
So \(\mathbf{B}^{-1}\mathbf{A}^{-1}\) satisfies Definition 5 for \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}\). The steps use Theorem 5 in Matrices and Theorem 8 in Matrices.










LemTheorem 2 (Inverse of a transpose)






Theorem 2 (Inverse of a transpose) If \(\mathbf{A}\) is an invertible \(p \times p\) matrix, then \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\) is invertible, and
\[{\mathopen{}\left({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\right)\mathclose{}}^{-1} = {\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}\]










ProofProof






Proof. Multiply \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\) by the candidate inverse on each side:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\,{\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}
&= {\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{A}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}
&& \text{(transpose of a product)}
\\
&= {\mathbf{I}_p}^{\top}
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{A}^{-1} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{I}_p
&& \text{(} \mathbf{I}_p \text{ is symmetric)}
\end{aligned}
\]
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{A}}^{\top}
&= {\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{A}^{-1}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}
&& \text{(transpose of a product)}
\\
&= {\mathbf{I}_p}^{\top}
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{A}^{-1} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{I}_p
&& \text{(} \mathbf{I}_p \text{ is symmetric)}
\end{aligned}
\]
So \({\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}\) satisfies Definition 5 for \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\). The first step of each display is Theorem 17 in Matrices.










ExmExample 6 (Inverting a transpose)






Example 6 (Inverting a transpose) For \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\), Example 5 gives \(\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & -0.5 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\), so Theorem 2 says
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathopen{}\left({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\right)\mathclose{}}^{-1}
&= {\mathopen{}\left(\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\right)\mathclose{}}^{-1} \\
&= {\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & 0 \\ -0.5 & 1 \end{bmatrix},
\end{aligned}
\]
and multiplying \(\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & 0 \\ -0.5 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) out does give \(\mathbf{I}_2\).










CorCorollary 1 (The inverse of a symmetric matrix is symmetric)






Corollary 1 (The inverse of a symmetric matrix is symmetric) If \(\mathbf{A}\) is symmetric and invertible, then \(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\) is symmetric.










ProofProof






Proof. \[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}
&= {\mathopen{}\left({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\right)\mathclose{}}^{-1}
&& \text{(inverse of a transpose)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}^{-1}
&& \text{(} \mathbf{A} \text{ is symmetric)}
\end{aligned}
\]
The first step is Theorem 2.










ExmExample 7 (Inverting a symmetric matrix)






Example 7 (Inverting a symmetric matrix) \(\mathbf{S} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) is symmetric, and \(\mathbf{S}^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 \\ -1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) is symmetric too, as Corollary 1 says.










LemTheorem 3 (An invertible factor can be cancelled)






Theorem 3 (An invertible factor can be cancelled) Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be an invertible \(p \times p\) matrix (Definition 6), and let \(\mathbf{B}\) and \(\mathbf{C}\) be \(p \times n\) matrices. If \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{C}\), then \(\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{C}\).










ProofProof






Proof. \[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{B}
&= \mathbf{I}_p\,\mathbf{B}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= (\mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{A})\,\mathbf{B}
&& \text{(}\href{#def-matrix-inverse}{\text{Definition~5}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}^{-1}(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}^{-1}(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{C})
&& \text{(} \mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{C} \text{)}
\\
&= (\mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{A})\,\mathbf{C}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{I}_p\,\mathbf{C}
&& \text{(}\href{#def-matrix-inverse}{\text{Definition~5}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{C}.
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
\(\square\)










CorCorollary 2 (An invertible factor can be cancelled on the right)






Corollary 2 (An invertible factor can be cancelled on the right) Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be an invertible \(p \times p\) matrix, and let \(\mathbf{B}\) and \(\mathbf{C}\) be \(n \times p\) matrices. If \(\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{C}\mathbf{A}\), then \(\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{C}\).
The proof of Theorem 3 works with every product reversed: multiply both sides on the right by \(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\).










ExmExample 8 (Solving for a matrix by cancelling an invertible factor)






Example 8 (Solving for a matrix by cancelling an invertible factor) Let \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\), which is invertible with \(\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & -0.5 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) (Example 5). If \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}\) for a \(2 \times 1\) matrix \(\mathbf{B}\), then multiplying both sides on the left by \(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\), as in the proof of Theorem 3,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{B}
&= \mathbf{A}^{-1} \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Check: \(\mathbf{A} \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 + 1 \\ 0 + 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}\).










ExmExample 9 (A product of two nonzero matrices can be the zero matrix)






Example 9 (A product of two nonzero matrices can be the zero matrix) With
\[
\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\mathbf{B} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix},
\]
neither matrix is the zero matrix (Definition 4 in Matrices), but
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \cdot 0 + 0 \cdot 0 & 1 \cdot 0 + 0 \cdot 1 \\ 0 \cdot 0 + 0 \cdot 0 & 0 \cdot 0 + 0 \cdot 1 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \mathbf{0}_{2 \times 2}.
\end{aligned}
\]
For numbers, \(ab = 0\) forces \(a = 0\) or \(b = 0\). For matrices it does not.
Because \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{0}_{2 \times 2} = \mathbf{0}_{2 \times 2} = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}\) while \(\mathbf{B} \neq \mathbf{0}_{2 \times 2}\), the factor \(\mathbf{A}\) cannot be cancelled from \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{0}_{2 \times 2}\). This does not contradict Theorem 3, because \(\mathbf{A}\) is singular: its second row is zero, so \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{C}\) has a zero second row for every \(2 \times 2\) matrix \(\mathbf{C}\), and \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{C}\) can never be \(\mathbf{I}_2\).










RemarkRemark 4 (Matrix arithmetic compared with scalar arithmetic)






Remark 4 (Matrix arithmetic compared with scalar arithmetic). Sums and differences. Matrix addition has every property that addition of numbers has: it is commutative (Theorem 1 in Matrices), associative (Theorem 2 in Matrices), has the zero matrix as its identity (Theorem 3 in Matrices), and gives each matrix a negative (Theorem 4 in Matrices). The only new condition is that \(\mathbf{A} + \mathbf{B}\) is defined only when \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{B}\) have the same dimensions.
Products. Matrix multiplication keeps three properties of multiplication of numbers: it is associative (Theorem 5 in Matrices), it is distributive over addition (Theorem 6 in Matrices), and it has an identity, \(\mathbf{I}_p\) (Theorem 8 in Matrices). It differs from multiplication of numbers in four ways:


\(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}\) is defined only when the number of columns of \(\mathbf{A}\) equals the number of rows of \(\mathbf{B}\) (Definition 7 in Matrices).
It is not commutative: \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} \neq \mathbf{B}\mathbf{A}\) in general (Remark 2 in Matrices).
A nonzero square matrix need not have an inverse (Example 5).

\(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{0}\) can hold with \(\mathbf{A} \neq \mathbf{0}\) and \(\mathbf{B} \neq \mathbf{0}\) (Example 9), so a factor cannot be cancelled from \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{C}\) unless it is invertible (Theorem 3).











DefDefinition 7 (Idempotent matrix)






Definition 7 (Idempotent matrix) A square matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) is idempotent [4] if
\[\mathbf{A}^2 = \mathbf{A}\]










ExmExample 10 (An idempotent matrix)






Example 10 (An idempotent matrix) For \(\mathbf{P} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{P}^2
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \cdot 1 + 0 \cdot 0 & 1 \cdot 0 + 0 \cdot 0 \\ 0 \cdot 1 + 0 \cdot 0 & 0 \cdot 0 + 0 \cdot 0 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of matrix multiplication)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \mathbf{P},
&& \text{(multiply and add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\mathbf{P}\) is idempotent.










ExmExample 11 (A matrix that is not idempotent)






Example 11 (A matrix that is not idempotent) For \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}^2
&= \begin{bmatrix} 2 \cdot 2 + 0 \cdot 0 & 2 \cdot 0 + 0 \cdot 0 \\ 0 \cdot 2 + 0 \cdot 0 & 0 \cdot 0 + 0 \cdot 0 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 4 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix} \\
&\ne \mathbf{A},
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\mathbf{A}\) is not idempotent.










DefDefinition 8 (Orthogonal projection matrix)






Definition 8 (Orthogonal projection matrix) A square matrix \(\mathbf{P}\) is an orthogonal projection matrix (also called an orthogonal projector) if it is both symmetric (Definition 3) and idempotent (Definition 7):
\[{\mathbf{P}}^{\top} = \mathbf{P}
\qquad \text{and} \qquad
\mathbf{P}^2 = \mathbf{P}\]










DefDefinition 9 (Oblique projection)






Definition 9 (Oblique projection) A square matrix is an oblique projection if it is idempotent (Definition 7) but not symmetric (Definition 3).










ExmExample 12 (An orthogonal projection and an oblique one)






Example 12 (An orthogonal projection and an oblique one) \(\mathbf{P} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\) is symmetric, and \(\mathbf{P}^2 = \mathbf{P}\), so \(\mathbf{P}\) is an orthogonal projection matrix; it maps \((v_1, v_2)\) to \((v_1, 0)\).
\(\mathbf{Q} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\) is idempotent:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{Q}^2
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 0 & 1 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 0 \\ 0 \cdot 1 + 0 \cdot 0 & 0 \cdot 1 + 0 \cdot 0 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \mathbf{Q},
\end{aligned}
\]
but \({\mathbf{Q}}^{\top} \neq \mathbf{Q}\), so \(\mathbf{Q}\) is an oblique projection, not an orthogonal projection matrix.










RemarkRemark 5 (What “projection matrix” means in these notes)






Remark 5 (What “projection matrix” means in these notes). Some texts call any idempotent matrix a projection matrix, so that both \(\mathbf{P}\) and \(\mathbf{Q}\) in Example 12 would count as one. Regression texts often say “projection matrix” when they mean an orthogonal one. In these notes, “projection matrix” always means an orthogonal projection matrix in the sense of Definition 8, such as \(\mathbf{P}\) in Example 12, and never an oblique projection (Definition 9) such as \(\mathbf{Q}\).










LemTheorem 4 (Complement of a projection matrix)






Theorem 4 (Complement of a projection matrix) If \(\mathbf{P}\) is a \(p \times p\) orthogonal projection matrix (Definition 8), then \(\mathbf{I}_p - \mathbf{P}\) is also an orthogonal projection matrix.










ProofProof






Proof. We verify symmetry and idempotency.
Symmetry: \[
\begin{aligned}
{(\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P})}^{\top}
&= {\mathbf{I}}^{\top} - {\mathbf{P}}^{\top} \\
&= \mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P}
\end{aligned}
\]
Idempotency: \[\begin{aligned}
(\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P})^2
&= (\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P})(\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P}) \\
&= \mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P} - \mathbf{P} + \mathbf{P}^2 \\
&= \mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P} - \mathbf{P} + \mathbf{P} \\
&= \mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P}
\end{aligned}\]










LemTheorem 5 (Projection matrices produce orthogonal decompositions)






Theorem 5 (Projection matrices produce orthogonal decompositions) If \(\mathbf{P}\) is a \(p \times p\) orthogonal projection matrix (Definition 8) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is any vector of length \(p\), then the two components of the decomposition
\[\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \underbrace{\mathbf{P}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}_{\text{projected}} + \underbrace{(\mathbf{I}_p - \mathbf{P})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}_{\text{residual}}\]
are orthogonal:
\[\mathbf{P}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \;\perp\; (\mathbf{I}_p - \mathbf{P})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\]










ProofProof






Proof. \[\begin{aligned}
{(\mathbf{P}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{P}}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}\,\mathbf{P}\,(\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{P} - \mathbf{P}^2)\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{P} - \mathbf{P})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}\,\mathbf{0}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \\
&= 0
\end{aligned}\]
where the second line uses symmetry (\({\mathbf{P}}^{\top} = \mathbf{P}\)) and the fourth line uses idempotency (\(\mathbf{P}^2 = \mathbf{P}\)).










DefDefinition 10 (Orthogonal matrix)






Definition 10 (Orthogonal matrix) A \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{Q}\) is orthogonal [5] if
\[
\underbrace{{\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}}_{p \times p}\,\underbrace{\mathbf{Q}}_{p \times p} = \mathbf{I}_p
\]










ExmExample 13 (A rotation matrix)






Example 13 (A rotation matrix) The matrix
\[
\mathbf{Q} = \begin{bmatrix} 0.6 & -0.8 \\ 0.8 & 0.6 \end{bmatrix}
\]
rotates each vector in the plane counterclockwise by the angle \(\theta\) with \(\cos\theta= 0.6\) and \(\sin\theta= 0.8\) (about \(53\) degrees) [6, Ch. 8], Example 8.1, p. 207. It is orthogonal:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\mathbf{Q}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 0.6 & 0.8 \\ -0.8 & 0.6 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} 0.6 & -0.8 \\ 0.8 & 0.6 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of the transpose)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix}
0.36 + 0.64 & -0.48 + 0.48 \\
-0.48 + 0.48 & 0.64 + 0.36
\end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of matrix multiplication)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 14 (Orthogonal columns are not enough)






Example 14 (Orthogonal columns are not enough) \(\mathbf{Q} = 2 \mathbf{I}_2\) has orthogonal columns \((2, 0)\) and \((0, 2)\), but
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbf{Q}}^{\top} \mathbf{Q}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\ 0 & 2 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\ 0 & 2 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 4 & 0 \\ 0 & 4 \end{bmatrix} \\
&\ne \mathbf{I}_2,
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\mathbf{Q}\) is not an orthogonal matrix: its columns have norm \(2\), not \(1\).










RemarkRemark 6 (The columns of an orthogonal matrix are orthonormal)






Remark 6 (The columns of an orthogonal matrix are orthonormal). Entry \((i, j)\) of \({\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\mathbf{Q}\) is the dot product of column \(i\) and column \(j\) of \(\mathbf{Q}\), so \({\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\mathbf{Q} = \mathbf{I}_p\) says that the columns of \(\mathbf{Q}\) are orthonormal (Definition 23 in Vectors). For a square matrix, \({\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\mathbf{Q} = \mathbf{I}_p\) also implies \(\mathbf{Q}{\mathbf{Q}}^{\top} = \mathbf{I}_p\), so \(\mathbf{Q}^{-1} = {\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\) (Definition 5) [6, Ch. 8], Theorem 8.1 and Definition 8.1, p. 209.
For example, the columns of \(\mathbf{Q}\) in Example 13 are \((0.6, 0.8)\) and \((-0.8, 0.6)\). The diagonal entries \(0.36 + 0.64 = 1\) of \({\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\mathbf{Q}\) are their squared norms, and the off-diagonal entries \(-0.48 + 0.48 = 0\) are their dot product. Multiplying in the other order also gives \(\mathbf{I}_2\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{Q}{\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 0.6 & -0.8 \\ 0.8 & 0.6 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} 0.6 & 0.8 \\ -0.8 & 0.6 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of the transpose)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix}
0.36 + 0.64 & 0.48 - 0.48 \\
0.48 - 0.48 & 0.64 + 0.36
\end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of matrix multiplication)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
So \(\mathbf{Q}^{-1} = {\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\).










LemTheorem 6 (Orthogonal matrices preserve length)






Theorem 6 (Orthogonal matrices preserve length) If \(\mathbf{Q}\) is a \(p \times p\) orthogonal matrix (Definition 10) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) is a vector of length \(p\), then
\[
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
\]










ProofProof






Proof. \[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&= (\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) \cdot (\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})
&& \text{(definition of the Euclidean norm)}
\\
&= {(\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})
&& \text{(dot product as a matrix product)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})
&& \text{(transpose of a product)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,({\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\mathbf{Q})\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(regroup; matrix multiplication is associative)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,\mathbf{I}_p\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(definition of an orthogonal matrix)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(identity matrix)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(definition of the Euclidean norm)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Both norms are nonnegative square roots, so equal squares give \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\). The second step is Example 6 in Matrices, the third is Theorem 17 in Matrices, and the fourth is Theorem 5 in Matrices.










ExmExample 15 (Rotating a vector keeps its length)






Example 15 (Rotating a vector keeps its length) With \(\mathbf{Q}\) from Example 13 and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= (3, 4)\) from Example 15 in Vectors:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 0.6 \cdot 3 - 0.8 \cdot 4 \\ 0.8 \cdot 3 + 0.6 \cdot 4 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of matrix-vector multiplication)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1.8 - 3.2 \\ 2.4 + 2.4 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(multiply)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} -1.4 \\ 4.8 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
&= \sqrt{(-1.4)^2 + 4.8^2}
&& \text{(definition of the norm)}
\\
&= \sqrt{1.96 + 23.04}
&& \text{(square)}
\\
&= \sqrt{25}
&& \text{(add)}
\\
&= 5
&& \text{(take the square root)}
\end{aligned}
\]
which is \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = 5\).

Some special matrices appeared earlier:

the zero matrix (Definition 4 in Matrices), in the section on matrix addition
square matrices (Definition 8 in Matrices), in the section on square and identity matrices
the identity matrix (Definition 12 in Matrices), in that same section







DefDefinition 1 (Order of a square matrix)






Definition 1 (Order of a square matrix) The order of a square matrix (Definition 8 in Matrices) is its number of rows, which equals its number of columns.










ExmExample 1 (Orders of square matrices)






Example 1 (Orders of square matrices) \(\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{bmatrix}\) has order \(2\), and \([5]\) has order \(1\). A \(2 \times 3\) matrix has no order, since it is not square (Example 8 in Matrices).










DefDefinition 2 (Matrix power)






Definition 2 (Matrix power) For a square matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) of order \(p\) and a positive integer \(k\), the \(k\)-th power of \(\mathbf{A}\) is:
\[\mathbf{A}^k = \underbrace{\mathbf{A}\,\mathbf{A}\cdots\mathbf{A}}_{k \text{ copies}}\]
In particular, \(\mathbf{A}^2 = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{A}\).










ExmExample 2 (Powers of a \(2 \times 2\) matrix)






Example 2 (Powers of a \(2 \times 2\) matrix) Let \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}^2
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 0 & 1 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 1 \\ 0 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 0 & 0 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 1 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of matrix multiplication)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix},
&& \text{(multiply and add)}
\\
\mathbf{A}^3 = \mathbf{A}^2 \mathbf{A}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \cdot 1 + 2 \cdot 0 & 1 \cdot 1 + 2 \cdot 1 \\ 0 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 0 & 0 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 1 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of matrix multiplication)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 3 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}.
&& \text{(multiply and add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
A \(2 \times 3\) matrix \(\mathbf{B}\) is not square (Definition 8 in Matrices), so \(\mathbf{B}^2\) is not defined; indeed \(\mathbf{B} \mathbf{B}\) would multiply a matrix with \(3\) columns by one with \(2\) rows, which Definition 7 in Matrices does not allow.










DefDefinition 3 (Symmetric matrix)






Definition 3 (Symmetric matrix) A square matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) is symmetric [1] if \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top} = \mathbf{A}\), i.e., \(a_{ij} = a_{ji}\) for all \(i\) and \(j\).










RemarkRemark 1 (Symmetric matrices in statistics)






Remark 1 (Symmetric matrices in statistics). Covariance matrices are symmetric: entry \((i, j)\) of the covariance matrix of a random vector \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{Y}}\vphantom{Y}}\) is \({\operatorname{Cov}\mathopen{}\left(Y_i, Y_j\right)\mathclose{}}\), and \({\operatorname{Cov}\mathopen{}\left(Y_i, Y_j\right)\mathclose{}} = {\operatorname{Cov}\mathopen{}\left(Y_j, Y_i\right)\mathclose{}}\). For example, if \({\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(Y_1\right)\mathclose{}} = 4\), \({\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(Y_2\right)\mathclose{}} = 9\), and \({\operatorname{Cov}\mathopen{}\left(Y_1, Y_2\right)\mathclose{}} = 1\), the covariance matrix of \((Y_1, Y_2)\) is
\[
\begin{bmatrix} 4 & 1 \\ 1 & 9 \end{bmatrix}.
\]










ExmExample 3 (A matrix that is not symmetric)






Example 3 (A matrix that is not symmetric) \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{bmatrix}\) is not symmetric:
\[
\begin{aligned}
a_{12} &= 2 \\
&\ne 3 \\
&= a_{21}.
\end{aligned}
\]










DefDefinition 4 (Diagonal matrix)






Definition 4 (Diagonal matrix) A square matrix \(\mathbf{D}\) is a diagonal matrix [2] if all off-diagonal entries are zero: \(d_{ij} = 0\) whenever \(i \neq j\):
\[
\mathbf{D} =
\begin{bmatrix}
d_1    & 0      & \cdots & 0      \\
0      & d_2    & \cdots & 0      \\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
0      & 0      & \cdots & d_p
\end{bmatrix}
\]










RemarkRemark 2 (\(\operatorname{diag}\) notation)






Remark 2 (\(\operatorname{diag}\) notation). For numbers \(d_1, \ldots, d_p\), \(\operatorname{diag}(d_1, \ldots, d_p)\) is the \(p \times p\) diagonal matrix with diagonal entries \(d_1, \ldots, d_p\). For example,
\[
\operatorname{diag}(2, 5) = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\ 0 & 5 \end{bmatrix},
\]
and the \(p \times p\) identity matrix is \(\mathbf{I}_p = \operatorname{diag}(1, \ldots, 1)\).










ExmExample 4 (A matrix that is not diagonal)






Example 4 (A matrix that is not diagonal) \(\mathbf{D} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 0 & 5 \end{bmatrix}\) is not diagonal: its off-diagonal entry \(d_{12} = 1 \ne 0\).










DefDefinition 5 (Matrix inverse)






Definition 5 (Matrix inverse) For a square \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\), the inverse \(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\) (if it exists) is the unique matrix satisfying:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}\,\mathbf{A}^{-1}
&= \mathbf{A}^{-1}\,\mathbf{A} \\
&= \mathbf{I}_p
\end{aligned}
\]










DefDefinition 6 (Invertible matrix, non-singular matrix)






Definition 6 (Invertible matrix, non-singular matrix) A \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) is invertible [3] (or non-singular) if some \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{B}\) satisfies
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}
&= \mathbf{B}\mathbf{A} \\
&= \mathbf{I}_p,
\end{aligned}
\]
where \(\mathbf{I}_p\) is the identity matrix (Definition 12 in Matrices). A square matrix that is not invertible is singular.










ExmExample 5 (An invertible matrix and a singular one)






Example 5 (An invertible matrix and a singular one) The matrix \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) is invertible, with \(\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & -0.5 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\): multiplying out gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}\mathbf{A}^{-1} &= \mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{A} \\
&= \mathbf{I}_2.
\end{aligned}
\]
The matrix \(\mathbf{M} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) is singular: for any \(2 \times 2\) matrix \(\mathbf{C}\), the two rows of \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}\) are equal, so \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}\) can never be \(\mathbf{I}_2\), whose two rows differ.










RemarkRemark 3 (Invertible matrices and inverses)






Remark 3 (Invertible matrices and inverses). Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be a \(p \times p\) matrix, and suppose that two \(p \times p\) matrices \(\mathbf{B}\) and \(\mathbf{C}\) satisfy
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} &= \mathbf{B}\mathbf{A} \\
&= \mathbf{I}_p
\end{aligned}
\]
and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}\mathbf{C} &= \mathbf{C}\mathbf{A} \\
&= \mathbf{I}_p.
\end{aligned}
\]
Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{B}
&= \mathbf{B}\,\mathbf{I}_p
&& \text{(identity matrix)}
\\
&= \mathbf{B}(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{C})
&& \text{(} \mathbf{A}\mathbf{C} = \mathbf{I}_p \text{)}
\\
&= (\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A})\mathbf{C}
&& \text{(matrix multiplication is associative)}
\\
&= \mathbf{I}_p\,\mathbf{C}
&& \text{(} \mathbf{B}\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{I}_p \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{C}
&& \text{(identity matrix)}
\end{aligned}
\]
The steps use Theorem 5 in Matrices and Theorem 8 in Matrices. Since \(\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{C}\), at most one matrix satisfies these equations, which is the uniqueness that Definition 5 asserts. When \(\mathbf{A}\) is invertible, the matrix \(\mathbf{B}\) in Definition 6 is therefore the inverse \(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\) of \(\mathbf{A}\), and \(\mathbf{A}\) is invertible exactly when it has an inverse. For example, for \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) in Example 5, \(\begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & -0.5 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) is the only \(2 \times 2\) matrix \(\mathbf{B}\) with
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} &= \mathbf{B}\mathbf{A} \\
&= \mathbf{I}_2,
\end{aligned}
\]
so it is \(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\).










LemTheorem 1 (Inverse of a product)






Theorem 1 (Inverse of a product) If \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{B}\) are invertible \(p \times p\) matrices, then \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}\) is invertible, and
\[(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})^{-1} = \mathbf{B}^{-1}\mathbf{A}^{-1}\]










ProofProof






Proof. Multiply \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}\) by the candidate inverse on the right:
\[
\begin{aligned}
(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})(\mathbf{B}^{-1}\mathbf{A}^{-1})
&= \mathbf{A}(\mathbf{B}\mathbf{B}^{-1})\mathbf{A}^{-1}
&& \text{(matrix multiplication is associative)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}\,\mathbf{I}_p\,\mathbf{A}^{-1}
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{B}^{-1} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}\mathbf{A}^{-1}
&& \text{(identity matrix)}
\\
&= \mathbf{I}_p
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{A}^{-1} \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
and on the left:
\[
\begin{aligned}
(\mathbf{B}^{-1}\mathbf{A}^{-1})(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})
&= \mathbf{B}^{-1}(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{A})\mathbf{B}
&& \text{(matrix multiplication is associative)}
\\
&= \mathbf{B}^{-1}\,\mathbf{I}_p\,\mathbf{B}
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{A}^{-1} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{B}^{-1}\mathbf{B}
&& \text{(identity matrix)}
\\
&= \mathbf{I}_p
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{B}^{-1} \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
So \(\mathbf{B}^{-1}\mathbf{A}^{-1}\) satisfies Definition 5 for \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}\). The steps use Theorem 5 in Matrices and Theorem 8 in Matrices.










LemTheorem 2 (Inverse of a transpose)






Theorem 2 (Inverse of a transpose) If \(\mathbf{A}\) is an invertible \(p \times p\) matrix, then \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\) is invertible, and
\[{\mathopen{}\left({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\right)\mathclose{}}^{-1} = {\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}\]










ProofProof






Proof. Multiply \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\) by the candidate inverse on each side:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\,{\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}
&= {\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{A}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}
&& \text{(transpose of a product)}
\\
&= {\mathbf{I}_p}^{\top}
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{A}^{-1} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{I}_p
&& \text{(} \mathbf{I}_p \text{ is symmetric)}
\end{aligned}
\]
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{A}}^{\top}
&= {\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{A}^{-1}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}
&& \text{(transpose of a product)}
\\
&= {\mathbf{I}_p}^{\top}
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{A}^{-1} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{I}_p
&& \text{(} \mathbf{I}_p \text{ is symmetric)}
\end{aligned}
\]
So \({\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}\) satisfies Definition 5 for \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\). The first step of each display is Theorem 17 in Matrices.










ExmExample 6 (Inverting a transpose)






Example 6 (Inverting a transpose) For \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\), Example 5 gives \(\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & -0.5 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\), so Theorem 2 says
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathopen{}\left({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\right)\mathclose{}}^{-1}
&= {\mathopen{}\left(\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\right)\mathclose{}}^{-1} \\
&= {\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & 0 \\ -0.5 & 1 \end{bmatrix},
\end{aligned}
\]
and multiplying \(\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & 0 \\ -0.5 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) out does give \(\mathbf{I}_2\).










CorCorollary 1 (The inverse of a symmetric matrix is symmetric)






Corollary 1 (The inverse of a symmetric matrix is symmetric) If \(\mathbf{A}\) is symmetric and invertible, then \(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\) is symmetric.










ProofProof






Proof. \[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}
&= {\mathopen{}\left({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\right)\mathclose{}}^{-1}
&& \text{(inverse of a transpose)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}^{-1}
&& \text{(} \mathbf{A} \text{ is symmetric)}
\end{aligned}
\]
The first step is Theorem 2.










ExmExample 7 (Inverting a symmetric matrix)






Example 7 (Inverting a symmetric matrix) \(\mathbf{S} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) is symmetric, and \(\mathbf{S}^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 \\ -1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) is symmetric too, as Corollary 1 says.










LemTheorem 3 (An invertible factor can be cancelled)






Theorem 3 (An invertible factor can be cancelled) Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be an invertible \(p \times p\) matrix (Definition 6), and let \(\mathbf{B}\) and \(\mathbf{C}\) be \(p \times n\) matrices. If \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{C}\), then \(\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{C}\).










ProofProof






Proof. \[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{B}
&= \mathbf{I}_p\,\mathbf{B}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= (\mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{A})\,\mathbf{B}
&& \text{(}\href{#def-matrix-inverse}{\text{Definition~5}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}^{-1}(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}^{-1}(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{C})
&& \text{(} \mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{C} \text{)}
\\
&= (\mathbf{A}^{-1}\mathbf{A})\,\mathbf{C}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{I}_p\,\mathbf{C}
&& \text{(}\href{#def-matrix-inverse}{\text{Definition~5}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{C}.
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
\(\square\)










CorCorollary 2 (An invertible factor can be cancelled on the right)






Corollary 2 (An invertible factor can be cancelled on the right) Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be an invertible \(p \times p\) matrix, and let \(\mathbf{B}\) and \(\mathbf{C}\) be \(n \times p\) matrices. If \(\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{C}\mathbf{A}\), then \(\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{C}\).
The proof of Theorem 3 works with every product reversed: multiply both sides on the right by \(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\).










ExmExample 8 (Solving for a matrix by cancelling an invertible factor)






Example 8 (Solving for a matrix by cancelling an invertible factor) Let \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\), which is invertible with \(\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & -0.5 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) (Example 5). If \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}\) for a \(2 \times 1\) matrix \(\mathbf{B}\), then multiplying both sides on the left by \(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\), as in the proof of Theorem 3,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{B}
&= \mathbf{A}^{-1} \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Check: \(\mathbf{A} \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 + 1 \\ 0 + 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}\).










ExmExample 9 (A product of two nonzero matrices can be the zero matrix)






Example 9 (A product of two nonzero matrices can be the zero matrix) With
\[
\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\mathbf{B} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix},
\]
neither matrix is the zero matrix (Definition 4 in Matrices), but
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \cdot 0 + 0 \cdot 0 & 1 \cdot 0 + 0 \cdot 1 \\ 0 \cdot 0 + 0 \cdot 0 & 0 \cdot 0 + 0 \cdot 1 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \mathbf{0}_{2 \times 2}.
\end{aligned}
\]
For numbers, \(ab = 0\) forces \(a = 0\) or \(b = 0\). For matrices it does not.
Because \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{0}_{2 \times 2} = \mathbf{0}_{2 \times 2} = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}\) while \(\mathbf{B} \neq \mathbf{0}_{2 \times 2}\), the factor \(\mathbf{A}\) cannot be cancelled from \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{0}_{2 \times 2}\). This does not contradict Theorem 3, because \(\mathbf{A}\) is singular: its second row is zero, so \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{C}\) has a zero second row for every \(2 \times 2\) matrix \(\mathbf{C}\), and \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{C}\) can never be \(\mathbf{I}_2\).










RemarkRemark 4 (Matrix arithmetic compared with scalar arithmetic)






Remark 4 (Matrix arithmetic compared with scalar arithmetic). Sums and differences. Matrix addition has every property that addition of numbers has: it is commutative (Theorem 1 in Matrices), associative (Theorem 2 in Matrices), has the zero matrix as its identity (Theorem 3 in Matrices), and gives each matrix a negative (Theorem 4 in Matrices). The only new condition is that \(\mathbf{A} + \mathbf{B}\) is defined only when \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{B}\) have the same dimensions.
Products. Matrix multiplication keeps three properties of multiplication of numbers: it is associative (Theorem 5 in Matrices), it is distributive over addition (Theorem 6 in Matrices), and it has an identity, \(\mathbf{I}_p\) (Theorem 8 in Matrices). It differs from multiplication of numbers in four ways:


\(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}\) is defined only when the number of columns of \(\mathbf{A}\) equals the number of rows of \(\mathbf{B}\) (Definition 7 in Matrices).
It is not commutative: \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} \neq \mathbf{B}\mathbf{A}\) in general (Remark 2 in Matrices).
A nonzero square matrix need not have an inverse (Example 5).

\(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{0}\) can hold with \(\mathbf{A} \neq \mathbf{0}\) and \(\mathbf{B} \neq \mathbf{0}\) (Example 9), so a factor cannot be cancelled from \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{C}\) unless it is invertible (Theorem 3).











DefDefinition 7 (Idempotent matrix)






Definition 7 (Idempotent matrix) A square matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) is idempotent [4] if
\[\mathbf{A}^2 = \mathbf{A}\]










ExmExample 10 (An idempotent matrix)






Example 10 (An idempotent matrix) For \(\mathbf{P} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{P}^2
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \cdot 1 + 0 \cdot 0 & 1 \cdot 0 + 0 \cdot 0 \\ 0 \cdot 1 + 0 \cdot 0 & 0 \cdot 0 + 0 \cdot 0 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of matrix multiplication)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \mathbf{P},
&& \text{(multiply and add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\mathbf{P}\) is idempotent.










ExmExample 11 (A matrix that is not idempotent)






Example 11 (A matrix that is not idempotent) For \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}^2
&= \begin{bmatrix} 2 \cdot 2 + 0 \cdot 0 & 2 \cdot 0 + 0 \cdot 0 \\ 0 \cdot 2 + 0 \cdot 0 & 0 \cdot 0 + 0 \cdot 0 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 4 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix} \\
&\ne \mathbf{A},
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\mathbf{A}\) is not idempotent.










DefDefinition 8 (Orthogonal projection matrix)






Definition 8 (Orthogonal projection matrix) A square matrix \(\mathbf{P}\) is an orthogonal projection matrix (also called an orthogonal projector) if it is both symmetric (Definition 3) and idempotent (Definition 7):
\[{\mathbf{P}}^{\top} = \mathbf{P}
\qquad \text{and} \qquad
\mathbf{P}^2 = \mathbf{P}\]










DefDefinition 9 (Oblique projection)






Definition 9 (Oblique projection) A square matrix is an oblique projection if it is idempotent (Definition 7) but not symmetric (Definition 3).










ExmExample 12 (An orthogonal projection and an oblique one)






Example 12 (An orthogonal projection and an oblique one) \(\mathbf{P} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\) is symmetric, and \(\mathbf{P}^2 = \mathbf{P}\), so \(\mathbf{P}\) is an orthogonal projection matrix; it maps \((v_1, v_2)\) to \((v_1, 0)\).
\(\mathbf{Q} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\) is idempotent:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{Q}^2
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 0 & 1 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 0 \\ 0 \cdot 1 + 0 \cdot 0 & 0 \cdot 1 + 0 \cdot 0 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \mathbf{Q},
\end{aligned}
\]
but \({\mathbf{Q}}^{\top} \neq \mathbf{Q}\), so \(\mathbf{Q}\) is an oblique projection, not an orthogonal projection matrix.










RemarkRemark 5 (What “projection matrix” means in these notes)






Remark 5 (What “projection matrix” means in these notes). Some texts call any idempotent matrix a projection matrix, so that both \(\mathbf{P}\) and \(\mathbf{Q}\) in Example 12 would count as one. Regression texts often say “projection matrix” when they mean an orthogonal one. In these notes, “projection matrix” always means an orthogonal projection matrix in the sense of Definition 8, such as \(\mathbf{P}\) in Example 12, and never an oblique projection (Definition 9) such as \(\mathbf{Q}\).










LemTheorem 4 (Complement of a projection matrix)






Theorem 4 (Complement of a projection matrix) If \(\mathbf{P}\) is a \(p \times p\) orthogonal projection matrix (Definition 8), then \(\mathbf{I}_p - \mathbf{P}\) is also an orthogonal projection matrix.










ProofProof






Proof. We verify symmetry and idempotency.
Symmetry: \[
\begin{aligned}
{(\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P})}^{\top}
&= {\mathbf{I}}^{\top} - {\mathbf{P}}^{\top} \\
&= \mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P}
\end{aligned}
\]
Idempotency: \[\begin{aligned}
(\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P})^2
&= (\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P})(\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P}) \\
&= \mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P} - \mathbf{P} + \mathbf{P}^2 \\
&= \mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P} - \mathbf{P} + \mathbf{P} \\
&= \mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P}
\end{aligned}\]










LemTheorem 5 (Projection matrices produce orthogonal decompositions)






Theorem 5 (Projection matrices produce orthogonal decompositions) If \(\mathbf{P}\) is a \(p \times p\) orthogonal projection matrix (Definition 8) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is any vector of length \(p\), then the two components of the decomposition
\[\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \underbrace{\mathbf{P}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}_{\text{projected}} + \underbrace{(\mathbf{I}_p - \mathbf{P})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}_{\text{residual}}\]
are orthogonal:
\[\mathbf{P}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \;\perp\; (\mathbf{I}_p - \mathbf{P})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\]










ProofProof






Proof. \[\begin{aligned}
{(\mathbf{P}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{P}}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}\,\mathbf{P}\,(\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{P} - \mathbf{P}^2)\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{P} - \mathbf{P})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}\,\mathbf{0}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \\
&= 0
\end{aligned}\]
where the second line uses symmetry (\({\mathbf{P}}^{\top} = \mathbf{P}\)) and the fourth line uses idempotency (\(\mathbf{P}^2 = \mathbf{P}\)).










DefDefinition 10 (Orthogonal matrix)






Definition 10 (Orthogonal matrix) A \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{Q}\) is orthogonal [5] if
\[
\underbrace{{\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}}_{p \times p}\,\underbrace{\mathbf{Q}}_{p \times p} = \mathbf{I}_p
\]










ExmExample 13 (A rotation matrix)






Example 13 (A rotation matrix) The matrix
\[
\mathbf{Q} = \begin{bmatrix} 0.6 & -0.8 \\ 0.8 & 0.6 \end{bmatrix}
\]
rotates each vector in the plane counterclockwise by the angle \(\theta\) with \(\cos\theta= 0.6\) and \(\sin\theta= 0.8\) (about \(53\) degrees) [6, Ch. 8], Example 8.1, p. 207. It is orthogonal:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\mathbf{Q}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 0.6 & 0.8 \\ -0.8 & 0.6 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} 0.6 & -0.8 \\ 0.8 & 0.6 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of the transpose)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix}
0.36 + 0.64 & -0.48 + 0.48 \\
-0.48 + 0.48 & 0.64 + 0.36
\end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of matrix multiplication)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 14 (Orthogonal columns are not enough)






Example 14 (Orthogonal columns are not enough) \(\mathbf{Q} = 2 \mathbf{I}_2\) has orthogonal columns \((2, 0)\) and \((0, 2)\), but
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbf{Q}}^{\top} \mathbf{Q}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\ 0 & 2 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\ 0 & 2 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 4 & 0 \\ 0 & 4 \end{bmatrix} \\
&\ne \mathbf{I}_2,
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\mathbf{Q}\) is not an orthogonal matrix: its columns have norm \(2\), not \(1\).










RemarkRemark 6 (The columns of an orthogonal matrix are orthonormal)






Remark 6 (The columns of an orthogonal matrix are orthonormal). Entry \((i, j)\) of \({\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\mathbf{Q}\) is the dot product of column \(i\) and column \(j\) of \(\mathbf{Q}\), so \({\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\mathbf{Q} = \mathbf{I}_p\) says that the columns of \(\mathbf{Q}\) are orthonormal (Definition 23 in Vectors). For a square matrix, \({\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\mathbf{Q} = \mathbf{I}_p\) also implies \(\mathbf{Q}{\mathbf{Q}}^{\top} = \mathbf{I}_p\), so \(\mathbf{Q}^{-1} = {\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\) (Definition 5) [6, Ch. 8], Theorem 8.1 and Definition 8.1, p. 209.
For example, the columns of \(\mathbf{Q}\) in Example 13 are \((0.6, 0.8)\) and \((-0.8, 0.6)\). The diagonal entries \(0.36 + 0.64 = 1\) of \({\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\mathbf{Q}\) are their squared norms, and the off-diagonal entries \(-0.48 + 0.48 = 0\) are their dot product. Multiplying in the other order also gives \(\mathbf{I}_2\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{Q}{\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 0.6 & -0.8 \\ 0.8 & 0.6 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} 0.6 & 0.8 \\ -0.8 & 0.6 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of the transpose)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix}
0.36 + 0.64 & 0.48 - 0.48 \\
0.48 - 0.48 & 0.64 + 0.36
\end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of matrix multiplication)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
So \(\mathbf{Q}^{-1} = {\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\).










LemTheorem 6 (Orthogonal matrices preserve length)






Theorem 6 (Orthogonal matrices preserve length) If \(\mathbf{Q}\) is a \(p \times p\) orthogonal matrix (Definition 10) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) is a vector of length \(p\), then
\[
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
\]










ProofProof






Proof. \[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&= (\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) \cdot (\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})
&& \text{(definition of the Euclidean norm)}
\\
&= {(\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})
&& \text{(dot product as a matrix product)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})
&& \text{(transpose of a product)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,({\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\mathbf{Q})\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(regroup; matrix multiplication is associative)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,\mathbf{I}_p\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(definition of an orthogonal matrix)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(identity matrix)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(definition of the Euclidean norm)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Both norms are nonnegative square roots, so equal squares give \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\). The second step is Example 6 in Matrices, the third is Theorem 17 in Matrices, and the fourth is Theorem 5 in Matrices.










ExmExample 15 (Rotating a vector keeps its length)






Example 15 (Rotating a vector keeps its length) With \(\mathbf{Q}\) from Example 13 and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= (3, 4)\) from Example 15 in Vectors:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 0.6 \cdot 3 - 0.8 \cdot 4 \\ 0.8 \cdot 3 + 0.6 \cdot 4 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of matrix-vector multiplication)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1.8 - 3.2 \\ 2.4 + 2.4 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(multiply)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} -1.4 \\ 4.8 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{Q}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
&= \sqrt{(-1.4)^2 + 4.8^2}
&& \text{(definition of the norm)}
\\
&= \sqrt{1.96 + 23.04}
&& \text{(square)}
\\
&= \sqrt{25}
&& \text{(add)}
\\
&= 5
&& \text{(take the square root)}
\end{aligned}
\]
which is \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = 5\).

2 Quadratic Forms






DefDefinition 11 (Quadratic form)






Definition 11 (Quadratic form) A quadratic form [7] is a mathematical expression of the structure
\[{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\, \mathbf{S}\, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\]
where \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) is a \(p \times 1\) vector and \(\mathbf{S}\) is a \(p \times p\) matrix.










RemarkRemark 7 (Quadratic forms in statistics)






Remark 7 (Quadratic forms in statistics). A quadratic form extends the scalar expression \(c x^2\) to vectors: with \(p = 1\) and \(\mathbf{S} = [c]\), \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,\mathbf{S}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= c x^2\). With \(p = 2\),
\[
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}
\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 0 & 3 \end{bmatrix}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
= x_1^2 + 2 x_1 x_2 + 3 x_2^2,
\]
which is \(1 + 4 + 12 = 17\) at \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= (1, 2)\).
Quadratic forms occur often in statistics:

The residual sum of squares in linear regression (see Vector Calculus) is a quadratic form.
The variance of a linear combination of estimates (see Inference about Gaussian Linear Regression Models) is a quadratic form: \({\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\beta}}\vphantom{\beta}}}\right)\mathclose{}} = {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,{\operatorname{Var}\mathopen{}\left(\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\beta}}\vphantom{\beta}}}\right)\mathclose{}}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\).











LemTheorem 7 (A quadratic form is additive in its matrix)






Theorem 7 (A quadratic form is additive in its matrix) If \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{B}\) are \(p \times p\) matrices, \(c\) is a number and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) is a vector of length \(p\), then
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}(\mathbf{A} + \mathbf{B})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \\
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}(c\,\mathbf{A})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}&= c\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}.
\end{aligned}
\]
So for a fixed \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\), the map \(\mathbf{S} \mapsto {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) is linear in \(\mathbf{S}\).










ProofProof






Proof. By the distributive law (Theorem 6 in Matrices), applied on the left and then on the right,
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}(\mathbf{A} + \mathbf{B})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&= \mathopen{}\left({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\mathbf{A} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\mathbf{B}\right)\mathclose{}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(distribute } {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \text{ over the sum)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(distribute } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\text{ over the sum)}
\end{aligned}
\]
For the scalar, a number can be moved out of any product, so \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}(c\,\mathbf{A})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= c\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\).










ExmExample 16 (Combining two quadratic forms into one)






Example 16 (Combining two quadratic forms into one) Take \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 0 & 3 \end{bmatrix}\), \(\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{I}\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= (1, 2)\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} &= 1 + 4 + 12 = 17, \\
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{I} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} &= 1 + 4 = 5, \\
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}(\mathbf{A} + \mathbf{I})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 2 \\ 0 & 4 \end{bmatrix}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\\
&= 2 + 4 + 16 \\
&= 22,
\end{aligned}
\]
which equals \(17 + 5\). In particular, \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}(\mathbf{A} + c\,\mathbf{I})\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + c\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2\) for every number \(c\), because \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{I} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2\). This is how two quadratic terms in the same vector combine into a single quadratic form.










DefDefinition 12 (Symmetric part of a square matrix)






Definition 12 (Symmetric part of a square matrix) The symmetric part of a \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{S}\) is
\[\frac{1}{2}\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{S} + {\mathbf{S}}^{\top}\right)\mathclose{}\]










ExmExample 17 (The symmetric part of a \(2 \times 2\) matrix)






Example 17 (The symmetric part of a \(2 \times 2\) matrix) For \(\mathbf{S} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 0 & 3 \end{bmatrix}\), the symmetric part is
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{1}{2}\mathopen{}\left(
\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 0 & 3 \end{bmatrix}
+ \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 2 & 3 \end{bmatrix}
\right)\mathclose{}
&= \frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 2 \\ 2 & 6 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 3 \end{bmatrix},
\end{aligned}
\]
which is symmetric.










LemTheorem 8 (A quadratic form depends only on the symmetric part)






Theorem 8 (A quadratic form depends only on the symmetric part) If \(\mathbf{S}\) is a \(p \times p\) matrix and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) is a vector of length \(p\), then
\[
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
=
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\mathopen{}\left(\frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{S}+{\mathbf{S}}^{\top})\right)\mathclose{}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}.
\]
So the value of a quadratic form depends only on the symmetric part (Definition 12) of \(\mathbf{S}\).










ProofProof






Proof. The quadratic form \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) is a \(1 \times 1\) matrix, so it equals its own transpose:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&= {\mathopen{}\left({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}
&& \text{(a } 1 \times 1 \text{ matrix is symmetric)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{S}}^{\top}\,{\mathopen{}\left({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}
&& \text{(transpose of a product, twice)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{S}}^{\top}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(transposing twice changes nothing)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Averaging the two expressions for \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&= \frac{1}{2}\mathopen{}\left({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{S}}^{\top}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(average of two equal numbers)}
\\
&= \frac{1}{2}\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{S} + {\mathbf{S}}^{\top}\right)\mathclose{}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(distributive law)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\mathopen{}\left(\frac{1}{2}(\mathbf{S}+{\mathbf{S}}^{\top})\right)\mathclose{}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(move the scalar } \tfrac{1}{2} \text{ inside)}
\end{aligned}
\]
The distributive step is Theorem 6 in Matrices, and the transpose step is Theorem 17 in Matrices.










ExmExample 18 (Replacing a matrix by its symmetric part)






Example 18 (Replacing a matrix by its symmetric part) With \(\mathbf{S} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 0 & 3 \end{bmatrix}\) from Example 17 and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= (1, 1)\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&= 1 + 2 + 0 + 3 \\
&= 6 \\
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 3 \end{bmatrix}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&= 1 + 1 + 1 + 3 \\
&= 6
\end{aligned}
\]
Both quadratic forms equal the sum of their matrix’s entries, because every entry of \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) is \(1\).

3 Trace and Matrix Inner Product






DefDefinition 13 (Trace)






Definition 13 (Trace) The trace [8] of a \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{M}\) is the sum of its diagonal entries:
\[\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{M}) \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\sum_{i=1}^pM_{ii}\]










ExmExample 19 (The trace of a \(2 \times 2\) matrix)






Example 19 (The trace of a \(2 \times 2\) matrix) \[
\begin{aligned}
{\operatorname{tr}\mathopen{}\left(\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{bmatrix}\right)\mathclose{}}
&= 1 + 4 \\
&= 5
\end{aligned}
\]










LemTheorem 9 (The trace of a product does not depend on the order)






Theorem 9 (The trace of a product does not depend on the order) For an \(m \times n\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) and an \(n \times m\) matrix \(\mathbf{B}\):
\[
{\operatorname{tr}\mathopen{}\left(\underbrace{\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}}_{m \times m}\right)\mathclose{}}
= {\operatorname{tr}\mathopen{}\left(\underbrace{\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A}}_{n \times n}\right)\mathclose{}}
\]










ProofProof






Proof. \[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})
&= \sum_{i=1}^{m} (\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})_{ii}
&& \text{(definition of the trace)}
\\
&= \sum_{i=1}^{m} \sum_{s=1}^{n} a_{is}\, b_{si}
&& \text{(definition of matrix multiplication)}
\\
&= \sum_{s=1}^{n} \sum_{i=1}^{m} a_{is}\, b_{si}
&& \text{(swap the order of two finite sums)}
\\
&= \sum_{s=1}^{n} \sum_{i=1}^{m} b_{si}\, a_{is}
&& \text{(multiplication of numbers is commutative)}
\\
&= \sum_{s=1}^{n} (\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A})_{ss}
&& \text{(definition of matrix multiplication)}
\\
&= \operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A})
&& \text{(definition of the trace)}
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 20 (Traces of the two products of a \(2 \times 3\) and a \(3 \times 2\) matrix)






Example 20 (Traces of the two products of a \(2 \times 3\) and a \(3 \times 2\) matrix) Let
\[
\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 3 \end{bmatrix}
\qquad
\mathbf{B} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 2 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}
\]
The products are
\[
\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} = \begin{bmatrix} 5 & 2 \\ 2 & 4 \end{bmatrix}
\qquad
\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 & 0 \\ 2 & 5 & 3 \\ 0 & 1 & 3 \end{bmatrix}
\]
The trace needs only the diagonal entries. For \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}\), which is \(2 \times 2\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})
&= (\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})_{11} + (\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})_{22}
&& \text{(definition of the trace)}
\\
&= (1 \cdot 1 + 2 \cdot 2 + 0 \cdot 0) + (0 \cdot 0 + 1 \cdot 1 + 3 \cdot 1)
&& \text{(definition of matrix multiplication)}
\\
&= (1 + 4 + 0) + (0 + 1 + 3)
&& \text{(multiply)}
\\
&= 5 + 4
&& \text{(add within each entry)}
\\
&= 9
&& \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
For \(\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A}\), which is \(3 \times 3\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A})
&= (\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A})_{11} + (\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A})_{22} + (\mathbf{B}\mathbf{A})_{33}
&& \text{(definition of the trace)}
\\
&= (1 \cdot 1 + 0 \cdot 0) + (2 \cdot 2 + 1 \cdot 1) + (0 \cdot 0 + 1 \cdot 3)
&& \text{(definition of matrix multiplication)}
\\
&= (1 + 0) + (4 + 1) + (0 + 3)
&& \text{(multiply)}
\\
&= 1 + 5 + 3
&& \text{(add within each entry)}
\\
&= 9
&& \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
The products have different sizes, but their traces agree.










RemarkRemark 8 (Moving a factor of a triple product)






Remark 8 (Moving a factor of a triple product). Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be \(m \times n\), \(\mathbf{B}\) be \(n \times k\), and \(\mathbf{C}\) be \(k \times m\), so that \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}\) is \(m \times k\) and \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{C}\) is square. Applying Theorem 9 to the \(m \times k\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}\) and the \(k \times m\) matrix \(\mathbf{C}\) moves the last factor of the triple product to the front [6, Ch. 1], Theorem 1.5 and eq. 1.17, p. 19:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{C})
&= {\operatorname{tr}\mathopen{}\left((\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})\,\mathbf{C}\right)\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(matrix multiplication is associative)}
\\
&= {\operatorname{tr}\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{C}\,(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})\right)\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(the theorem, applied to the two factors } \mathbf{A}\mathbf{B} \text{ and } \mathbf{C} \text{)}
\\
&= \operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})
&& \text{(matrix multiplication is associative)}
\end{aligned}
\]
For example, let
\[
\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}
\qquad
\mathbf{B} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}
\qquad
\mathbf{C} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{bmatrix}
\]
Then
\[
\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}
= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \cdot 1 + 2 \cdot 1 & 1 \cdot 0 + 2 \cdot 1 \\ 0 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 1 & 0 \cdot 0 + 1 \cdot 1 \end{bmatrix}
= \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 2 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}
\qquad
\mathbf{C}\mathbf{A}
= \begin{bmatrix} 0 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 0 & 0 \cdot 2 + 1 \cdot 1 \\ 1 \cdot 1 + 0 \cdot 0 & 1 \cdot 2 + 0 \cdot 1 \end{bmatrix}
= \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}
\]
so
\[
(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B})\,\mathbf{C}
= \begin{bmatrix} 3 \cdot 0 + 2 \cdot 1 & 3 \cdot 1 + 2 \cdot 0 \\ 1 \cdot 0 + 1 \cdot 1 & 1 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 0 \end{bmatrix}
= \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 3 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}
\qquad
(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{A})\,\mathbf{B}
= \begin{bmatrix} 0 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 1 & 0 \cdot 0 + 1 \cdot 1 \\ 1 \cdot 1 + 2 \cdot 1 & 1 \cdot 0 + 2 \cdot 1 \end{bmatrix}
= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 3 & 2 \end{bmatrix}
\]
and both traces are the same:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{C}) &= 2 + 1 \\
&= 3
\end{aligned}
\]
and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}) &= 1 + 2 \\
&= 3.
\end{aligned}
\]










DefDefinition 14 (Matrix inner product)






Definition 14 (Matrix inner product) The inner product of two \(n \times p\) matrices \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{B}\) is
\[
{\mathopen{}\left\langle \mathbf{A}, \mathbf{B}\right\rangle\mathclose{}}
\stackrel{\text{def}}{=}
{\operatorname{tr}\mathopen{}\left(\underbrace{{\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\mathbf{B}}_{p \times p}\right)\mathclose{}}
\]





Banerjee and Roy [6, Ch. 15], eq. 15.7, p. 491 defines the same inner product on \(n \times p\) matrices with the trace.







LemTheorem 10 (The matrix inner product multiplies matching entries)






Theorem 10 (The matrix inner product multiplies matching entries) For two \(n \times p\) matrices \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{B}\):
\[
{\mathopen{}\left\langle \mathbf{A}, \mathbf{B}\right\rangle\mathclose{}} = \sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^pa_{ij}\, b_{ij}
\]










ProofProof






Proof. \[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathopen{}\left\langle \mathbf{A}, \mathbf{B}\right\rangle\mathclose{}}
&= \operatorname{tr}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\mathbf{B})
&& \text{(definition of the inner product)}
\\
&= \sum_{j=1}^p({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\mathbf{B})_{jj}
&& \text{(definition of the trace)}
\\
&= \sum_{j=1}^p\sum_{i=1}^n({\mathbf{A}}^{\top})_{ji}\, b_{ij}
&& \text{(definition of matrix multiplication)}
\\
&= \sum_{j=1}^p\sum_{i=1}^na_{ij}\, b_{ij}
&& \text{(definition of the transpose)}
\\
&= \sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^pa_{ij}\, b_{ij}
&& \text{(swap the order of two finite sums)}
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 21 (The inner product of two \(2 \times 2\) matrices)






Example 21 (The inner product of two \(2 \times 2\) matrices) Let
\[
\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{bmatrix}
\qquad
\mathbf{B} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ -1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}
\]
From Definition 14:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathopen{}\left\langle \mathbf{A}, \mathbf{B}\right\rangle\mathclose{}}
&= {\operatorname{tr}\mathopen{}\left({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\mathbf{B}\right)\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(definition of the inner product)}
\\
&= {\operatorname{tr}\mathopen{}\left(
{\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{bmatrix}}^{\top}
\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ -1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}
\right)\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(substitute)}
\\
&= {\operatorname{tr}\mathopen{}\left(
\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 3 \\ 2 & 4 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ -1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}
\right)\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(definition of the transpose)}
\\
&= {\operatorname{tr}\mathopen{}\left(\begin{bmatrix} -3 & 7 \\ -4 & 10 \end{bmatrix}\right)\mathclose{}}
&& \text{(multiply)}
\\
&= -3 + 10
&& \text{(definition of the trace)}
\\
&= 7
&& \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
From Theorem 10:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathopen{}\left\langle \mathbf{A}, \mathbf{B}\right\rangle\mathclose{}}
&= 1 \cdot 0 + 2 \cdot 1 + 3 \cdot(-1) + 4 \cdot 2
&& \text{(multiply matching entries and add)}
\\
&= 0 + 2 - 3 + 8
&& \text{(multiply)}
\\
&= 7
&& \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]










RemarkRemark 9 (The matrix inner product as a dot product)






Remark 9 (The matrix inner product as a dot product). Theorem 10 says that the matrix inner product is the dot product (Definition 8 in Vectors) of the two matrices’ entries, each listed as one vector of length \(np\), with both matrices’ entries listed in the same order.
For example, listing the entries of \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{B}\) in Example 21 row by row gives \((1, 2, 3, 4)\) and \((0, 1, -1, 2)\), and
\[
\begin{aligned}
(1, 2, 3, 4) \cdot (0, 1, -1, 2) &= 7 \\
&= {\mathopen{}\left\langle \mathbf{A}, \mathbf{B}\right\rangle\mathclose{}}.
\end{aligned}
\]










DefDefinition 15 (Frobenius norm)






Definition 15 (Frobenius norm) The Frobenius norm [9] of an \(n \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) is
\[
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{A}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_F \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\sqrt{{\mathopen{}\left\langle \mathbf{A}, \mathbf{A}\right\rangle\mathclose{}}}
\]
where \({\mathopen{}\left\langle \cdot, \cdot\right\rangle\mathclose{}}\) is the matrix inner product (Definition 14).










ExmExample 22 (The Frobenius norm of a \(2 \times 2\) matrix)






Example 22 (The Frobenius norm of a \(2 \times 2\) matrix) For \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{bmatrix}\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{A}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_F
&= \sqrt{{\mathopen{}\left\langle \mathbf{A}, \mathbf{A}\right\rangle\mathclose{}}}
&& \text{(definition of the Frobenius norm)}
\\
&= \sqrt{1 \cdot 1 + 2 \cdot 2 + 3 \cdot 3 + 4 \cdot 4}
&& \text{(sum of products of matching entries)}
\\
&= \sqrt{1 + 4 + 9 + 16}
&& \text{(multiply)}
\\
&= \sqrt{30}
&& \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
The second step is Theorem 10.










RemarkRemark 10 (The Frobenius norm is the Euclidean norm of the entries)






Remark 10 (The Frobenius norm is the Euclidean norm of the entries). By Theorem 10, \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{A}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_F^2 = \sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^pa_{ij}^2\), a sum of squares, so the square root is always defined. \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{A}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_F\) is the Euclidean norm (Definition 20 in Vectors) of the entries of \(\mathbf{A}\), listed as one vector of length \(np\) [6, Ch. 15], Definition 15.4, p. 492.
For example, listing the entries of \(\mathbf{A}\) in Example 22 row by row gives the vector \((1, 2, 3, 4)\) of length \(2 \cdot 2 = 4\), and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert(1, 2, 3, 4)\right\rVert\mathclose{} &= \sqrt{1 + 4 + 9 + 16} \\
&= \sqrt{30} \\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{A}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_F.
\end{aligned}
\]










LemTheorem 11 (The squared Frobenius norm adds up the squared column norms)






Theorem 11 (The squared Frobenius norm adds up the squared column norms) If \(\mathbf{A}\) is an \(n \times p\) matrix with columns \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_p\), then
\[
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{A}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_F^2 = \sum_{j=1}^p\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_j\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2.
\]










ProofProof






Proof. Entry \(i\) of \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_j\) is \(a_{ij}\), so
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{A}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_F^2
&= \sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^pa_{ij}^2
&& \text{(}\href{#rem-frobenius-norm-entries}{\text{Remark~10}}\text{)}
\\
&= \sum_{j=1}^p\sum_{i=1}^na_{ij}^2
&& \text{(swap the order of two finite sums)}
\\
&= \sum_{j=1}^p\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_j\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#def-euclidean-norm}{\text{Definition~20 in Vectors}}\text{, applied to each column)}
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 23 (The Frobenius norm of a \(2 \times 2\) matrix, column by column)






Example 23 (The Frobenius norm of a \(2 \times 2\) matrix, column by column) The matrix \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{bmatrix}\) in Example 22 has columns \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1 = (1, 3)\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_2 = (2, 4)\), so
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{A}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_F^2
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 + \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_2\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-frobenius-norm-columns}{\text{Theorem~11}}\text{)}
\\
&= (1^2 + 3^2) + (2^2 + 4^2)
&& \text{(definition of the norm)}
\\
&= 10 + 20
&& \text{(square and add)}
\\
&= 30
&& \text{(add)}
\end{aligned}
\]
which matches \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{A}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_F = \sqrt{30}\) from Example 22.

4 Matrix Decompositions






DefDefinition 16 (Eigenvalue)






Definition 16 (Eigenvalue) Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be a \(p \times p\) matrix. A real number \(\lambda\) is an eigenvalue of \(\mathbf{A}\) if some real vector \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \neq \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) of length \(p\) satisfies
\[
\underbrace{\mathbf{A}}_{p \times p}\,\underbrace{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}_{p \times 1}
= \lambda\,\underbrace{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}_{p \times 1}
\]










DefDefinition 17 (Eigenvector)






Definition 17 (Eigenvector) In Definition 16, any such \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is an eigenvector of \(\mathbf{A}\) for \(\lambda\).










ExmExample 24 (Eigenvectors of a \(2 \times 2\) matrix)






Example 24 (Eigenvectors of a \(2 \times 2\) matrix) Let \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\).
\((1, 1)\) is an eigenvector for the eigenvalue \(3\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 2 + 1 \\ 1 + 2 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of matrix-vector multiplication)}
\\
&= 3 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(factor out } 3 \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
\((1, -1)\) is an eigenvector for the eigenvalue \(1\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 2 - 1 \\ 1 - 2 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of matrix-vector multiplication)}
\\
&= 1 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(factor out } 1 \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]










RemarkRemark 11 (Multiples of an eigenvector)






Remark 11 (Multiples of an eigenvector). Multiplying by \(\mathbf{A}\) rescales an eigenvector by \(\lambda\) and does not change the line it lies on. Any nonzero multiple \(c\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) of an eigenvector is also an eigenvector for the same \(\lambda\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}(c\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&= c\,\mathbf{A}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(move the scalar } c \text{ to the front)}
\\
&= c\,\lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \text{ is an eigenvector for } \lambda\text{)}
\\
&= \lambda\,(c\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(multiplication of numbers is commutative)}
\end{aligned}
\]
For example, in Example 24, \(2\,(1, 1) = (2, 2)\) is also an eigenvector for the eigenvalue \(3\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 2 \cdot 2 + 1 \cdot 2 \\ 1 \cdot 2 + 2 \cdot 2 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of matrix-vector multiplication)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 4 + 2 \\ 2 + 4 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(multiply)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 6 \\ 6 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(add)}
\\
&= 3 \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(factor out } 3 \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 25 (A vector that is not an eigenvector)






Example 25 (A vector that is not an eigenvector) For the same \(\mathbf{A}\), \((1, 0)\) is not an eigenvector:
\[
\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
= \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
\]
and no number \(\lambda\) gives \((2, 1) = \lambda\,(1, 0)\), because the second entries would need \(1 = \lambda\cdot 0\).










RemarkRemark 12 (Real and complex eigenvalues)






Remark 12 (Real and complex eigenvalues). These notes take \(\lambda\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) to be real. Banerjee and Roy [6, Ch. 11], Definition 11.1, pp. 312-313 also allows complex eigenvalues and eigenvectors, because some real matrices, such as a rotation by \(90\) degrees, have no real eigenvalues [6, Ch. 11], Example 11.2, p. 312.
For example, \(\mathbf{R} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & -1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\) rotates each vector in the plane counterclockwise by \(90\) degrees: \(\mathbf{R}\,(v_1, v_2) = (-v_2, v_1)\). Suppose \(\mathbf{R}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) for a real number \(\lambda\). Matching entries gives \(-v_2 = \lambda v_1\) and \(v_1 = \lambda v_2\). Substituting the second equation into the first gives \(-v_2 = \lambda^2 v_2\), so \((1 + \lambda^2)\, v_2 = 0\). Since \(1 + \lambda^2 &gt; 0\), \(v_2 = 0\), and then
\[
\begin{aligned}
v_1 &= \lambda v_2 \\
&= 0.
\end{aligned}
\]
So the only solution is \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), and \(\mathbf{R}\) has no real eigenvalue.










