DefDefinition 7 (Upper triangular matrix)






Definition 7 (Upper triangular matrix) A square matrix \(\mathbf{U}\) is upper triangular [11] if every entry below the diagonal is \(0\): \(u_{ij} = 0\) whenever \(i &gt; j\).










DefDefinition 8 (Lower triangular matrix)






Definition 8 (Lower triangular matrix) A square matrix \(\mathbf{L}\) is lower triangular if every entry above the diagonal is \(0\): \(\ell_{ij} = 0\) whenever \(i &lt; j\).










DefDefinition 9 (Unit lower triangular matrix)






Definition 9 (Unit lower triangular matrix) A lower triangular matrix (Definition 8) whose diagonal entries are all \(1\) is unit lower triangular.










ExmExample 24 (Triangular and not)






Example 24 (Triangular and not)  


\(\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 & -1 \\ 0 & \frac{1}{2} & \frac{1}{2} \\ 0 & 0 & -1 \end{bmatrix}\) is upper triangular: its entries below the diagonal, in positions \((2, 1)\), \((3, 1)\) and \((3, 2)\), are \(0\).

\(\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 4 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) is unit lower triangular.
A diagonal matrix (Definition 4 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) is both upper and lower triangular.

\(\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\) is neither: its \((2, 1)\) entry is not \(0\), and neither is its \((1, 2)\) entry.











LemTheorem 17 (Solving a triangular system by substitution)






Theorem 17 (Solving a triangular system by substitution) Let \(\mathbf{U}\) be an \(n \times n\) upper triangular matrix (Definition 7) whose diagonal entries \(u_{11}, \ldots, u_{nn}\) are all nonzero. For every \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} \in \mathbb{R}^n\), the system \(\mathbf{U} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\) has exactly one solution, given for \(i = n, n - 1, \ldots, 1\) in turn by
\[
x_i = \frac{1}{u_{ii}} \mathopen{}\left(b_i - \sum_{j=i+1}^{n} u_{ij}\,x_j\right)\mathclose{}
\]
(back substitution). Likewise, if \(\mathbf{L}\) is lower triangular with nonzero diagonal, \(\mathbf{L} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\) has exactly one solution, given for \(i = 1, \ldots, n\) by \(y_i = \frac{1}{\ell_{ii}} \mathopen{}\left(b_i - \sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \ell_{ij}\,y_j\right)\mathclose{}\) (forward substitution).










ProofProof






Proof. Row \(i\) of \(\mathbf{U} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\) reads
\[
\begin{aligned}
b_i
&= \sum_{j=1}^nu_{ij}\,x_j
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matvec-mult}{\text{Definition~13 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \sum_{j=i}^{n} u_{ij}\,x_j
&& \text{(} u_{ij} = 0 \text{ for } j &lt; i \text{)}
\\
&= u_{ii}\,x_i + \sum_{j=i+1}^{n} u_{ij}\,x_j.
&& \text{(split off the } j = i \text{ term)}
\end{aligned}
\]
This equation is equivalent to each of
\[
\begin{aligned}
u_{ii}\,x_i &= b_i - \sum_{j=i+1}^{n} u_{ij}\,x_j
&& \text{(subtract the sum from both sides)}
\\
x_i &= \frac{1}{u_{ii}} \mathopen{}\left(b_i - \sum_{j=i+1}^{n} u_{ij}\,x_j\right)\mathclose{}.
&& \text{(divide by } u_{ii} \ne 0 \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Row \(n\) involves only \(x_n\), so it fixes \(x_n\); once \(x_{i+1}, \ldots, x_n\) are fixed, row \(i\) fixes \(x_i\). So the rows, taken from the last to the first, hold exactly when \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) is the vector the formula builds: there is one solution, and only one. For \(\mathbf{L}\), row \(i\) reads \(\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \ell_{ij}\,y_j + \ell_{ii}\,y_i = b_i\), and the same argument runs from the first row to the last.










ExmExample 25 (Back substitution on a \(3 \times 3\) system)






Example 25 (Back substitution on a \(3 \times 3\) system) Solve \(\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 & -1 \\ 0 & \frac{1}{2} & \frac{1}{2} \\ 0 & 0 & -1 \end{bmatrix} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
= \begin{bmatrix} 8 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}\):

\[
\begin{aligned}
x_3 &= \frac{1}{-1} \cdot 1 \\
&= -1;
\end{aligned}
\]
\[
\begin{aligned}
x_2 &= \frac{1}{1/2} \mathopen{}\left(1 - \tfrac{1}{2} \cdot(-1)\right)\mathclose{} \\
&= 2 \cdot\tfrac{3}{2} \\
&= 3;
\end{aligned}
\]
\[
\begin{aligned}
x_1 &= \frac{1}{2} \mathopen{}\left(8 - 1 \cdot 3 - (-1)(-1)\right)\mathclose{} \\
&= \frac{1}{2} \cdot 4 \\
&= 2.
\end{aligned}
\]

So \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = (2, 3, -1)\). Without a nonzero diagonal the method fails: \(\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = (1, 1)\) has no solution, since its second row reads \(0 = 1\).










LemTheorem 18 (QR factorization)






Theorem 18 (QR factorization) Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be an \(m \times n\) matrix with \(\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{A}) = n\). Then
\[
\underbrace{\mathbf{A}}_{m \times n} = \underbrace{\mathbf{Q}}_{m \times n}\,\underbrace{\mathbf{R}}_{n \times n},
\]
where the columns of \(\mathbf{Q}\) are orthonormal (Definition 23 in Vectors) and \(\mathbf{R}\) is upper triangular (Definition 7) with positive diagonal entries.










ProofProof






Proof. The \(n\) columns \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_n\) of \(\mathbf{A}\) are linearly independent (Definition 3 in Subspaces and Rank), so the Gram-Schmidt process (Definition 11 in Inner Products and Orthogonality) runs all \(n\) steps and gives orthonormal \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_n\) (Theorem 11 in Inner Products and Orthogonality, parts 1 and 3); let \(\mathbf{Q}\) have these columns. At step \(i\), \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), and rearranging the orthogonalize step,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i + \sum_{j=1}^{i-1} (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j
&& \text{(add the sum to both sides of the orthogonalize step)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i\right\rVert\mathclose{}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i + \sum_{j=1}^{i-1} (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j.
&& \text{(normalize step: } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i = \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i\right\rVert\mathclose{}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Let \(\mathbf{R}\) be the \(n \times n\) matrix with \(r_{ji} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i\) for \(j &lt; i\), \(r_{ii} = \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i\right\rVert\mathclose{} &gt; 0\), and \(r_{ji} = 0\) for \(j &gt; i\); it is upper triangular with positive diagonal. Column \(i\) of \(\mathbf{Q} \mathbf{R}\) is \(\mathbf{Q}\) times column \(i\) of \(\mathbf{R}\) (Definition 7 in Matrices), and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{Q}\,(r_{1i}, \ldots, r_{ni})
&= \sum_{j=1}^nr_{ji}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-subspaces.qmd#thm-matvec-columns}{\text{Theorem~3 in Subspaces and Rank}}\text{)}
\\
&= \sum_{j=1}^{i} r_{ji}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j
&& \text{(} r_{ji} = 0 \text{ for } j &gt; i \text{)}
\\
&= r_{ii}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i + \sum_{j=1}^{i-1} r_{ji}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j
&& \text{(split off the } j = i \text{ term)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i\right\rVert\mathclose{}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i + \sum_{j=1}^{i-1} (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j
&& \text{(the entries of } \mathbf{R} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i.
&& \text{(the display)}
\end{aligned}
\]
So \(\mathbf{Q} \mathbf{R} = \mathbf{A}\).










ExmExample 26 (A QR factorization from Gram-Schmidt)






Example 26 (A QR factorization from Gram-Schmidt) For \(\mathbf{A}\) with columns \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1 = (1, 1, 0)\), \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_2 = (1, 0, 1)\), \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_3 = (0, 1, 1)\), Example 23 in Inner Products and Orthogonality and Example 25 in Inner Products and Orthogonality found \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,(1, 1, 0)\), \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\,(1, -1, 2)\), \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_3 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\,(-1, 1, 1)\), with \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_1\right\rVert\mathclose{} = \sqrt{2}\), \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_2\right\rVert\mathclose{} = \sqrt{3/2}\), \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_3\right\rVert\mathclose{} = 2/\sqrt{3}\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_2 &= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_3 \\
&= \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\end{aligned}
\]
and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2 \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_3 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\). So
\[
\mathbf{R} = \begin{bmatrix}
\sqrt{2} & \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\
0 & \sqrt{3/2} & \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}} \\
0 & 0 & \tfrac{2}{\sqrt{3}}
\end{bmatrix}.
\]
As a check on the second column,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 + \sqrt{3/2}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2 &= \tfrac{1}{2}\,(1, 1, 0) + \tfrac{1}{2}\,(1, -1, 2) \\
&= (1, 0, 1) \\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_2.
\end{aligned}
\]










DefDefinition 10 (Least squares solution)






Definition 10 (Least squares solution) Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be \(m \times n\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} \in \mathbb{R}^m\). A least squares solution [12] of \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\) is a vector \(\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} \in \mathbb{R}^n\) such that
\[
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - \mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} \le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
\quad \text{for every } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \in \mathbb{R}^n.
\]










ExmExample 27 (Fitting a constant)






Example 27 (Fitting a constant) Let \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} = (1, 3)\). The system \(x\,(1, 1) = (1, 3)\) is inconsistent (Definition 6): its first entry needs \(x = 1\) and its second needs \(x = 3\). For any number \(x\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - \mathbf{A} x\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 &= (1 - x)^2 + (3 - x)^2 \\
&= 2\,(x - 2)^2 + 2,
\end{aligned}
\]
which is smallest at \(x = 2\), so \(\hat{x} = 2\) is the least squares solution. The value \(x = 1\) is not one: it gives \(0 + 4 = 4 &gt; 2\). The normal equations read \(2x = 4\), and their solution is \(x = 2\) too.










DefDefinition 11 (Normal equations)






Definition 11 (Normal equations) For \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\) as in Definition 10, the equations \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = {\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\) are the normal equations [13].










LemTheorem 19 (Least squares solutions solve the normal equations)






Theorem 19 (Least squares solutions solve the normal equations) Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be \(m \times n\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} \in \mathbb{R}^m\).


\(\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}\) is a least squares solution (Definition 10) exactly when it solves the normal equations \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} = {\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\).
The normal equations are consistent (Definition 6).
Every least squares solution gives the same fitted vector \(\mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}\): the orthogonal projection of \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\) onto \(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})\) (Definition 3).
The least squares solution is unique exactly when \(\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{A}) = n\), and then \(\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} = ({\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathbf{A})^{-1} {\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\).











ProofProof






Proof. Let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\) be the orthogonal projection of \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\) onto \(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})\). The vectors \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\), as \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) ranges over \(\mathbb{R}^n\), are exactly the points of \(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})\) (Definition 1 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity). Least squares solutions are the solutions of \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\). If \(\mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\), then for every \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\), \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \in \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})\), so \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - \mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} \le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\) (Theorem 3), and \(\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}\) is a least squares solution. Conversely, let \(\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}\) be a least squares solution. \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} \in \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})\), so \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} = \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_0\) for some \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_0\) (Definition 1 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity), and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - \mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
&\le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_0\right\rVert\mathclose{}
&& \text{(}\href{#def-least-squares}{\text{Definition~10}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
&& \text{(} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} = \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_0 \text{)}
\\
&\le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - \mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}\right\rVert\mathclose{},
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-closest-point}{\text{Theorem~3}}\text{, with } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} = \mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} \in \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A}) \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so the last inequality is an equality, and the equality case of Theorem 3 gives \(\mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\).
Parts 1 and 3. \(\mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\) holds exactly when \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - \mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} \in \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})^\perp\): if \(\mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\), then \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} \in \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})^\perp\) (Definition 3); conversely, if \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - \mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} \in \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})^\perp\), then \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} = \mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} + (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - \mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}})\) is a split into \(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})\) and \(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})^\perp\), so \(\mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\) by the uniqueness in Theorem 8 in Direct Sums and Orthogonal Complements. And \(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})^\perp = \mathcal{N}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top})\) (Theorem 7 in Direct Sums and Orthogonal Complements), where
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - \mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}})
&= {\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - {\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}})
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7}}\text{)}
\\
&= {\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - {\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}},
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
which is \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_n\) exactly when the normal equations hold.
Part 2. The vector \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_0\) above satisfies \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_0 = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\), so it is a least squares solution, and it solves the normal equations by part 1.
Part 4. The least squares solutions are exactly the solutions of \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\), a consistent system, so there is exactly one when \(\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{A}) = n\) and more than one otherwise (Corollary 1). When \(\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{A}) = n\), \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathbf{A}\) is invertible (Theorem 1), and with \(\mathbf{M} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}{\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathbf{A}\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}
&= \mathbf{I}_n\,\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= (\mathbf{M}^{-1} \mathbf{M})\,\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-inverse}{\text{Definition~5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{M}^{-1}\,(\mathbf{M} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{M}^{-1}\,{\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}.
&& \text{(the normal equations)}
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 28 (A least squares line)






Example 28 (A least squares line) Fit \(y = x_1 + x_2\,t\) to the points \((t, y) = (1, 1), (2, 2), (3, 2)\): \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \\ 1 & 3 \end{bmatrix}\) (the \(\mathbf{X}\) of Example 1) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} = (1, 2, 2)\). \(\mathbf{A}\) has rank \(2\) (Example 2 in Subspaces and Rank), so the solution is unique (part 4). \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 6 \\ 6 & 14 \end{bmatrix}\) has inverse \(\frac{1}{6}\begin{bmatrix} 14 & -6 \\ -6 & 3 \end{bmatrix}\) (Example 1), and
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} &= (1 + 2 + 2,\; 1 + 4 + 6) \\
&= (5, 11),
\end{aligned}
\]
so
\[
\begin{aligned}
\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}
&= \frac{1}{6} \mathopen{}\left(14 \cdot 5 - 6 \cdot 11,\; -6 \cdot 5 + 3 \cdot 11\right)\mathclose{} \\
&= \frac{1}{6}\,(4, 3) \\
&= \mathopen{}\left(\tfrac{2}{3}, \tfrac{1}{2}\right)\mathclose{}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The fitted values are \(\mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} = \mathopen{}\left(\tfrac{7}{6}, \tfrac{5}{3}, \tfrac{13}{6}\right)\mathclose{}\), and the residuals \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} - \mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} = \mathopen{}\left(-\tfrac{1}{6}, \tfrac{1}{3}, -\tfrac{1}{6}\right)\mathclose{}\) are orthogonal to both columns of \(\mathbf{A}\): \(-\tfrac{1}{6} + \tfrac{1}{3} - \tfrac{1}{6} = 0\) and \(-\tfrac{1}{6} + \tfrac{2}{3} - \tfrac{1}{2} = 0\).










ExmExample 29 (Many least squares solutions, one fitted vector)






Example 29 (Many least squares solutions, one fitted vector) With the rank-\(1\) matrix \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 1 & 2 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) (Example 2 in Subspaces and Rank) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} = (1, 2, 2)\), \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 6 \\ 6 & 12 \end{bmatrix}\) and \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} = (5, 10)\), so both normal equations say \(3 x_1 + 6 x_2 = 5\), that is, \(x_1 + 2 x_2 = \tfrac{5}{3}\). There are infinitely many least squares solutions, such as \((\tfrac{5}{3}, 0)\) and \((0, \tfrac{5}{6})\), as part 4 predicts for \(\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{A}) = 1 &lt; 2\); but every one gives the same fitted vector
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} &= (x_1 + 2 x_2)\,(1, 1, 1) \\
&= \tfrac{5}{3}\,(1, 1, 1),
\end{aligned}
\]
as part 3 says.










LemTheorem 20 (Solving least squares by QR)






Theorem 20 (Solving least squares by QR) Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be \(m \times n\) with \(\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{A}) = n\), with QR factorization \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{Q} \mathbf{R}\) (Theorem 18), and let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} \in \mathbb{R}^m\). The least squares solution (Definition 10) is the unique solution of
\[
\underbrace{\mathbf{R}}_{n \times n}\,\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} = \underbrace{{\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}}_{n \times m}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}},
\]
which back substitution computes (Theorem 17). When \(m = n\), it is the unique solution of \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\).










ProofProof






Proof. \(\mathbf{R}\) is upper triangular with positive diagonal, so \(\mathbf{R} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = {\mathbf{Q}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\) has exactly one solution \(\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}\) (Theorem 17). \({\mathbf{Q}}^{\top} \mathbf{Q} = \mathbf{I}_n\), as in the proof of Theorem 4. Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}
&= {(\mathbf{Q} \mathbf{R})}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{Q} \mathbf{R})\,\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}
&& \text{(substitute } \mathbf{A} = \mathbf{Q} \mathbf{R} \text{)}
\\
&= {\mathbf{R}}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{Q} \mathbf{R})\,\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-transpose-product}{\text{Theorem~17 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= {\mathbf{R}}^{\top}\,({\mathbf{Q}}^{\top} \mathbf{Q})\,(\mathbf{R} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= {\mathbf{R}}^{\top}\,\mathbf{I}_n\,(\mathbf{R} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}})
&& \text{(} {\mathbf{Q}}^{\top} \mathbf{Q} = \mathbf{I}_n \text{)}
\\
&= {\mathbf{R}}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{R} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= {\mathbf{R}}^{\top}\,({\mathbf{Q}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}})
&& \text{(} \mathbf{R} \hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} = {\mathbf{Q}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} \text{)}
\\
&= ({\mathbf{R}}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{Q}}^{\top})\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= {(\mathbf{Q} \mathbf{R})}^{\top}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-transpose-product}{\text{Theorem~17 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= {\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}},
&& \text{(} \mathbf{A} = \mathbf{Q} \mathbf{R} \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}\) solves the normal equations and is the least squares solution (Theorem 19), which is unique because \(\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{A}) = n\). When \(m = n\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{A}) &= n \\
&= m,
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\) is consistent for every \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\) (Corollary 1, part 1), so its solution leaves residual \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), is a least squares solution, and is therefore \(\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}\).










ExmExample 30 (The least squares line by QR)






Example 30 (The least squares line by QR) For \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\) of Example 28, Gram-Schmidt gives \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\,(1, 1, 1)\); then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 \cdot (1, 2, 3) &= \tfrac{6}{\sqrt{3}} \\
&= 2\sqrt{3},
\end{aligned}
\]
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_2 &= (1, 2, 3) - 2\,(1, 1, 1) \\
&= (-1, 0, 1)
\end{aligned}
\]
with norm \(\sqrt{2}\), and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,(-1, 0, 1)\). So
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{R}
&= \begin{bmatrix} \sqrt{3} & 2\sqrt{3} \\ 0 & \sqrt{2} \end{bmatrix}, \\
{\mathbf{Q}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}
&= \mathopen{}\left(\tfrac{1 + 2 + 2}{\sqrt{3}},\; \tfrac{-1 + 0 + 2}{\sqrt{2}}\right)\mathclose{} \\
&= \mathopen{}\left(\tfrac{5}{\sqrt{3}}, \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)\mathclose{}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Back substitution gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
x_2 &= \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \cdot \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\
&= \tfrac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}
\]
and
\[
\begin{aligned}
x_1 &= \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{3}} \mathopen{}\left(\tfrac{5}{\sqrt{3}} - 2\sqrt{3} \cdot \tfrac{1}{2}\right)\mathclose{} \\
&= \tfrac{5}{3} - 1 \\
&= \tfrac{2}{3},
\end{aligned}
\]
the solution found in Example 28.










DefDefinition 12 (LU factorization)






Definition 12 (LU factorization) An LU factorization [14] of an \(n \times n\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) is a product \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{L} \mathbf{U}\) with \(\mathbf{L}\) unit lower triangular (Definition 9) and \(\mathbf{U}\) upper triangular (Definition 7).










ExmExample 31 (An LU factorization of a \(3 \times 3\) matrix)






Example 31 (An LU factorization of a \(3 \times 3\) matrix) Let \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 & -1 \\ -3 & -1 & 2 \\ -2 & 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\), \(\mathbf{L} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ -\frac{3}{2} & 1 & 0 \\ -1 & 4 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) (unit lower triangular) and \(\mathbf{U} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 & -1 \\ 0 & \frac{1}{2} & \frac{1}{2} \\ 0 & 0 & -1 \end{bmatrix}\) (upper triangular). By Definition 7 in Matrices, entry \((i, j)\) of \(\mathbf{L} \mathbf{U}\) is \(\sum_k \ell_{ik}\,u_{kj}\), so row \(i\) of \(\mathbf{L} \mathbf{U}\) is \(\sum_k \ell_{ik}\) times row \(k\) of \(\mathbf{U}\). Row 1 is \(1 \cdot(2, 1, -1) = (2, 1, -1)\); row 2 is \(-\tfrac{3}{2}\,(2, 1, -1) + (0, \tfrac{1}{2}, \tfrac{1}{2}) = (-3, -1, 2)\), and row 3 is \(-(2, 1, -1) + 4\,(0, \tfrac{1}{2}, \tfrac{1}{2}) + (0, 0, -1) = (-2, 1, 2)\). These are the rows of \(\mathbf{A}\), so \(\mathbf{L} \mathbf{U} = \mathbf{A}\) is an LU factorization. The below-diagonal entries of \(\mathbf{L}\) are the negatives of the multiples of earlier rows that Gaussian elimination adds to reduce \(\mathbf{A}\) to \(\mathbf{U}\).










ExmExample 32 (An invertible matrix with no LU factorization)






Example 32 (An invertible matrix with no LU factorization) \(\mathbf{P} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\) is invertible: \(\mathbf{P}^2 = \mathbf{I}_2\), so \(\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \mathbf{P}\) (Definition 5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions); but it has no LU factorization. If \(\mathbf{P} = \mathbf{L} \mathbf{U}\), the \((1, 1)\) entry gives \(0 = 1 \cdot u_{11}\), so \(u_{11} = 0\), and then the \((2, 1)\) entry gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
1 &= \ell_{21}\,u_{11} \\
&= 0,
\end{aligned}
\]
which is impossible. Swapping the two rows first removes the obstacle: the swapped matrix is \(\mathbf{I}_2 = \mathbf{I}_2\,\mathbf{I}_2\).










LemTheorem 21 (Solving a system with an LU factorization)






Theorem 21 (Solving a system with an LU factorization) If \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{L} \mathbf{U}\) is an LU factorization (Definition 12) and the diagonal entries of \(\mathbf{U}\) are all nonzero, then for every \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} \in \mathbb{R}^n\) the system \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\) has exactly one solution: solve \(\mathbf{L} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\) by forward substitution, then \(\mathbf{U} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\) by back substitution (Theorem 17).










ProofProof






Proof. \(\mathbf{L}\) has diagonal entries \(1\) and \(\mathbf{U}\) has nonzero ones, so each of the two triangular systems has exactly one solution (Theorem 17). If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) are those solutions, then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} &= \mathbf{L}\,(\mathbf{U} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) \\
&= \mathbf{L} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}
\end{aligned}
\]
(Theorem 5 in Matrices), so \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) is a solution. Conversely, if \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\), then \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathbf{U} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) satisfies
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{L} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} &= \mathbf{L}\,(\mathbf{U} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) \\
&= (\mathbf{L} \mathbf{U})\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \\
&= \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}
\end{aligned}
\]
(Theorem 5 in Matrices), so \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\) is the unique solution of that system, and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) is then the unique solution of \(\mathbf{U} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\).










ExmExample 33 (Two triangular solves)






Example 33 (Two triangular solves) With \(\mathbf{L}\) and \(\mathbf{U}\) of Example 31, solve \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = (8, -11, -3)\). Forward substitution on \(\mathbf{L} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = (8, -11, -3)\) gives \(y_1 = 8\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
y_2 &= -11 + \tfrac{3}{2} \cdot 8 \\
&= 1,
\end{aligned}
\]
\[
\begin{aligned}
y_3 &= -3 + 8 - 4 \cdot 1 \\
&= 1.
\end{aligned}
\]
Back substitution on \(\mathbf{U} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = (8, 1, 1)\) is Example 25, which gives \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = (2, 3, -1)\). As a check,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}\,(2, 3, -1) &= (4 + 3 + 1,\; -6 - 3 - 2,\; -4 + 3 - 2) \\
&= (8, -11, -3).
\end{aligned}
\]

DefDefinition 1 (Design matrix, model matrix)






Definition 1 (Design matrix, model matrix) In a regression model with \(n\) observations and \(p\) predictors, the design matrix [1] (or model matrix) \(\mathbf{X}\) is the \(n \times p\) matrix whose \(i\)-th row \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_i}^{\top}\) holds, for observation \(i\), the \(p\) entries the model uses. These can be covariates, a \(1\) for the intercept, \(0/1\) columns for categories, or functions of covariates, such as \(x^2\). For a straight-line fit, for example, the design matrix has a first column of 1s, which allows the intercept to be estimated [1], [2, Sec. 2.4]. In symbols,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{X}
&=
\begin{bmatrix}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_1}^{\top} \\
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_2}^{\top} \\
\vdots \\
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_n}^{\top}
\end{bmatrix} \\
&=
\begin{bmatrix}
x_{11} & x_{12} & \cdots & x_{1p} \\
x_{21} & x_{22} & \cdots & x_{2p} \\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
x_{n1} & x_{n2} & \cdots & x_{np}
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
\]










RemarkRemark 1 (Products with the design matrix)






Remark 1 (Products with the design matrix). The product \(\mathbf{X}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\beta}}\vphantom{\beta}}\) collects the values \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_i}^{\top}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\beta}}\vphantom{\beta}}\) for all \(n\) observations into a single \(n \times 1\) vector:
\[
\mathbf{X}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\beta}}\vphantom{\beta}}=
\begin{bmatrix}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_1}^{\top}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\beta}}\vphantom{\beta}}\\
\vdots \\
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_n}^{\top}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\beta}}\vphantom{\beta}}
\end{bmatrix}
\]
For example, take the rank-\(2\) matrix from Example 2 in Subspaces and Rank as \(\mathbf{X}\), with \(n = 3\) observations and \(p = 2\) columns: an intercept column and one covariate. With \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\beta}}\vphantom{\beta}}= (1, 2)\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{X}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\beta}}\vphantom{\beta}}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \\ 1 & 3 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 5 \\ 7 \end{bmatrix}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The matrix \({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\) is \(p \times p\) and symmetric, since
\[
\begin{aligned}
{({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})}^{\top} &= {\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\,{({\mathbf{X}}^{\top})}^{\top} \\
&= {\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}
\end{aligned}
\]
(Theorem 17 in Matrices). When \({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\) is invertible, it appears in the least squares solution (Definition 10, defined later in this chapter) \(\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\beta}}\vphantom{\beta}}}= ({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\).










LemTheorem 1 (\({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\) is invertible when \(\mathbf{X}\) has full column rank)






Theorem 1 (\({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\) is invertible when \(\mathbf{X}\) has full column rank) If \(\mathbf{X}\) is an \(n \times p\) matrix with \(\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{X}) = p\) (Definition 2 in Subspaces and Rank), then the \(p \times p\) matrix \({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\) is invertible.










ProofProof






Proof. Let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}}\) be a vector of length \(p\) with \({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
0
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}}}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}}
&& \text{(multiply } {\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\text{ on the left by } {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}}}^{\top} \text{)}
\\
&= {\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{X}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{X}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}}\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(transpose of a product)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{X}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(definition of the Euclidean norm)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\mathbf{X}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\). Because \(\mathbf{X}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}} = c_1 (\text{column } 1) + \cdots + c_p (\text{column } p)\) and the columns of \(\mathbf{X}\) are linearly independent, \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\). So the only solution of \({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) is \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), which says that the columns of the square matrix \({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\) are linearly independent, and a square matrix with linearly independent columns is invertible [3], Corollary 5.7, p. 143.










ExmExample 1 (Inverting \({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\))






Example 1 (Inverting \({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\)) For the rank-\(2\) matrix \(\mathbf{X}= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \\ 1 & 3 \end{bmatrix}\) from Example 2 in Subspaces and Rank:
\[
{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}= \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 6 \\ 6 & 14 \end{bmatrix},
\qquad
{\mathopen{}\left({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\right)\mathclose{}}^{-1} = \frac{1}{6}\begin{bmatrix} 14 & -6 \\ -6 & 3 \end{bmatrix},
\]
and multiplying the two out gives \(\mathbf{I}_2\).










DefDefinition 2 (Hat matrix)






Definition 2 (Hat matrix) For an \(n \times p\) design matrix \(\mathbf{X}\) (Definition 1) with \(\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{X}) = p\), the hat matrix [4] is the \(n \times n\) matrix
\[
\underbrace{\mathbf{H}}_{n \times n}
\stackrel{\text{def}}{=}
\underbrace{\mathbf{X}}_{n \times p}
\underbrace{({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}}_{p \times p}
\underbrace{{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}}_{p \times n}
\]
The inverse exists by Theorem 1.










ExmExample 2 (The hat matrix of an intercept-only model)






Example 2 (The hat matrix of an intercept-only model) With \(n = 2\) observations and only an intercept, \(\mathbf{X}= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}\) (\(2 \times 1\), rank \(1\)), so \({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}= 2\) and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{H}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \cdot\frac{1}{2} \cdot\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & 0.5 \\ 0.5 & 0.5 \end{bmatrix}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Then \(\mathbf{H}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}= (\bar{y}, \bar{y})\), where \(\bar{y} = (y_1 + y_2)/2\): the fitted values of an intercept-only model are the sample mean.










LemTheorem 2 (Hat matrix is a projection matrix)






Theorem 2 (Hat matrix is a projection matrix) If \(\mathbf{X}\) is an \(n \times p\) design matrix with \(\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{X}) = p\), then the hat matrix \(\mathbf{H}\) (Definition 2) is an orthogonal projection matrix (Definition 8 in Special Matrices and Decompositions).










ProofProof






Proof. We verify symmetry and idempotency. Both use that \({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\) is symmetric,
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathopen{}\left({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top} &= {\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\,{\mathopen{}\left({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top} \\
&= {\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}
\end{aligned}
\]
(Theorem 17 in Matrices), so its inverse is symmetric too (Corollary 1 in Special Matrices and Decompositions).
Symmetry: \[\begin{aligned}
{\mathbf{H}}^{\top}
&= {\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{X}({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{H} \text{)}
\\
&= {({\mathbf{X}}^{\top})}^{\top} \cdot {\mathopen{}\left(({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top} \cdot {\mathbf{X}}^{\top}
&& \text{(transpose of a product, twice)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X}\cdot {\mathopen{}\left(({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top} \cdot {\mathbf{X}}^{\top}
&& \text{(transposing twice changes nothing)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X}\cdot ({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1} \cdot {\mathbf{X}}^{\top}
&& \text{(the inverse of a symmetric matrix is symmetric)}
\\
&= \mathbf{H}
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{H} \text{)}
\end{aligned}\]
Idempotency: \[\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{H}^2
&= \mathbf{X}({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top} \cdot \mathbf{X}({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{H} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X}({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}
&& \text{(regroup; matrix multiplication is associative)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X}\,\mathbf{I}_p\,({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}
&& \text{(definition of the inverse)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X}({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}
&& \text{(identity matrix)}
\\
&= \mathbf{H}
&& \text{(definition of } \mathbf{H} \text{)}
\end{aligned}\]










ExmExample 3 (The intercept-only hat matrix is a projection)






Example 3 (The intercept-only hat matrix is a projection) For \(\mathbf{H} = \begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & 0.5 \\ 0.5 & 0.5 \end{bmatrix}\) from Example 2, \({\mathbf{H}}^{\top} = \mathbf{H}\), and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{H}^2
&= \begin{bmatrix} 0.5 \cdot 0.5 + 0.5 \cdot 0.5 & 0.5 \cdot 0.5 + 0.5 \cdot 0.5 \\ 0.5 \cdot 0.5 + 0.5 \cdot 0.5 & 0.5 \cdot 0.5 + 0.5 \cdot 0.5 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & 0.5 \\ 0.5 & 0.5 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \mathbf{H},
\end{aligned}
\]
as Theorem 2 says.










RemarkRemark 2 (Why it is called the hat matrix)






Remark 2 (Why it is called the hat matrix). The hat matrix gives the fitted values of the least squares solution (Definition 10, defined later in this chapter):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}} &= \mathbf{X}\hat{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\beta}}\vphantom{\beta}}}\\
&= \mathbf{X}({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X})^{-1}{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\\
&= \mathbf{H}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Multiplying by \(\mathbf{H}\) “puts a hat” on \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\), which is where the name comes from. In Example 2, \(\mathbf{H}\) puts a hat on \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\) by replacing each \(y_i\) with the sample mean \(\bar{y}\).











