This section is adapted from Zhou [6] and Zhou [10], used under the MIT License (see the license text in Section 2 in Subspaces and Rank). The first source states that every invertible matrix has an LU factorization; that is false without reordering rows (the \(2 \times 2\) row swap \(\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\) has none), so this section states only how an LU factorization, when one exists, solves a system. The least squares results are proved here from the closest-point theorem (Theorem 3) rather than by differentiating, and the QR factorization is derived from Gram-Schmidt (Theorem 11 in Inner Products and Orthogonality). These parts of the source are not part of this section:

