the identity relating the average, the standard deviation and the root mean square of a vector












LemTheorem 1 (Basic properties of the Euclidean norm)






Theorem 1 (Basic properties of the Euclidean norm) For any \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \in \mathbb{R}^p\) and any number \(c\):


\(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} \ge 0\), and \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = 0\) if and only if \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\);

\(\mathopen{}\left\lVert c\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = \mathopen{}\left|c\right|\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\).











ProofProof






Proof. Part 1. \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = \sqrt{x_1^2 + \cdots + x_p^2}\) (Equation 2 in Vectors) is the square root of a sum of squares, so it is at least \(0\). It is \(0\) exactly when \(x_1^2 + \cdots + x_p^2 = 0\), and a sum of squares is \(0\) exactly when every term is \(0\), that is, when every \(x_i = 0\): if some \(x_i \ne 0\), its square is positive and the sum is positive, and if every \(x_i = 0\), the sum is \(0\).
Part 2.
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert c\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
&= \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^p(c\,x_i)^2}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#eq-l2-norm}{\text{Equation~2 in Vectors}}\text{, and }\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-scalar-mult}{\text{Definition~6 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^pc^2 x_i^2}
&& \text{(} (c\,x_i)^2 = c^2 x_i^2 \text{)}
\\
&= \sqrt{c^2 \sum_{i=1}^px_i^2}
&& \text{(factor } c^2 \text{ out of the sum)}
\\
&= \sqrt{c^2}\,\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^px_i^2}
&& \text{(the square root of a product of nonnegative numbers)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left|c\right|\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}.
&& \text{(} \sqrt{c^2} = \mathopen{}\left|c\right|\mathclose{} \text{, and }\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#eq-l2-norm}{\text{Equation~2 in Vectors}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 6 (Scaling a vector scales its length)






Example 6 (Scaling a vector scales its length) For \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = (3, 4)\), \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = 5\) (Example 15 in Vectors). Then \(-2\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = (-6, -8)\) has norm
\[
\begin{aligned}
\sqrt{36 + 64} &= 10 \\
&= \mathopen{}\left|-2\right|\mathclose{} \cdot 5,
\end{aligned}
\]
as part 2 says. Dividing by the norm gives a vector of length \(1\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\tfrac{1}{5}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} &= \tfrac{1}{5} \cdot 5 \\
&= 1,
\end{aligned}
\]
and \(\tfrac{1}{5}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = (0.6, 0.8)\) is the unit vector in Example 15 in Vectors.










LemTheorem 2 (The squared norm of a sum)






Theorem 2 (The squared norm of a sum) For any \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \in \mathbb{R}^p\),
\[
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 + 2\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) + \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2.
\]
In particular, if \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \perp \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\) (Definition 19 in Vectors), then \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 = \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 + \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2\) (the Pythagorean theorem) [5].










ProofProof






Proof. \[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&= (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) \cdot (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})
&& \text{(square both sides of }\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#eq-l2-norm}{\text{Equation~2 in Vectors}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \cdot (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-direct-sums.qmd#thm-dot-linear}{\text{Theorem~5 in Direct Sums and Orthogonal Complements}}\text{, first slot, } a \text{ and } b \text{ both equal to } 1 \text{)}
\\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-direct-sums.qmd#thm-dot-linear}{\text{Theorem~5 in Direct Sums and Orthogonal Complements}}\text{, second slot, } a \text{ and } b \text{ both equal to } 1 \text{, twice)}
\\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#thm-lincom-symmetric}{\text{Theorem~1 in Vectors}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + 2\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}
&& \text{(combine like terms)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 + 2\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) + \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2.
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#eq-l2-norm}{\text{Equation~2 in Vectors}}\text{, squared)}
\end{aligned}
\]
If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \perp \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\), then \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = 0\), and the middle term drops out.










ExmExample 7 (Checking the expansion)






Example 7 (Checking the expansion) For \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = (3, 0)\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = (1, 4)\): \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = (4, 4)\), so
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 &= 16 + 16 \\
&= 32,
\end{aligned}
\]
and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 + 2\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) + \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 &= 9 + 2 \cdot 3 + 17 \\
&= 32.
\end{aligned}
\]
For the orthogonal vectors \((3, 0)\) and \((0, 4)\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert(3, 4)\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 &= 25 \\
&= 9 + 16,
\end{aligned}
\]
the \(3\)-\(4\)-\(5\) right triangle.










LemTheorem 3 (Cauchy-Schwarz inequality)






Theorem 3 (Cauchy-Schwarz inequality) For any \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \in \mathbb{R}^p\),
\[
\mathopen{}\left|\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right|\mathclose{} \le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{},
\]
with equality exactly when \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\) are linearly dependent (Definition 1 in Subspaces and Rank) [6].










ProofProof






Proof. If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}&= \sum_i x_i \cdot 0 \\
&= 0
\end{aligned}
\]

and \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = 0\) (Theorem 1, part 1), so both sides are \(0\) and equality holds; and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\) are linearly dependent, because \(0\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + 1\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\).

If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\). Then \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} &gt; 0\) (Theorem 1, part 1). Let \(t \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) / \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} - t\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + (-1)\,(t\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + (-1)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + (-t)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(} (-1)\,(t\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) = (-t)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \text{, entrywise)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 + 2\,\mathopen{}\left(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot ((-t)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})\right)\mathclose{} + \mathopen{}\left\lVert(-t)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-norm-sum-square}{\text{Theorem~2}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 + 2\,(-t)\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) + \mathopen{}\left\lVert(-t)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-direct-sums.qmd#thm-dot-linear}{\text{Theorem~5 in Direct Sums and Orthogonal Complements}}\text{, special case } b = 0 \text{)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 + 2\,(-t)\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) + \mathopen{}\left(\mathopen{}\left|-t\right|\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\right)\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-norm-properties}{\text{Theorem~1}}\text{, part 2)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 + 2\,(-t)\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) + \mathopen{}\left|-t\right|\mathclose{}^2\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(} (ab)^2 = a^2 b^2 \text{)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 - 2t\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) + t^2\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(} 2\,(-t) = -2t \text{ and } \mathopen{}\left|-t\right|\mathclose{}^2 = t^2 \text{)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 - 2\,\frac{(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})^2}{\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2}
+ \frac{(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})^2}{\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^4}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(substitute } t \text{)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 - 2\,\frac{(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})^2}{\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2}
+ \frac{(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})^2}{\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2}
&& \text{(cancel one factor } \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 \text{)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 - \frac{(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})^2}{\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2}.
&& \text{(combine the last two terms)}
\end{aligned}
\tag{1}\]
The left side is at least \(0\) (Theorem 1, part 1), so
\[
\begin{aligned}
0
&\le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 - \frac{(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})^2}{\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2}
&& \text{(}\href{#eq-cauchy-schwarz-chain}{\text{Equation~1}}\text{)}
\\
\frac{(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})^2}{\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2}
&\le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(add } (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})^2 / \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 \text{ to both sides)}
\\
(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})^2
&\le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2,
&& \text{(multiply both sides by } \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 &gt; 0 \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
and taking nonnegative square roots of both sides, which preserves the inequality, gives \(\mathopen{}\left|\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right|\mathclose{} \le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\).
When equality holds (\(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\)). Both sides of the inequality are nonnegative, so equality holds exactly when \((\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})^2 = \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2\), that is (dividing by \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 &gt; 0\) and rearranging), when \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 - (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})^2 / \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 = 0\). By Equation 1, that quantity is \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} - t\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2\), so equality holds exactly when \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} - t\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) (Theorem 1, part 1), that is, when \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = t\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\).

If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = t\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\), then \(1\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + (-t)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), so the two vectors are linearly dependent.

Conversely, suppose \(a\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + b\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) with \(a, b\) not both \(0\). Then \(a \ne 0\): otherwise \(b \ne 0\) and \(b\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), so
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} &= \tfrac{1}{b}\,(b\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) \\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}},
\end{aligned}
\]
contrary to assumption. Subtracting \(b\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\) from both sides gives \(a\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = -b\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\), and multiplying by \(\tfrac{1}{a}\) gives \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = s\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\) with \(s = -b/a\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left|\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right|\mathclose{}
&= \mathopen{}\left|(s\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right|\mathclose{}
&& \text{(substitute } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = s\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left|s\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})\right|\mathclose{}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-direct-sums.qmd#thm-dot-linear}{\text{Theorem~5 in Direct Sums and Orthogonal Complements}}\text{, special case } b = 0 \text{, first slot)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left|s\right|\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left|\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right|\mathclose{}
&& \text{(} \mathopen{}\left|ab\right|\mathclose{} = \mathopen{}\left|a\right|\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left|b\right|\mathclose{} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left|s\right|\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 \ge 0 \text{, }\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#eq-l2-norm}{\text{Equation~2 in Vectors}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left(\mathopen{}\left|s\right|\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\right)\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
&& \text{(regroup)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert s\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-norm-properties}{\text{Theorem~1}}\text{, part 2)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{},
&& \text{(} s\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so equality holds.












ExmExample 8 (A strict case and an equality case)






Example 8 (A strict case and an equality case)  


For \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = (1, 2)\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = (3, 4)\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} &= 3 + 8 \\
&= 11,
\end{aligned}
\]
while \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = \sqrt{5} \cdot 5 \approx 11.18\). The inequality is strict, and the vectors are linearly independent: \((3, 4)\) is not a multiple of \((1, 2)\), since \(3 \cdot 2 \ne 4\).


For \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = (1, 2)\) and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} &= (2, 4) \\
&= 2\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}:
\end{aligned}
\]
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} &= 2 + 8 \\
&= 10
\end{aligned}
\]
and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} &= \sqrt{5}\,\sqrt{20} \\
&= \sqrt{100} \\
&= 10,
\end{aligned}
\]
so equality holds.












LemTheorem 4 (Triangle inequality)






Theorem 4 (Triangle inequality) For any \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \in \mathbb{R}^p\),
\[
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} \le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} + \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}.
\]










ProofProof






Proof. \[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 + 2\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) + \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-norm-sum-square}{\text{Theorem~2}}\text{)}
\\
&\le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 + 2\,\mathopen{}\left|\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right|\mathclose{} + \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(a number is at most its absolute value)}
\\
&\le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 + 2\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} + \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-cauchy-schwarz}{\text{Theorem~3}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} + \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\right)\mathclose{}^2.
&& \text{(expand the square)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Both \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\) and \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} + \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\) are nonnegative (Theorem 1), and for nonnegative numbers \(a^2 \le b^2\) implies \(a \le b\).










ExmExample 9 (A strict case and an equality case)






Example 9 (A strict case and an equality case)  


For \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = (3, 0)\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = (0, 4)\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} &= \mathopen{}\left\lVert(3, 4)\right\rVert\mathclose{} \\
&= 5,
\end{aligned}
\]
while
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} + \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} &= 3 + 4 \\
&= 7.
\end{aligned}
\]


For \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = (1, 0)\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = (2, 0)\), which point the same way:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert(3, 0)\right\rVert\mathclose{} &= 3 \\
&= 1 + 2,
\end{aligned}
\]
so equality holds.












DefDefinition 4 (Norm (normed vector space, normed space))






Definition 4 (Norm (normed vector space, normed space)) A norm [7] on a real vector space \(V\) is a function \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\cdot\right\rVert\mathclose{} : V \to [0, \infty)\) that assigns to each vector \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\in V\) a nonnegative real number \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\), and satisfies the following three axioms for all \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\in V\) and all scalars \(c \in \mathbb{R}\):


Positivity and definiteness: \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} \ge 0\), and \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = 0\) if and only if \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\);

Absolute homogeneity: \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert c\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = \mathopen{}\left|c\right|\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\);

Subadditivity (triangle inequality): \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}+ \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} \le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} + \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\) [8].

A vector space equipped with a norm is called a normed vector space (or normed space).










ExmExample 10 (Euclidean space as a normed vector space)






Example 10 (Euclidean space as a normed vector space) The Euclidean space \(\mathbb{R}^p\) equipped with the Euclidean norm (Definition 20 in Vectors) is a normed vector space:

Positivity and absolute homogeneity hold by Theorem 1.
The triangle inequality holds by Theorem 4.

Every inner product space \((\mathbb{R}^p, {\mathopen{}\left\langle \cdot, \cdot\right\rangle\mathclose{}})\) (Definition 2) naturally defines an induced norm \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\sqrt{{\mathopen{}\left\langle \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rangle\mathclose{}}}\), which satisfies all three norm axioms.










DefDefinition 5 (Vector \(p\)-norm (\(\ell_p\) norm, \(\ell_1\) norm, taxicab norm, Manhattan norm, \(\ell_2\) norm, infinity norm))






Definition 5 (Vector \(p\)-norm (\(\ell_p\) norm, \(\ell_1\) norm, taxicab norm, Manhattan norm, \(\ell_2\) norm, infinity norm)) For a vector \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= (x_1, \ldots, x_p) \in \mathbb{R}^p\) and any real number \(k \ge 1\), the vector \(p\)-norm [9] (or \(\ell_p\) norm, traditionally named with parameter \(p\) but denoted here with exponent \(k\) to avoid clashing with the ambient vector dimension \(p\)) is:
\[
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathopen{}\left(\sum_{i=1}^p\mathopen{}\left|x_i\right|\mathclose{}^k\right)\mathclose{}^{1/k}.
\]
The cases \(k = 1\) and \(k = 2\) are named the \(\ell_1\) norm (also the taxicab norm or Manhattan norm) and the \(\ell_2\) norm.










ExmExample 11 (Calculating \(\ell_1\), \(\ell_2\), and \(\ell_\infty\) norms)






Example 11 (Calculating \(\ell_1\), \(\ell_2\), and \(\ell_\infty\) norms) For \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= (3, -4) \in \mathbb{R}^2\):


\(\ell_1\) norm:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_1 &= \mathopen{}\left|3\right|\mathclose{} + \mathopen{}\left|-4\right|\mathclose{} \\
&= 3 + 4 \\
&= 7.
\end{aligned}
\]


\(\ell_2\) norm:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_2 &= \sqrt{3^2 + (-4)^2} \\
&= \sqrt{9 + 16} \\
&= \sqrt{25} \\
&= 5.
\end{aligned}
\]


\(\ell_\infty\) norm:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_\infty &= \max(\mathopen{}\left|3\right|\mathclose{}, \mathopen{}\left|-4\right|\mathclose{}) \\
&= 4.
\end{aligned}
\]


Notice that \(4 \le 5 \le 7\). For any vector \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\in \mathbb{R}^p\), the norms satisfy the chain of inequalities:
\[
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_\infty \le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_2 \le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_1 \le \sqrt{p}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_2 \le p\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_\infty.
\]










RemarkRemark 3 (Unit balls and regularization in machine learning)






Remark 3 (Unit balls and regularization in machine learning). The unit ball in \(\mathbb{R}^2\) under the \(\ell_p\) norm is the set \(\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\in \mathbb{R}^2 : \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k \le 1\right\}\mathclose{}\):

For \(k = 1\), the unit ball is a diamond (cross-polytope) with sharp vertices along the coordinate axes \((\pm 1, 0)\) and \((0, \pm 1)\).
For \(k = 2\), the unit ball is the smooth round Euclidean unit disk.
For \(k = \infty\), the unit ball is an axis-aligned square with vertices \((\pm 1, \pm 1)\).

In regression and machine learning, adding an \(\ell_1\) penalty (Lasso regularization) to an objective function encourages sparse solutions: the level curves of the loss function tend to make contact with the sharp corners of the \(\ell_1\) unit ball on the coordinate axes, setting irrelevant feature weights exactly to zero. In contrast, the smooth spherical \(\ell_2\) penalty (Ridge regularization) shrinks all weights toward zero without setting them exactly to zero.










DefDefinition 6 (Infinity norm (\(\ell_\infty\) norm, maximum norm, Chebyshev norm))






Definition 6 (Infinity norm (\(\ell_\infty\) norm, maximum norm, Chebyshev norm)) For a vector \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= (x_1, \ldots, x_p) \in \mathbb{R}^p\), the infinity norm [10] (also the \(\ell_\infty\) norm, maximum norm, or Chebyshev norm) is defined separately from the \(p\)-norms (Definition 5) by
\[
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_\infty \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\max_{1 \le i \le p} \mathopen{}\left|x_i\right|\mathclose{}.
\]










LemTheorem 5 (The \(\ell_1\), \(\ell_2\) and infinity norms, and the norm axioms)






Theorem 5 (The \(\ell_1\), \(\ell_2\) and infinity norms, and the norm axioms) Let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= (x_1, \ldots, x_p) \in \mathbb{R}^p\).


\(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_1 = \sum_{i=1}^p\mathopen{}\left|x_i\right|\mathclose{}\), and \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_2 = \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^px_i^2}\), which is the Euclidean norm (Definition 20 in Vectors).

\(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_\infty = \lim_{k \to \infty} \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k\).
For every real number \(k \ge 1\), and for \(k = \infty\), \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\cdot\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k\) is a norm on \(\mathbb{R}^p\) (Definition 4).











ProofProof






Proof. Part 1. Setting \(k = 1\) in Definition 5 gives \(\mathopen{}\left(\sum_{i=1}^p\mathopen{}\left|x_i\right|\mathclose{}\right)\mathclose{}^{1/1} = \sum_{i=1}^p\mathopen{}\left|x_i\right|\mathclose{}\). Setting \(k = 2\) gives \(\mathopen{}\left(\sum_{i=1}^p\mathopen{}\left|x_i\right|\mathclose{}^2\right)\mathclose{}^{1/2}\), and \(\mathopen{}\left|x_i\right|\mathclose{}^2 = x_i^2\) for every real number \(x_i\).
Part 2. If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), every \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k\) is \(0\), and so is \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_\infty\). Otherwise, let \(M \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\max_i \mathopen{}\left|x_i\right|\mathclose{} &gt; 0\), and pick \(j\) with \(\mathopen{}\left|x_j\right|\mathclose{} = M\). Every term of \(\sum_{i=1}^p\mathopen{}\left|x_i\right|\mathclose{}^k\) is at most \(M^k\), and the \(j\)th term equals \(M^k\), so
\[
M^k \le \sum_{i=1}^p\mathopen{}\left|x_i\right|\mathclose{}^k \le p\,M^k.
\]
Taking the \(1/k\) power, which preserves order on \([0, \infty)\), gives
\[
M \le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k \le p^{1/k} M.
\]
As \(k \to \infty\), \(p^{1/k} = {\operatorname{exp}\mathopen{}\left\{\frac{\ln p}{k}\right\}\mathclose{}} \to 1\), so \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k\) is squeezed between \(M\) and a quantity that tends to \(M\). Thus \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k \to M = \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_\infty\).
Part 3, finite \(k\).

Positivity and definiteness. Each term \(\mathopen{}\left|x_i\right|\mathclose{}^k\) is at least \(0\), so \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k \ge 0\). The sum \(\sum_{i=1}^p\mathopen{}\left|x_i\right|\mathclose{}^k\) is \(0\) exactly when every term is \(0\), that is, when \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\).

Absolute homogeneity. For a number \(c\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert c\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k
&= \mathopen{}\left(\sum_{i=1}^p\mathopen{}\left|c\,x_i\right|\mathclose{}^k\right)\mathclose{}^{1/k}
&& \text{(definition)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left(\mathopen{}\left|c\right|\mathclose{}^k \sum_{i=1}^p\mathopen{}\left|x_i\right|\mathclose{}^k\right)\mathclose{}^{1/k}
&& \text{(} \mathopen{}\left|c\,x_i\right|\mathclose{} = \mathopen{}\left|c\right|\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left|x_i\right|\mathclose{} \text{, and factor out } \mathopen{}\left|c\right|\mathclose{}^k \text{)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left|c\right|\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k.
&& \text{(take the } 1/k \text{ power of each factor)}
\end{aligned}
\]


Triangle inequality. Let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\in \mathbb{R}^p\). If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) or \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), both sides of \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}+ \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k \le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k + \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k\) are equal. Otherwise, let \(a \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k &gt; 0\), \(b \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k &gt; 0\), \(\lambda \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\frac{a}{a + b}\), \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}/ a\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}/ b\). By absolute homogeneity, \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k = \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k = 1\), and
\[
\frac{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}+ \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}}{a + b} = \lambda\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + (1 - \lambda)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}.
\]
The function \(s \mapsto s^k\) is non-decreasing and convex on \([0, \infty)\) (its second derivative \(k (k - 1) s^{k - 2}\) is nonnegative). So for each coordinate \(i\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left|\lambda u_i + (1 - \lambda) v_i\right|\mathclose{}^k
&\le \mathopen{}\left(\lambda \mathopen{}\left|u_i\right|\mathclose{} + (1 - \lambda) \mathopen{}\left|v_i\right|\mathclose{}\right)\mathclose{}^k
&& \text{(triangle inequality for numbers; } s^k \text{ is non-decreasing)}
\\
&\le \lambda \mathopen{}\left|u_i\right|\mathclose{}^k + (1 - \lambda) \mathopen{}\left|v_i\right|\mathclose{}^k.
&& \text{(} s^k \text{ is convex)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Summing over \(i\), and using \(\sum_{i=1}^p\mathopen{}\left|u_i\right|\mathclose{}^k = \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k^k = 1\) and likewise for \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\),
\[
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\lambda\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + (1 - \lambda)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k^k \le \lambda + (1 - \lambda) = 1.
\]
So \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}+ \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k / (a + b) \le 1\) by absolute homogeneity, that is, \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}+ \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k \le a + b\).


Part 3, \(k = \infty\). Every \(\mathopen{}\left|x_i\right|\mathclose{}\) is at least \(0\), and the largest of them is \(0\) exactly when \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\). For a number \(c\), \(\max_i \mathopen{}\left|c\,x_i\right|\mathclose{} = \mathopen{}\left|c\right|\mathclose{} \max_i \mathopen{}\left|x_i\right|\mathclose{}\). For each \(i\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left|x_i + y_i\right|\mathclose{}
&\le \mathopen{}\left|x_i\right|\mathclose{} + \mathopen{}\left|y_i\right|\mathclose{}
&& \text{(triangle inequality for numbers)}
\\
&\le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_\infty + \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_\infty,
&& \text{(each is at most the maximum)}
\end{aligned}
\]
and taking the maximum over \(i\) on the left gives \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}+ \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_\infty \le \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_\infty + \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_\infty\).










ExmExample 12 (The \(\ell_k\) norm approaches the maximum norm as \(k\) grows)






Example 12 (The \(\ell_k\) norm approaches the maximum norm as \(k\) grows) Take \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= (3, -4)\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}= (-1, 5)\) in \(\mathbb{R}^2\). The code computes \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_k\) for several values of \(k\), including \(k = \infty\) as the maximum, and checks the triangle inequality for \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\) at each of them.

lk_norm &lt;- function(x, k) {
  if (is.infinite(k)) max(abs(x)) else sum(abs(x)^k)^(1 / k)
}

x &lt;- c(3, -4)
y &lt;- c(-1, 5)
ks &lt;- c(1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, Inf)
norms &lt;- sapply(ks, function(k) lk_norm(x, k))
triangle &lt;- sapply(
  ks,
  function(k) lk_norm(x + y, k) &lt;= lk_norm(x, k) + lk_norm(y, k)
)
data.frame(k = ks, norm = norms, triangle_holds = triangle)






By part 1 of Theorem 5, the \(\ell_1\) norm 7 equals \(\mathopen{}\left|3\right|\mathclose{} + \mathopen{}\left|-4\right|\mathclose{} = 7\), and the \(\ell_2\) norm 5 equals \(\sqrt{3^2 + (-4)^2} = 5\). By part 2, the norms approach the maximum norm 4: at \(k = 64\) the \(\ell_k\) norm is within 0.00000000063 of it. By part 3, the triangle inequality holds at all 8 of the 8 values of \(k\).










DefDefinition 7 (Cosine similarity)






Definition 7 (Cosine similarity) The cosine similarity [11] between two nonzero vectors \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\in \mathbb{R}^p\) is their dot product divided by the product of their lengths:
\[
\operatorname{cossim}(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\frac{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}}{\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}}.
\]










ExmExample 13 (Cosine similarity of parallel, orthogonal, and arbitrary vectors)






Example 13 (Cosine similarity of parallel, orthogonal, and arbitrary vectors) For vectors in \(\mathbb{R}^2\):


Parallel vectors \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= (1, 2)\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}= (2, 4)\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{cossim}(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) &= \frac{2 + 8}{\sqrt{5}\,\sqrt{20}} \\
&= \frac{10}{10} \\
&= 1.
\end{aligned}
\]


Orthogonal vectors \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= (1, 2)\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}= (-2, 1)\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{cossim}(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) &= \frac{-2 + 2}{\sqrt{5}\,\sqrt{5}} \\
&= \frac{0}{5} \\
&= 0.
\end{aligned}
\]


Arbitrary vectors \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= (1, 0)\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}= (1, 1)\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{cossim}(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) &= \frac{1 \cdot 1 + 0 \cdot 1}{1 \cdot\sqrt{2}} \\
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}.
\end{aligned}
\]


When both vectors have unit Euclidean norm (\(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = 1\) and \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = 1\)), cosine similarity equals their inner product: \(\operatorname{cossim}(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) = {\mathopen{}\left\langle \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rangle\mathclose{}}\).










DefDefinition 8 (Angle between two vectors)






Definition 8 (Angle between two vectors) The angle between two nonzero vectors, \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \in \mathbb{R}^p\), is
\[
\theta\stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\arccos\mathopen{}\left(\operatorname{cossim}(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})\right)\mathclose{}.
\]
In words, the angle is the arccosine of the cosine similarity of the two vectors.










RemarkRemark 4 (Why the angle is well defined)






Remark 4 (Why the angle is well defined). The cosine similarity is defined, because both vectors are nonzero (Theorem 1). By Theorem 3, it lies in the closed interval \([-1, 1]\), which is where the arccosine, \(\arccos\), is defined. So the angle \(\theta\) lies in \([0, \pi]\), with cosine \(\cos\theta= \operatorname{cossim}(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}})\).










CorCorollary 1 (A right angle means orthogonal)






Corollary 1 (A right angle means orthogonal) For nonzero vectors \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \in \mathbb{R}^p\) with angle \(\theta\) (Definition 8),
\[
\theta= \frac{\pi}{2}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = 0,
\]
so for nonzero vectors, orthogonal (Definition 19 in Vectors) means at a right angle.










ProofProof






Proof. Since \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} &gt; 0\) (Theorem 1), \(\operatorname{cossim}(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) = 0\) exactly when \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = 0\) (Definition 7), so \(\cos\theta= 0\) exactly when \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = 0\) (Remark 4). On \([0, \pi]\), \(\cos\) vanishes only at \(\pi/2\).










ExmExample 14 (Some angles in the plane)






Example 14 (Some angles in the plane)  

\((1, 0)\) and \((1, 1)\): \(\cos\theta= \frac{1}{1 \cdot\sqrt{2}}\), so \(\theta= \pi/4\).

\((1, 2)\) and \((-2, 1)\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\cos\theta&= \frac{-2 + 2}{\sqrt{5}\,\sqrt{5}} \\
&= 0,
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\theta= \pi/2\).


\((1, 0)\) and \((-3, 0)\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\cos\theta&= \frac{-3}{1 \cdot 3} \\
&= -1,
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\theta= \pi\).

\((1, 0)\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\): there is no angle, because the ratio would divide by \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = 0\).











LemTheorem 6 (The sign of the inner product)






Theorem 6 (The sign of the inner product) For nonzero \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\in \mathbb{R}^p\) with angle \(\theta\in [0, \pi]\) between them,
\[\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_2 \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_2 \cos\theta,\]
so:


\(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} &gt; 0\) when the vectors point broadly in the same direction (\(\theta&lt; \pi/2\)).

\(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = 0\) exactly when the vectors are orthogonal (\(\theta= \pi/2\)).

\(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} &lt; 0\) when the vectors point broadly in opposing directions (\(\theta&gt; \pi/2\)).











ProofProof






Proof. By the definition of the angle, \(\cos\theta\) is the cosine similarity \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} / (\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_2 \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}_2)\), which gives the displayed equation. Both vectors are nonzero, so their norms are positive, and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\) has the sign of \(\cos\theta\). On \([0, \pi]\), the cosine is positive for \(\theta&lt; \pi/2\), zero at \(\pi/2\) and negative for \(\theta&gt; \pi/2\).










ExmExample 15 (The sign of the inner product in the plane)






Example 15 (The sign of the inner product in the plane)  


\((1, 0)\) and \((1, 1)\) have inner product \(1 &gt; 0\) and angle \(\pi/4 &lt; \pi/2\).

\((1, 2)\) and \((-2, 1)\) have inner product \(-2 + 2 = 0\) and angle \(\pi/2\).

\((1, 0)\) and \((-3, 0)\) have inner product \(-3 &lt; 0\) and angle \(\pi &gt; \pi/2\).

These are the pairs of Example 14.










RemarkRemark 5 (How the two angles relate)






Remark 5 (How the two angles relate). For nonzero vectors in the plane, \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\), the angle between the vectors is the angle on the unit circle between the points \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} / \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\), \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} / \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\). The cosine similarity of the vectors is the dot product of these points. Rotating the plane puts the first point at \((1, 0)\), with no change to the distance between the points along the circle or to their dot product. For the angle on the unit circle, \(\theta\), the second point is then \((\cos\theta, \pm\sin\theta)\), so the dot product of the points is \(\cos\theta\). Applying \(\arccos\) to it returns \(\theta\), which lies in \([0, \pi]\).










DefDefinition 9 (Cosine similarity in an inner product space)






Definition 9 (Cosine similarity in an inner product space) In a general real inner product space (Definition 2) with inner product \({\mathopen{}\left\langle \cdot, \cdot\right\rangle\mathclose{}}\) and induced norm \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\cdot\right\rVert\mathclose{}\), the cosine similarity is
\[
\operatorname{cossim}(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\frac{{\mathopen{}\left\langle \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rangle\mathclose{}}}{\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}}.
\]










CorCorollary 2 (Cosine similarity of unit vectors is their inner product)






Corollary 2 (Cosine similarity of unit vectors is their inner product) In a real inner product space (Definition 2), for all vectors \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\) with \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = 1\),
\[
\operatorname{cossim}(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) = {\mathopen{}\left\langle \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rangle\mathclose{}}.
\]










ProofProof






Proof. Both norms in the denominator of Definition 9 equal \(1\), so
\[
\operatorname{cossim}(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) = \frac{{\mathopen{}\left\langle \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rangle\mathclose{}}}{1 \cdot 1} = {\mathopen{}\left\langle \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rangle\mathclose{}}.
\]










LemTheorem 7 (Pearson correlation is the cosine similarity of the centered vectors)






Theorem 7 (Pearson correlation is the cosine similarity of the centered vectors) Let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\in \mathbb{R}^n\) be two vectors that are not constant, with means \(\bar{x} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^nx_i\) and \(\bar{y} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^ny_i\). Write \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\mathrm{c}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}- \bar{x}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{1}}\vphantom{1}}\) and \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}}^{\mathrm{c}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}- \bar{y}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{1}}\vphantom{1}}\) for the centered vectors, whose means are zero. Then the Pearson correlation coefficient \(r\) of the \(n\) pairs \((x_i, y_i)\) is the cosine similarity (Definition 7) of the centered vectors:
\[
r = \operatorname{cossim}({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\mathrm{c}}, {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}}^{\mathrm{c}}).
\]










ProofProof






Proof. Entry \(i\) of \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\mathrm{c}}\) is \(x_i - \bar{x}\), and entry \(i\) of \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}}^{\mathrm{c}}\) is \(y_i - \bar{y}\). Neither centered vector is zero, because neither \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) nor \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\) is constant, so the cosine similarity is defined. The three pieces of the cosine similarity are
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\mathrm{c}} \cdot {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}}^{\mathrm{c}} &= \sum_{i=1}^n(x_i - \bar{x})(y_i - \bar{y}), \\
\mathopen{}\left\lVert{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\mathrm{c}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} &= \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i - \bar{x})^2}, \\
\mathopen{}\left\lVert{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}}^{\mathrm{c}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} &= \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^n(y_i - \bar{y})^2},
\end{aligned}
\]
by the definitions of the dot product and the Euclidean norm. Dividing the first by the product of the other two gives the formula for \(r\).










ExmExample 16 (Centering changes the cosine similarity)






Example 16 (Centering changes the cosine similarity) Take \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= (1, 2, 3)\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}= (1, 3, 2)\). Their means are \(\bar{x} = \bar{y} = 2\), so \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\mathrm{c}} = (-1, 0, 1)\) and \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}}^{\mathrm{c}} = (-1, 1, 0)\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\mathrm{c}} \cdot {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}}^{\mathrm{c}} &= 1 + 0 + 0 = 1, \\
\mathopen{}\left\lVert{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\mathrm{c}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} &= \mathopen{}\left\lVert{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}}^{\mathrm{c}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = \sqrt{2},
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(r = \operatorname{cossim}({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\mathrm{c}}, {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}}^{\mathrm{c}}) = 1 / 2\).
Without centering, \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}= 1 + 6 + 6 = 13\) and \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} = \sqrt{14}\), so \(\operatorname{cossim}(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}) = 13 / 14\). The uncentered value is close to \(1\) mostly because both vectors have positive entries and the same mean, not because they move together.










CorCorollary 3 (Pearson correlation is the cosine of the angle between the centered vectors)






Corollary 3 (Pearson correlation is the cosine of the angle between the centered vectors) For the two data vectors \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}\in \mathbb{R}^n\) of Theorem 7, the centered vectors \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\mathrm{c}}\), \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}}^{\mathrm{c}}\) are nonzero, so they have an angle \(\theta\). The Pearson correlation coefficient \(r\) satisfies
\[
r = \cos\theta.
\]










ProofProof






Proof. By Theorem 7, \(r = \operatorname{cossim}({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\mathrm{c}}, {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}}^{\mathrm{c}})\). By Definition 8, \(\theta= \arccos\mathopen{}\left(\operatorname{cossim}({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\mathrm{c}}, {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}}^{\mathrm{c}})\right)\mathclose{}\), so \(\cos\theta= \operatorname{cossim}({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\mathrm{c}}, {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}}^{\mathrm{c}})\) (Remark 4).










ExmExample 17 (A correlation of one half is an angle of sixty degrees)






Example 17 (A correlation of one half is an angle of sixty degrees) In Example 16, the correlation is \(r = 1/2\), for the data vectors \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= (1, 2, 3)\), \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}= (1, 3, 2)\). By Corollary 3, the angle \(\theta\) between the centered vectors \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\mathrm{c}} = (-1, 0, 1)\), \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}}^{\mathrm{c}} = (-1, 1, 0)\) satisfies
\[
\cos\theta= \frac{1}{2},
\]
so the angle is \(\theta= \pi/3\).

3 Orthonormal bases and Gram-Schmidt






NoteSource





Like Section 2, this section is adapted from Zhou [4], used under the MIT License (see the license text in Section 2 in Subspaces and Rank). The source leaves the proof that orthonormal vectors are linearly independent to class; it is written out here.









LemTheorem 8 (The dot product of a vector with a linear combination)






Theorem 8 (The dot product of a vector with a linear combination) For vectors \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_k \in \mathbb{R}^p\) and numbers \(c_1, \ldots, c_k\), with \(k \ge 1\),
\[
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathopen{}\left(\sum_{j=1}^{k} c_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_j\right)\mathclose{}
= \sum_{j=1}^{k} c_j\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_j).
\]










ProofProof






Proof. Write \(u_{ji}\) for entry \(i\) of \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_j\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathopen{}\left(\sum_{j=1}^{k} c_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_j\right)\mathclose{}
&= \sum_{i=1}^px_i \sum_{j=1}^{k} c_j u_{ji}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#def-dot-product}{\text{Definition~8 in Vectors}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#def-linear-combination}{\text{Definition~9 in Vectors}}\text{)}
\\
&= \sum_{i=1}^p\sum_{j=1}^{k} x_i c_j u_{ji}
&& \text{(distribute each } x_i \text{ over the inner sum)}
\\
&= \sum_{j=1}^{k} \sum_{i=1}^px_i c_j u_{ji}
&& \text{(swap the order of the finite sums)}
\\
&= \sum_{j=1}^{k} \sum_{i=1}^pc_j x_i u_{ji}
&& \text{(commute the factors in each product)}
\\
&= \sum_{j=1}^{k} c_j \sum_{i=1}^px_i u_{ji}
&& \text{(factor } c_j \text{ out of the inner sum)}
\\
&= \sum_{j=1}^{k} c_j\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_j).
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#def-dot-product}{\text{Definition~8 in Vectors}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 18 (Dotting with a combination of three vectors)






Example 18 (Dotting with a combination of three vectors) Let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = (1, 2, 3)\) and take the combination \(2\,(1, 0, 0) - (0, 1, 0) + 4\,(0, 0, 1) = (2, -1, 4)\). Directly,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \cdot (2, -1, 4) &= 2 - 2 + 12 \\
&= 12.
\end{aligned}
\]
By Theorem 8, \(2 \cdot 1 - 1 \cdot 2 + 4 \cdot 3 = 12\), the same number.










LemTheorem 9 (Orthonormal vectors are linearly independent)






Theorem 9 (Orthonormal vectors are linearly independent) If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_k\) are orthonormal (Definition 23 in Vectors), then they are linearly independent (Definition 1 in Subspaces and Rank).










ProofProof






Proof. Suppose \(\sum_{j=1}^{k} c_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\). Take any \(i \in \mathopen{}\left\{1, \ldots, k\right\}\mathclose{}\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
0
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}
&& \text{(every term of the dot product is } 0 \text{)}
\\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i \cdot \mathopen{}\left(\sum_{j=1}^{k} c_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(substitute the supposed equation)}
\\
&= \sum_{j=1}^{k} c_j\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j)
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-dot-linear-sum}{\text{Theorem~8}}\text{)}
\\
&= c_i.
&& \text{(} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j \text{ is } 1 \text{ if } j = i \text{ and } 0 \text{ otherwise)}
\end{aligned}
\]
So every \(c_i = 0\).










ExmExample 19 (Three orthonormal vectors in \(\mathbb{R}^3\))






Example 19 (Three orthonormal vectors in \(\mathbb{R}^3\)) Let
\[
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 = (0, 0, 1), \quad
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,(1, 1, 0), \quad
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_3 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,(1, -1, 0).
\]
Each has norm \(1\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 &= \tfrac{1}{2}\,(1 + 1 + 0) \\
&= 1,
\end{aligned}
\]
and likewise for the others. They are mutually orthogonal: \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2 = 0\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_3 = 0\), because \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1\) has its only nonzero entry where the others have \(0\), and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2 \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_3 &= \tfrac{1}{2}\,(1 - 1 + 0) \\
&= 0.
\end{aligned}
\]
So by Theorem 9 they are linearly independent, with no need to solve \(c_1 \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 + c_2 \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2 + c_3 \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_3 = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\).
The converse fails: \((1, 0)\) and \((1, 1)\) are linearly independent, since \(c_1 (1, 0) + c_2 (1, 1) = (c_1 + c_2, c_2)\) is \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) only if \(c_2 = 0\) and then \(c_1 = 0\), but they are not orthonormal, since \((1, 0) \cdot (1, 1) = 1 \ne 0\).










DefDefinition 10 (Orthonormal basis)






Definition 10 (Orthonormal basis) An orthonormal basis [12] of a subspace \(\mathcal{S}\) of \(\mathbb{R}^p\) is a basis of \(\mathcal{S}\) (Definition 6 in Subspaces and Rank) whose vectors are orthonormal (Definition 23 in Vectors).










ExmExample 20 (Orthonormal bases of \(\mathbb{R}^3\))






Example 20 (Orthonormal bases of \(\mathbb{R}^3\))  

The indicator vectors \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{e}}\vphantom{e}}_1, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{e}}\vphantom{e}}_2, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{e}}\vphantom{e}}_3\) are orthonormal

and a basis of \(\mathbb{R}^3\) (Example 16 in Subspaces and Rank).


The vectors \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_3\) of Example 19 are orthonormal and linearly independent, so they are a basis of their span (Definition 6 in Subspaces and Rank), which therefore has dimension \(3\) (Definition 9 in Subspaces and Rank) and is all of \(\mathbb{R}^3\) (Theorem 11 in Subspaces and Rank, Example 16 in Subspaces and Rank). So they are an orthonormal basis of \(\mathbb{R}^3\) too.











ExmExample 21 (Bases that are not orthonormal, and orthonormal lists that are not bases)






Example 21 (Bases that are not orthonormal, and orthonormal lists that are not bases)  


\((1, 0), (1, 1)\) is a basis of \(\mathbb{R}^2\) (Example 11 in Subspaces and Rank). It is not an orthonormal basis, since \((1, 0) \cdot (1, 1) = 1\).

\((1, 0, 0), (0, 1, 0)\) is orthonormal but not a basis of \(\mathbb{R}^3\): its span is the plane \(z = 0\) (Example 6 in Subspaces and Rank), which does not contain \((0, 0, 1)\).











LemTheorem 10 (Coordinates in an orthonormal basis are dot products)






Theorem 10 (Coordinates in an orthonormal basis are dot products) If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_k\) is an orthonormal basis of a subspace \(\mathcal{S}\) (Definition 10), then every \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \in \mathcal{S}\) satisfies
\[
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \sum_{i=1}^{k} (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i.
\]










ProofProof






Proof. The basis spans \(\mathcal{S}\), so \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \sum_{j=1}^{k} c_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j\) for some numbers \(c_j\). For each \(i\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i \cdot \mathopen{}\left(\sum_{j=1}^{k} c_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(substitute)}
\\
&= \sum_{j=1}^{k} c_j\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j)
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-dot-linear-sum}{\text{Theorem~8}}\text{)}
\\
&= c_i,
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#def-orthonormal-vectors}{\text{Definition~23 in Vectors}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so each coefficient \(c_i\) is \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\).










ExmExample 22 (Expanding a vector in an orthonormal basis)






Example 22 (Expanding a vector in an orthonormal basis) With the orthonormal basis \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_3\) of \(\mathbb{R}^3\) from Example 20 and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = (1, 2, 3)\): \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = 3\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2 \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} &= \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,(1 + 2) \\
&= \tfrac{3}{\sqrt{2}}
\end{aligned}
\]
and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_3 \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} &= \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,(1 - 2) \\
&= -\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{i=1}^{3} (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i
&= 3\,(0, 0, 1) + \tfrac{3}{\sqrt{2}} \cdot\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,(1, 1, 0)
- \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \cdot\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,(1, -1, 0)
&& \text{(substitute)}
\\
&= (0, 0, 3) + \mathopen{}\left(\tfrac{3}{2}, \tfrac{3}{2}, 0\right)\mathclose{} + \mathopen{}\left(-\tfrac{1}{2}, \tfrac{1}{2}, 0\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(multiply out each term)}
\\
&= (1, 2, 3),
&& \text{(add entrywise)}
\end{aligned}
\]
which is \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\), as Theorem 10 says.










DefDefinition 11 (Gram-Schmidt process)






Definition 11 (Gram-Schmidt process) The Gram-Schmidt process [13] takes vectors \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_k \in \mathbb{R}^p\) and, for \(i = 1, 2, \ldots, k\) in turn:


Orthogonalize: \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i - \sum_{j=1}^{i-1} (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j\) (for \(i = 1\) the sum is empty, so \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_1 = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1\));

Test: if \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), stop;

Normalize: \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i / \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i\right\rVert\mathclose{}\).

The output is the list \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2, \ldots\) produced before the process stops, or all \(k\) of them if it never stops.










ExmExample 23 (Two steps of Gram-Schmidt in \(\mathbb{R}^3\))






Example 23 (Two steps of Gram-Schmidt in \(\mathbb{R}^3\)) Let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1 = (1, 1, 0)\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_2 = (1, 0, 1)\).

Step 1. \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_1 = (1, 1, 0)\), with norm \(\sqrt{2}\), so \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,(1, 1, 0)\).

Step 2.
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_2 &= \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,(1 + 0 + 0) \\
&= \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}},
\end{aligned}
\]
so
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_2
&= (1, 0, 1) - \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \cdot\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,(1, 1, 0)
&& \text{(orthogonalize)}
\\
&= (1, 0, 1) - \mathopen{}\left(\tfrac{1}{2}, \tfrac{1}{2}, 0\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(multiply out)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left(\tfrac{1}{2}, -\tfrac{1}{2}, 1\right)\mathclose{},
&& \text{(subtract entrywise)}
\end{aligned}
\]
with norm \(\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{4} + \tfrac{1}{4} + 1} = \sqrt{\tfrac{3}{2}}\), so
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2 &= \sqrt{\tfrac{2}{3}}\,\mathopen{}\left(\tfrac{1}{2}, -\tfrac{1}{2}, 1\right)\mathclose{} \\
&= \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\,(1, -1, 2).
\end{aligned}
\]


As a check,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2 &= \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{12}}\,(1 - 1 + 0) \\
&= 0.
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 24 (Gram-Schmidt stops on dependent vectors)






Example 24 (Gram-Schmidt stops on dependent vectors) Let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1 = (1, 2)\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_2 = (2, 4)\). Step 1 gives \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\,(1, 2)\). In step 2,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_2 &= \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\,(2 + 8) \\
&= \tfrac{10}{\sqrt{5}},
\end{aligned}
\]
and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_2
&= (2, 4) - \tfrac{10}{\sqrt{5}} \cdot\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\,(1, 2) \\
&= (2, 4) - 2\,(1, 2) \\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}},
\end{aligned}
\]
so the process stops: \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_2\) is a multiple of \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1\).










LemTheorem 11 (What Gram-Schmidt produces)






Theorem 11 (What Gram-Schmidt produces) Run the Gram-Schmidt process (Definition 11) on \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_k \in \mathbb{R}^p\).

If it has produced \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i\) without stopping, then \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i\) are orthonormal and \(\operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i\right\}\mathclose{}
= \operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i\right\}\mathclose{}\).
If it reaches step \(i\) (that is, it did not stop at steps \(1, \ldots, i - 1\)), it stops there exactly when \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i \in \operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_{i-1}\right\}\mathclose{}\).
It completes all \(k\) steps exactly when \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_k\) are linearly independent; then \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_k\) is an orthonormal basis of \(\operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_k\right\}\mathclose{}\) (Definition 10).











ProofProof






Proof. Parts 1 and 2, by induction on \(i\). Before step \(1\), the list of \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}\)’s is empty, which is orthonormal, and both spans are \(\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\right\}\mathclose{}\) (Definition 5 in Subspaces and Rank). Suppose part 1 holds after step \(i - 1\), and write
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{S}_{i-1} &= \operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_{i-1}\right\}\mathclose{} \\
&= \operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_{i-1}\right\}\mathclose{}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The orthonormal vectors \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_{i-1}\) are linearly independent (Theorem 9) and span \(\mathcal{S}_{i-1}\), so they are an orthonormal basis of it.
\(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i\) is orthogonal to the earlier \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}\)’s. For each \(l &lt; i\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_l \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_l \cdot \mathopen{}\left(1\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i + \sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \mathopen{}\left(-\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i\right)\mathclose{}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(}\href{#def-gram-schmidt}{\text{Definition~11}}\text{, as a linear combination)}
\\
&= 1\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_l \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i) + \sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \mathopen{}\left(-\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i\right)\mathclose{}\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_l \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j)
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-dot-linear-sum}{\text{Theorem~8}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_l \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i - \sum_{j=1}^{i-1} (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i)\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_l \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j)
&& \text{(} 1\,z = z \text{, and pull the minus signs out of the sum)}
\\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_l \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_l \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i
&& \text{(only the } j = l \text{ term survives, }\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#def-orthonormal-vectors}{\text{Definition~23 in Vectors}}\text{)}
\\
&= 0.
&& \text{(arithmetic)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Part 2 at step \(i\). If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), then \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i = \sum_{j&lt;i} (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j \in \mathcal{S}_{i-1}\). Conversely, if \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i \in \mathcal{S}_{i-1}\), then \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i = \sum_{j&lt;i} (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j\) (Theorem 10), so \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\).
Part 1 at step \(i\), if the process does not stop. Then \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), so \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i\right\rVert\mathclose{} &gt; 0\), and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i / \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i\right\rVert\mathclose{}\) has norm \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i\right\rVert\mathclose{} / \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i\right\rVert\mathclose{} = 1\) (Theorem 1) and for \(l &lt; i\)
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_l \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i
&= \frac{1}{\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i\right\rVert\mathclose{}}\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_l \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i)
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-direct-sums.qmd#thm-dot-linear}{\text{Theorem~5 in Direct Sums and Orthogonal Complements}}\text{, special case } b = 0 \text{)}
\\
&= 0,
&& \text{(} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i \text{ is orthogonal to } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_l \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_l = 0\) too (Theorem 1 in Vectors). So \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i\) are orthonormal. For the spans:


\(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i = \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i\right\rVert\mathclose{}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i + \sum_{j&lt;i} (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j\) is in \(\operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i\right\}\mathclose{}\), and so is each of \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_{i-1}\), which lie in \(\mathcal{S}_{i-1} = \operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_{i-1}\right\}\mathclose{}
\subseteq \operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i\right\}\mathclose{}\).

\(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i = \mathopen{}\left(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i - \sum_{j&lt;i} (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j\right)\mathclose{} / \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i\right\rVert\mathclose{}\) is in \(\operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i\right\}\mathclose{}\), because each \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_j\) with \(j &lt; i\) lies in \(\mathcal{S}_{i-1}\); and so is each of \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_{i-1}\).

A span is a subspace (Theorem 2 in Subspaces and Rank), so it contains every linear combination of vectors in it; each span therefore contains the other, and they are equal.
Part 3. If the process completes, then \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1 \notin \operatorname{span}\) of the empty list, and for each \(i \ge 2\), \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i \notin \operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_{i-1}\right\}\mathclose{}\) (part 2). Applying Theorem 8 in Subspaces and Rank \(k\) times, starting from the empty list, shows that \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_k\) are linearly independent. Conversely, if they are linearly independent, no \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i\) is a linear combination \(\sum_{j&lt;i} c_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_j\) of the earlier ones, because then \(\sum_{j&lt;i} c_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_j - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_i = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) would be a combination with a nonzero coefficient; so by part 2 the process never stops. When it completes, \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_k\) are orthonormal, hence linearly independent (Theorem 9), and span \(\operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_k\right\}\mathclose{}\) (part 1), so they are an orthonormal basis of it.










ExmExample 25 (Finishing an orthonormal basis of \(\mathbb{R}^3\))






Example 25 (Finishing an orthonormal basis of \(\mathbb{R}^3\)) Continue Example 23 with \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_3 = (0, 1, 1)\). \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_3 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\) and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2 \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_3 &= \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\,(0 - 1 + 2) \\
&= \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}},
\end{aligned}
\]
so
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_3
&= (0, 1, 1) - \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \cdot\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,(1, 1, 0) - \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}} \cdot\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\,(1, -1, 2)
&& \text{(orthogonalize)}
\\
&= (0, 1, 1) - \tfrac{1}{2}\,(1, 1, 0) - \tfrac{1}{6}\,(1, -1, 2)
&& \text{(multiply the scalars)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left(0 - \tfrac{1}{2} - \tfrac{1}{6},\; 1 - \tfrac{1}{2} + \tfrac{1}{6},\; 1 - 0 - \tfrac{2}{6}\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(subtract entrywise)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left(-\tfrac{2}{3}, \tfrac{2}{3}, \tfrac{2}{3}\right)\mathclose{},
&& \text{(arithmetic)}
\end{aligned}
\]
with norm \(\sqrt{3 \cdot\tfrac{4}{9}} = \tfrac{2}{\sqrt{3}}\), so \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_3 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\,(-1, 1, 1)\). The process did not stop, so by Theorem 11 \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_1, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_2, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_3\) are linearly independent and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_3\) is an orthonormal basis of their span. That span therefore has dimension \(3\) (Definition 9 in Subspaces and Rank), so it is \(\mathbb{R}^3\) (Theorem 11 in Subspaces and Rank, Example 16 in Subspaces and Rank). As a check,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_3 &= \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\,(-1 + 1 + 0) \\
&= 0
\end{aligned}
\]
and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2 \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_3 &= \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{18}}\,(-1 - 1 + 2) \\
&= 0.
\end{aligned}
\]










CorCorollary 4 (Every subspace has an orthonormal basis)






Corollary 4 (Every subspace has an orthonormal basis) Let \(\mathcal{S}\) be a subspace of \(\mathbb{R}^p\). Every orthonormal list of vectors in \(\mathcal{S}\) extends to an orthonormal basis of \(\mathcal{S}\) (Definition 10). Starting from the empty list shows that \(\mathcal{S}\) has an orthonormal basis.










ProofProof






Proof. Let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_r \in \mathcal{S}\) be orthonormal. They are linearly independent (Theorem 9), so they extend to a basis \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_r, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_{r+1}, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}_d\) of \(\mathcal{S}\) (Theorem 9 in Subspaces and Rank). Run the Gram-Schmidt process on this basis. It is linearly independent, so the process completes, and the output is an orthonormal basis of its span, which is \(\mathcal{S}\) (Theorem 11, part 3).
The first \(r\) outputs are \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_r\) themselves, by induction on \(i \le r\). Suppose the outputs before step \(i\) are \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_{i-1}\) (for \(i = 1\) there are none, and the sum below is empty). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i &= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_i - \sum_{j&lt;i} (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_j \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_i)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_j \\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_i,
\end{aligned}
\]
because each \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_j \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_i = 0\); \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_i = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_i \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), because \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_i\right\rVert\mathclose{} = 1\), so the process does not stop; and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_i &= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_i / \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_i\right\rVert\mathclose{} \\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_i.
\end{aligned}
\]
So the orthonormal basis contains the starting list.










ExmExample 26 (Extending one unit vector to an orthonormal basis of \(\mathbb{R}^3\))






Example 26 (Extending one unit vector to an orthonormal basis of \(\mathbb{R}^3\)) Start from the unit vector \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_1 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,(1, 1, 0)\). Extend it to a basis of \(\mathbb{R}^3\) as in Example 18 in Subspaces and Rank, starting from the empty list: \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_1\) is not in the span of the empty list, \(\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\right\}\mathclose{}\), because \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_1\right\rVert\mathclose{} = 1\); \((1, 0, 0)\) is not in \(\operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_1\right\}\mathclose{}\), because every multiple of \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_1\) has equal first and second entries, and \((0, 0, 1)\) is not in \(\operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_1, (1, 0, 0)\right\}\mathclose{}\), because every combination of those two vectors has third entry \(0\). So \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_1, (1, 0, 0), (0, 0, 1)\) are linearly independent (Theorem 8 in Subspaces and Rank), and three linearly independent vectors in \(\mathbb{R}^3\) are a basis of it (they are a basis of their span, which has dimension \(3\) (Definition 9 in Subspaces and Rank) and so is \(\mathbb{R}^3\) (Theorem 11 in Subspaces and Rank, Example 16 in Subspaces and Rank)). Gram-Schmidt on \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_1, (1, 0, 0), (0, 0, 1)\):

\(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}_1\).

\(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1 \cdot (1, 0, 0) = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\), so
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_2 &= (1, 0, 0) - \tfrac{1}{2}\,(1, 1, 0) \\
&= \mathopen{}\left(\tfrac{1}{2}, -\tfrac{1}{2}, 0\right)\mathclose{},
\end{aligned}
\]
with norm \(\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\), and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2 = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,(1, -1, 0)\).

\((0, 0, 1)\) is orthogonal to \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_1\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_2\), so \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}'_3 = (0, 0, 1)\), which already has norm \(1\), and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{q}}\vphantom{q}}_3 = (0, 0, 1)\).

The result is the orthonormal basis of Example 20, in a different order.

4 Outer product






DefDefinition 12 (Outer product)






Definition 12 (Outer product) The outer product [14] of a vector \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\) of length \(m\) and a vector \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) of length \(n\) is the \(m \times n\) matrix product (Definition 7 in Matrices) \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}\) of the column vector \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\) with the row vector \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}\). Its entries are
\[
\mathopen{}\left(\underbrace{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}_{m \times 1}\,\underbrace{{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}}_{1 \times n}\right)\mathclose{}_{ij}
= u_i v_j
\qquad \text{(definition of matrix multiplication; the sum has one term)}
\]










ExmExample 27 (An outer product)






Example 27 (An outer product) For \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} = (1, 2, 3)\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = (4, 5)\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix} 4 & 5 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(definition of the outer product)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix}
1 \cdot 4 & 1 \cdot 5 \\
2 \cdot 4 & 2 \cdot 5 \\
3 \cdot 4 & 3 \cdot 5
\end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(entry } (i, j) \text{ is } u_i v_j \text{)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix}
4 & 5 \\
8 & 10 \\
12 & 15
\end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(multiply)}
\end{aligned}
\]










RemarkRemark 6 (Outer product and dot product)






Remark 6 (Outer product and dot product). The dot product \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) (Example 6 in Matrices) needs two vectors of the same length and gives a number. The outer product \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}\) takes vectors of any two lengths and gives a matrix [3, Ch. 1], p. 11.
For example, \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} = (1, 2, 3)\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = (4, 5)\) in Example 27 have different lengths, so they have no dot product, but their outer product is a \(3 \times 2\) matrix. For \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}} = (1, 2)\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} = (3, 4)\), which have the same length, both products exist:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}}^{\top}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}
&= 1 \cdot 3 + 2 \cdot 4 \\
&= 11 \\
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{a}}\vphantom{a}}\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}}^{\top}
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \cdot 3 & 1 \cdot 4 \\ 2 \cdot 3 & 2 \cdot 4 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 4 \\ 6 & 8 \end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
\]










LemTheorem 12 (An outer product has rank one)






Theorem 12 (An outer product has rank one) If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\) is a nonzero vector of length \(m\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is a nonzero vector of length \(n\), then \(\operatorname{rank}(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}) = 1\) (Definition 2 in Subspaces and Rank).










ProofProof






Proof. Column \(j\) of \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}\) has entries \(u_1 v_j, \ldots, u_m v_j\), so it is the vector \(v_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\).
The rank is at least one. Because \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \neq \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), some entry \(v_j\) is nonzero. Then column \(j\), \(v_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\), is a nonzero vector, and a single nonzero vector is linearly independent (Definition 1 in Subspaces and Rank): \(c\,(v_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}) = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) forces \(c = 0\).
The rank is at most one. Take any two columns \(j \neq k\). If
\[
\begin{aligned}
v_j &= v_k \\
&= 0,
\end{aligned}
\]
both columns are \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), and \(1 \cdot(v_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}) + 1 \cdot(v_k \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}) = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\). Otherwise, use the coefficients \(c_j = v_k\) and \(c_k = -v_j\), which are not both zero:
\[
\begin{aligned}
v_k\,(v_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}) - v_j\,(v_k \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}})
&= (v_k v_j - v_j v_k)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}
&& \text{(collect the scalar multiples of } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} \text{)}
\\
&= 0 \cdot\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}
&& \text{(multiplication of numbers is commutative)}
\\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}
&& \text{(a zero multiple of a vector is } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Either way, every pair of columns has a combination with coefficients that are not all zero and that equals \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\). Now take any set of two or more columns. It contains a pair \(j \neq k\). Use that pair’s coefficients for columns \(j\) and \(k\), and the coefficient \(0\) for every other column in the set. This combination equals \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), and its coefficients are not all zero, so the set is not linearly independent (Definition 1 in Subspaces and Rank). The largest linearly independent set of columns therefore has one column.










ExmExample 28 (The rank of an outer product)






Example 28 (The rank of an outer product) In Example 27, the second column \((5, 10, 15)\) of \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top}\) is \(\frac{5}{4}\) times the first column \((4, 8, 12)\), because the columns are \(v_1 \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} = 4\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\) and \(v_2 \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} = 5\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\). So the two columns are not linearly independent, and the \(3 \times 2\) matrix has rank \(1\).

NoteSource





This section is adapted from Zhou [1], used under the MIT License (see the license text in Section 2 in Subspaces and Rank). These notes take eigenvalues to be real numbers (Definition 16 in Special Matrices and Decompositions), so the source’s complex eigenvalues are left out, and eigenvalues are characterized through null spaces rather than through the characteristic polynomial. Only the source’s material on similarity, diagonalization and the basic eigenvalue properties is adapted; its characteristic polynomial and algebraic multiplicity, its trace and determinant identities, and the part of its section on symmetric matrices that builds bases of the column space and null space from eigenvectors are left out. The rest of that section is covered by Theorem 12 in Special Matrices and Decompositions and Theorem 13 in Special Matrices and Decompositions. Its result that eigenvalues of orthogonal matrices have modulus \(1\) appears here in the real form \(\pm 1\). Three other parts of the source are not part of this section:

its statement that the eigenvalues of a lower triangular matrix are also its diagonal entries (Theorem 4 states the upper triangular case)
its three further examples of matrices with geometric multiplicity smaller than algebraic multiplicity (Example 2 gives one nilpotent example)
its code cells The source’s two-vector argument that eigenvectors for distinct eigenvalues are independent is extended here to any number of eigenvectors.












LemTheorem 1 (A square matrix is invertible exactly when it has full column rank)






Theorem 1 (A square matrix is invertible exactly when it has full column rank) An \(n \times n\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) is invertible (Definition 6 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) exactly when \(\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{A}) = n\) (full column rank, Definition 3 in Subspaces and Rank), that is, exactly when \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A}) = \mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_n\right\}\mathclose{}\).










ProofProof






Proof. Invertible implies rank \(n\). If \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_n\), then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&= \mathbf{I}_n \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= (\mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{A})\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-inverse}{\text{Definition~5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}^{-1}\,(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}^{-1}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_n
&& \text{(} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_n \text{)}
\\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_n,
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matvec-mult}{\text{Definition~13 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A}) = \mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_n\right\}\mathclose{}\), the nullity is \(0\), and \(\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{A}) = n\) (Theorem 7 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity).
Rank \(n\) implies invertible. Every system \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\) has exactly one solution (Corollary 1 in Projections and Linear Systems, both parts, with \(m = n\)). Let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}_j\) solve \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}_j = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{e}}\vphantom{e}}_j\) (Definition 18 in Vectors), and let \(\mathbf{B}\) have columns \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}_n\). Column \(j\) of \(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B}\) is \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}_j = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{e}}\vphantom{e}}_j\) (1), so \(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B} = \mathbf{I}_n\). For the other order,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}\,(\mathbf{B} \mathbf{A} - \mathbf{I}_n)
&= \mathbf{A} \mathbf{B} \mathbf{A} - \mathbf{A} \mathbf{I}_n
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{I}_n \mathbf{A} - \mathbf{A} \mathbf{I}_n
&& \text{(} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{B} = \mathbf{I}_n \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A} - \mathbf{A}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{0}_{n \times n},
&& \text{(arithmetic)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so every column of \(\mathbf{B} \mathbf{A} - \mathbf{I}_n\) is in \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A}) = \mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_n\right\}\mathclose{}\) (1), and \(\mathbf{B} \mathbf{A} = \mathbf{I}_n\). So \(\mathbf{B}\) satisfies Definition 6 in Special Matrices and Decompositions. The two conditions in the statement are equivalent by Theorem 7 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity: the nullity is \(0\) exactly when \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A}) = \mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_n\right\}\mathclose{}\), because a subspace of dimension \(0\) has the empty list as a basis, whose span is \(\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\right\}\mathclose{}\) (Definition 9 in Subspaces and Rank, Definition 5 in Subspaces and Rank), and \(\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\right\}\mathclose{}\) has dimension \(0\) (Example 16 in Subspaces and Rank).










ExmExample 1 (Rank decides invertibility)






Example 1 (Rank decides invertibility)  

\(\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) of Example 5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions has rank \(2\): \(c_1 (2, 0) + c_2 (1, 1) = (2c_1 + c_2, c_2)\) is \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) only if \(c_2 = 0\) and then \(c_1 = 0\). So it is invertible, as that example found by exhibiting the inverse.
\(\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) has rank less than \(2\): \((1, -1)\) is a nonzero vector in its null space. So it is singular, as Example 5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions found.











DefDefinition 1 (Eigenspace)






Definition 1 (Eigenspace) Let \(\lambda\) be an eigenvalue of a \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) (Definition 16 in Special Matrices and Decompositions). The eigenspace [2] of \(\lambda\) is \(\mathcal{E}_\lambda\stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A} - \lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p)\). It is a subspace of \(\mathbb{R}^p\) (Theorem 2 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity).










DefDefinition 2 (Geometric multiplicity)






Definition 2 (Geometric multiplicity) The dimension (Definition 9 in Subspaces and Rank) of the eigenspace \(\mathcal{E}_\lambda\) (Definition 1) of an eigenvalue \(\lambda\) is the geometric multiplicity of \(\lambda\).










ExmExample 2 (Eigenspaces of two matrices)






Example 2 (Eigenspaces of two matrices)  

For \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) of Example 24 in Special Matrices and Decompositions, \(\mathbf{A} - 3\,\mathbf{I}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} -1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{bmatrix}\) sends \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) to \((v_2 - v_1)\,(1, -1)\), so \(\mathcal{E}_3 = \operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{(1, 1)\right\}\mathclose{}\), with geometric multiplicity \(1\).
For \(\mathbf{J} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\), \(\mathbf{J} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = (v_2, 0)\).
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{J}\,(1, 0) &= (0, 0) \\
&= 0\,(1, 0),
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(0\) is an eigenvalue. If \(\mathbf{J} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) with \(\lambda\ne 0\), the second entry gives \(\lambda v_2 = 0\), so \(v_2 = 0\), and then the first gives \(\lambda v_1 = 0\), so \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\). So \(0\) is the only eigenvalue, and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{E}_0 &= \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{J}) \\
&= \mathopen{}\left\{(t, 0) : t \in \mathbb{R}\right\}\mathclose{},
\end{aligned}
\]
with geometric multiplicity \(1\).











ExmExample 3 (A number that is not an eigenvalue has no eigenspace)






Example 3 (A number that is not an eigenvalue has no eigenspace) For \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) and \(\lambda= 2\), \(\mathbf{A} - 2\,\mathbf{I}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\) sends \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) to \((v_2, v_1)\), which is \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) only for \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), so \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A} - 2\,\mathbf{I}_2) = \mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\right\}\mathclose{}\). And \(2\) is not an eigenvalue (Definition 16 in Special Matrices and Decompositions): \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = 2\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) reads \((2v_1 + v_2, v_1 + 2v_2) = (2v_1, 2v_2)\), so \(v_2 = 0\) and \(v_1 = 0\). So Definition 1 does not apply to \(2\).










LemTheorem 2 (Eigenvalues are where \(\mathbf{A} - \lambda\mathbf{I}\) is singular)






Theorem 2 (Eigenvalues are where \(\mathbf{A} - \lambda\mathbf{I}\) is singular) Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be a \(p \times p\) matrix and \(\lambda\) a real number. Then \(\lambda\) is an eigenvalue of \(\mathbf{A}\) (Definition 16 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) exactly when \(\mathbf{A} - \lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p\) is singular (Definition 6 in Special Matrices and Decompositions). In particular, \(\mathbf{A}\) is singular exactly when \(0\) is one of its eigenvalues; and each eigenspace \(\mathcal{E}_\lambda\) (Definition 1) is a subspace of \(\mathbb{R}^p\) consisting of \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) and the eigenvectors for \(\lambda\).










ProofProof






Proof. For any \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \in \mathbb{R}^p\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
(\mathbf{A} - \lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&= \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} - (\lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{, the difference law)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} - \lambda\,(\mathbf{I}_p \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{, the scalar factor)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} - \lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}},
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) exactly when \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \in \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A} - \lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p)\); the nonzero such \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) are the eigenvectors for \(\lambda\). So \(\lambda\) is an eigenvalue exactly when that null space contains a nonzero vector, which by Theorem 1 is exactly when \(\mathbf{A} - \lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p\) is singular. With \(\lambda= 0\), \(\mathbf{A} - 0\,\mathbf{I}_p = \mathbf{A}\). An eigenspace is a null space, so it is a subspace (Theorem 2 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity).










ExmExample 4 (Eigenvalues and singular matrices)






Example 4 (Eigenvalues and singular matrices) For \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\), \(\mathbf{A} - 3\,\mathbf{I}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} -1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{bmatrix}\) is singular (its columns are negatives of each other, so its rank is \(1\)), matching the eigenvalue \(3\) of Example 24 in Special Matrices and Decompositions. \(\mathbf{A}\) itself has rank \(2\), since \(c_1 (2, 1) + c_2 (1, 2) = (2c_1 + c_2, c_1 + 2c_2)\) is \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) only if
\[
\begin{aligned}
c_1 &= c_2 \\
&= 0
\end{aligned}
\]
(subtract twice the second entry from the first: \(-3c_2 = 0\), and then the second entry gives \(c_1 = 0\)); so it is invertible (Theorem 1), and \(0\) is not an eigenvalue. The singular matrix \(\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) has eigenvalue \(0\), with eigenvector \((1, -1)\).










LemTheorem 3 (Eigenvalues of shifts and powers)






Theorem 3 (Eigenvalues of shifts and powers) Let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) be an eigenvector of a \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) for the eigenvalue \(\lambda\) (Definition 16 in Special Matrices and Decompositions). Then

\(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is an eigenvector of \(\mathbf{A} + s\,\mathbf{I}_p\) for \(\lambda+ s\), for every number \(s\);
\(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is an eigenvector of \(\mathbf{A}^k\) (Definition 2 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) for \(\lambda^k\), for every positive integer \(k\).











ProofProof






Proof. Part 1.
\[
\begin{aligned}
(\mathbf{A} + s\,\mathbf{I}_p)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&= \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} + (s\,\mathbf{I}_p)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-distrib}{\text{Theorem~6 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} + s\,(\mathbf{I}_p \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{)}
\\
&= \lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} + s\,(\mathbf{I}_p \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(eigenvector)}
\\
&= \lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} + s \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= (\lambda+ s)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}.
&& \text{(add entrywise)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Part 2, by induction on \(k\). For \(k = 1\) it is the assumption. If \(\mathbf{A}^{k-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \lambda^{k-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\), then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}^k \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&= \mathbf{A}\,(\mathbf{A}^{k-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-power}{\text{Definition~2 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}\,(\lambda^{k-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(induction hypothesis)}
\\
&= \lambda^{k-1}\,(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{)}
\\
&= \lambda^{k-1}\,(\lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(eigenvector)}
\\
&= \lambda^k \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}.
&& \text{(multiply the numbers)}
\end{aligned}
\]
In both parts \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), so \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is an eigenvector.










ExmExample 5 (Shifting and squaring)






Example 5 (Shifting and squaring) For \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) with eigenvector \((1, 1)\) for \(3\) (Example 24 in Special Matrices and Decompositions):

\(\mathbf{A} - 2\,\mathbf{I}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\) sends \((1, 1)\) to \((1, 1) = (3 - 2)\,(1, 1)\);
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}^2 &= \begin{bmatrix} 2 \cdot 2 + 1 \cdot 1 & 2 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 2 \\ 1 \cdot 2 + 2 \cdot 1 & 1 \cdot 1 + 2 \cdot 2 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 5 & 4 \\ 4 & 5 \end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
\]

sends \((1, 1)\) to \((9, 9) = 3^2\,(1, 1)\).












LemTheorem 4 (The eigenvalues of an upper triangular matrix are its diagonal entries)






Theorem 4 (The eigenvalues of an upper triangular matrix are its diagonal entries) The eigenvalues (Definition 16 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) of an upper triangular \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{U}\) (Definition 7 in Projections and Linear Systems) are exactly its diagonal entries \(u_{11}, \ldots, u_{pp}\).










ProofProof






Proof. \(\mathbf{U} - \lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p\) is upper triangular, with diagonal entries \(u_{ii} - \lambda\) (Definition 12 in Matrices, Definition 5 in Matrices).
If \(\lambda\) is not a diagonal entry, every \(u_{ii} - \lambda\ne 0\), so \((\mathbf{U} - \lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) has exactly one solution, \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) (Theorem 17 in Projections and Linear Systems), and \(\lambda\) is not an eigenvalue (Theorem 2).
If \(\lambda\) is a diagonal entry, let \(k\) be the smallest index with \(u_{kk} = \lambda\), and write \(\mathbf{M} = \mathbf{U} - \lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p\), so \(m_{kk} = 0\) and \(m_{ii} \ne 0\) for \(i &lt; k\). Look for \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) with \(v_k = 1\) and \(v_j = 0\) for \(j &gt; k\). Entry \(i\) of \(\mathbf{M} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is
\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{j=1}^pm_{ij}\,v_j
&= \sum_{j=i}^{p} m_{ij}\,v_j
&& \text{(} m_{ij} = 0 \text{ for } j &lt; i \text{)}
\\
&= \sum_{j=i}^{k} m_{ij}\,v_j,
&& \text{(} v_j = 0 \text{ for } j &gt; k \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
where the last sum is empty, and so \(0\), when \(i &gt; k\). So:

for \(i &gt; k\) entry \(i\) is \(0\);
for \(i = k\) it is \(m_{kk}\,v_k = 0\);
for \(i &lt; k\) it is \(0\) exactly when \(v_i = -\frac{1}{m_{ii}} \sum_{j=i+1}^{k} m_{ij}\,v_j\), and these equations fix \(v_{k-1}, \ldots, v_1\) in turn, as in Theorem 17 in Projections and Linear Systems.

So \(\mathbf{M} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) with \(v_k = 1\), hence \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), and \(\lambda\) is an eigenvalue.










ExmExample 6 (Eigenvalues read off the diagonal)






Example 6 (Eigenvalues read off the diagonal) \(\mathbf{U} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 & -1 \\ 0 & \frac{1}{2} & \frac{1}{2} \\ 0 & 0 & -1 \end{bmatrix}\) of Example 24 in Projections and Linear Systems has eigenvalues \(2\), \(\tfrac{1}{2}\) and \(-1\). For \(\lambda= \tfrac{1}{2}\) (so \(k = 2\)), take \(v_2 = 1\), \(v_3 = 0\); row 1 of \((\mathbf{U} - \tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{I}_3)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is \(\tfrac{3}{2}\,v_1 + 1 = 0\), so \(v_1 = -\tfrac{2}{3}\). Check:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{U}\,(-\tfrac{2}{3}, 1, 0) &= (-\tfrac{4}{3} + 1, \tfrac{1}{2}, 0) \\
&= \tfrac{1}{2}\,(-\tfrac{2}{3}, 1, 0).
\end{aligned}
\]
The matrix \(\mathbf{J}\) of Example 2 is upper triangular with both diagonal entries \(0\), and \(0\) is its only eigenvalue.










LemTheorem 5 (Eigenvalues of idempotent and orthogonal matrices)






Theorem 5 (Eigenvalues of idempotent and orthogonal matrices)  

Every eigenvalue of an idempotent matrix (Definition 7 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) is \(0\) or \(1\).
Every eigenvalue of an orthogonal matrix (Definition 10 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) is \(1\) or \(-1\).











ProofProof






Proof. Let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) with \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\).
Part 1. If \(\mathbf{A}^2 = \mathbf{A}\), then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&= \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(eigenvector)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}^2 \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(idempotent)}
\\
&= \lambda^2 \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}},
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-eigen-shift-power}{\text{Theorem~3}}\text{, part 2)}
\end{aligned}
\]
and subtracting \(\lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) from both sides gives \((\lambda^2 - \lambda)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\). Some entry of \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is nonzero, so
\[
\begin{aligned}
\lambda^2 - \lambda&= \lambda\,(\lambda- 1) \\
&= 0,
\end{aligned}
\]
and a product of two numbers is \(0\) only when one of them is: \(\lambda= 0\) or \(\lambda= 1\).
Part 2. If \(\mathbf{A}\) is orthogonal, then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#thm-orthogonal-norm}{\text{Theorem~6 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
&& \text{(eigenvector)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left|\lambda\right|\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\right\rVert\mathclose{},
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-inner-products.qmd#thm-norm-properties}{\text{Theorem~1 in Inner Products and Orthogonality}}\text{, part 2)}
\end{aligned}
\]
and \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} &gt; 0\) (Theorem 1 in Inner Products and Orthogonality, part 1), so dividing by \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\) gives \(\mathopen{}\left|\lambda\right|\mathclose{} = 1\); since \(\lambda\) is real (Definition 16 in Special Matrices and Decompositions), \(\lambda= 1\) or \(\lambda= -1\).










ExmExample 7 (Projections and reflections)






Example 7 (Projections and reflections)  

The idempotent matrix \(\begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & 0.5 \\ 0.5 & 0.5 \end{bmatrix}\) of Example 3 in Projections and Linear Systems sends \((1, 1)\) to \((1, 1)\) and \((1, -1)\) to \((0, 0)\): eigenvalues \(1\) and \(0\).
The matrix \(\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\), which swaps the two entries, is orthogonal: its columns \((0, 1)\) and \((1, 0)\) are orthonormal (Remark 6 in Special Matrices and Decompositions). It sends \((1, 1)\) to \((1, 1)\) and \((1, -1)\) to \((-1, 1)\): eigenvalues \(1\) and \(-1\).
The rotation \(\mathbf{Q}\) of Example 13 in Special Matrices and Decompositions has no real eigenvalue. By part 2 the only candidates are \(\pm 1\). \(\mathbf{Q} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) reads \(-0.4\,v_1 - 0.8\,v_2 = 0\) and \(0.8\,v_1 - 0.4\,v_2 = 0\), so \(v_1 = -2 v_2\) and
\[
\begin{aligned}
v_2 &= 2 v_1 \\
&= -4 v_2,
\end{aligned}
\]
forcing \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\); \(\mathbf{Q} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = -\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) reads \(1.6\,v_1 - 0.8\,v_2 = 0\) and \(0.8\,v_1 + 1.6\,v_2 = 0\), so \(v_2 = 2 v_1\) and \(0.8\,v_1 + 3.2\,v_1 = 0\), forcing \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) again.











DefDefinition 3 (Similar matrices)






Definition 3 (Similar matrices) Two \(p \times p\) matrices \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{B}\) are similar [3] if \(\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}\) for some invertible \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{P}\) (Definition 6 in Special Matrices and Decompositions).










RemarkRemark 1 (Similarity read both ways)






Remark 1 (Similarity read both ways). If \(\mathbf{P}\) is invertible, then \(\mathbf{P}^{-1}\) is invertible with inverse \(\mathbf{P}\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{P} &= \mathbf{P} \mathbf{P}^{-1} \\
&= \mathbf{I}_p
\end{aligned}
\]

is the condition of Definition 6 in Special Matrices and Decompositions for \(\mathbf{P}^{-1}\) with \(\mathbf{P}\) as the other factor, and that factor is unique (Remark 3 in Special Matrices and Decompositions). If \(\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}\), then

\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{P} \mathbf{B} \mathbf{P}^{-1}
&= \mathbf{P}\,(\mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P})\,\mathbf{P}^{-1}
&& \text{(substitute } \mathbf{B} \text{)}
\\
&= (\mathbf{P} \mathbf{P}^{-1})\,\mathbf{A}\,(\mathbf{P} \mathbf{P}^{-1})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{I}_p\,\mathbf{A}\,\mathbf{I}_p
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-inverse}{\text{Definition~5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A},
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
and the same steps with \(\mathbf{P}\) and \(\mathbf{P}^{-1}\) exchanged turn \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{P} \mathbf{B} \mathbf{P}^{-1}\) back into \(\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}\). So \(\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}\) exactly when \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{P} \mathbf{B} \mathbf{P}^{-1}\), and similarity goes both ways: \(\mathbf{A}\) is similar to \(\mathbf{B}\) through \(\mathbf{P}^{-1}\), whose inverse is \(\mathbf{P}\).










ExmExample 8 (A matrix similar to a diagonal one)






Example 8 (A matrix similar to a diagonal one) Let \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) and \(\mathbf{P} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{bmatrix}\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{P}^2
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 1 & 1 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot(-1) \\ 1 \cdot 1 + (-1) \cdot 1 & 1 \cdot 1 + (-1)(-1) \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-mult}{\text{Definition~7 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\ 0 & 2 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(arithmetic)}
\\
&= 2\,\mathbf{I}_2,
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-scalar-mult}{\text{Definition~6 in Matrices}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-identity-matrix}{\text{Definition~12 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{P}\,(\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{P}) &= \tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{P}^2 \\
&= \mathbf{I}_2
\end{aligned}
\]
and likewise \((\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{P})\,\mathbf{P} = \mathbf{I}_2\) (Theorem 7 in Projections and Linear Systems): \(\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{P}\) (2). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}
&= (\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{P})\,\mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}
&& \text{(substitute } \mathbf{P}^{-1} \text{)}
\\
&= \tfrac{1}{2}\,(\mathbf{P} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{)}
\\
&= \tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{P}\,(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{P})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \tfrac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 1 \\ 3 & -1 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-mult}{\text{Definition~7 in Matrices}}\text{, for } \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P} \text{)}
\\
&= \tfrac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix} 6 & 0 \\ 0 & 2 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-mult}{\text{Definition~7 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix},
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-scalar-mult}{\text{Definition~6 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\mathbf{A}\) is similar to \(\operatorname{diag}(3, 1)\). At the other extreme, the identity matrix is similar only to itself:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{I}_p \mathbf{P} &= \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{P} \\
&= \mathbf{I}_p
\end{aligned}
\]
for every invertible \(\mathbf{P}\) (Theorem 8 in Matrices, 2).










LemTheorem 6 (Similar matrices have the same eigenvalues)






Theorem 6 (Similar matrices have the same eigenvalues) If \(\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}\) (Definition 3), then \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is an eigenvector of \(\mathbf{A}\) for \(\lambda\) exactly when \(\mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is an eigenvector of \(\mathbf{B}\) for \(\lambda\). So \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{B}\) have the same eigenvalues.










ProofProof






Proof. From \(\mathbf{A}\) to \(\mathbf{B}\). Let \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) with \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} = \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}
&= (\mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P})\,(\mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(substitute } \mathbf{B} \text{ and } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A}\,(\mathbf{P} \mathbf{P}^{-1})\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A}\,\mathbf{I}_p\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-inverse}{\text{Definition~5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{P}^{-1}\,(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{P}^{-1}\,(\lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(eigenvector)}
\\
&= \lambda\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}.
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
And \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\): if \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} &= \mathbf{I}_p \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \\
&= \mathbf{P}\,(\mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}) \\
&= \mathbf{P}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}
\end{aligned}
\]
(Theorem 8 in Matrices, 2, Theorem 5 in Matrices, Definition 13 in Matrices), which is false.
From \(\mathbf{B}\) to \(\mathbf{A}\). By Remark 1,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A} &= \mathbf{P} \mathbf{B} \mathbf{P}^{-1} \\
&= (\mathbf{P}^{-1})^{-1} \mathbf{B}\,\mathbf{P}^{-1},
\end{aligned}
\]
so the first part, with \(\mathbf{P}^{-1}\) in place of \(\mathbf{P}\) and the roles of \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{B}\) exchanged, shows that if \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} = \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is an eigenvector of \(\mathbf{B}\) for \(\lambda\), then \(\mathbf{P} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}\) is an eigenvector of \(\mathbf{A}\) for \(\lambda\); and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{P} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} &= \mathbf{P} \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
\end{aligned}
\]
(Theorem 5 in Matrices, 2, Theorem 8 in Matrices).










ExmExample 9 (Eigenvectors carried across a similarity)






Example 9 (Eigenvectors carried across a similarity) In Example 8, \(\mathbf{B} = \operatorname{diag}(3, 1)\) has eigenvectors \((1, 0)\) for \(3\) and \((0, 1)\) for \(1\): \(\operatorname{diag}(3, 1)\,(1, 0) = (3, 0)\) and \(\operatorname{diag}(3, 1)\,(0, 1) = (0, 1)\). \(\mathbf{P}\,(1, 0) = (1, 1)\) and \(\mathbf{P}\,(0, 1) = (1, -1)\) are the eigenvectors of \(\mathbf{A}\) for \(3\) and \(1\) found in Example 24 in Special Matrices and Decompositions.










LemTheorem 7 (Eigenvectors for distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent)






Theorem 7 (Eigenvectors for distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent) If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_k\) are eigenvectors of a \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) for eigenvalues \(\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_k\) that are all different, then \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_k\) are linearly independent (Definition 1 in Subspaces and Rank).










ProofProof






Proof. By induction on \(k\). For \(k = 1\), \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_1 \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) is linearly independent on its own. Suppose the result holds for \(k - 1\) eigenvectors, and \(\sum_{i=1}^{k} c_i \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_i = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}
&= (\mathbf{A} - \lambda_k \mathbf{I}_p) \sum_{i=1}^{k} c_i \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_i
&& \text{(multiply the supposed equation by } \mathbf{A} - \lambda_k \mathbf{I}_p \text{)}
\\
&= \sum_{i=1}^{k} c_i\,(\mathbf{A} - \lambda_k \mathbf{I}_p)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_i
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-subspaces.qmd#thm-matvec-linear}{\text{Theorem~4 in Subspaces and Rank}}\text{)}
\\
&= \sum_{i=1}^{k} c_i\,(\lambda_i - \lambda_k)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_i
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-eigen-shift-power}{\text{Theorem~3}}\text{, part 1, with } s = -\lambda_k \text{)}
\\
&= \sum_{i=1}^{k-1} c_i\,(\lambda_i - \lambda_k)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_i.
&& \text{(the } i = k \text{ term is } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
By the induction hypothesis \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_{k-1}\) are linearly independent, so every \(c_i\,(\lambda_i - \lambda_k) = 0\) for \(i &lt; k\), and since \(\lambda_i \ne \lambda_k\), every \(c_i = 0\) for \(i &lt; k\). The supposed equation then reads \(c_k \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_k = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) with \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_k \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), so \(c_k = 0\) too.










ExmExample 10 (Independent eigenvectors)






Example 10 (Independent eigenvectors) The eigenvectors \((1, 1)\) for \(3\) and \((1, -1)\) for \(1\) of Example 24 in Special Matrices and Decompositions are linearly independent, as Theorem 7 says they must be: \(c_1 (1, 1) + c_2 (1, -1) = (c_1 + c_2, c_1 - c_2)\) is \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) only if
\[
\begin{aligned}
c_1 &= c_2 \\
&= 0.
\end{aligned}
\]
Two eigenvectors for the same eigenvalue need not be independent: \((1, 1)\) and \((2, 2)\) are both eigenvectors for \(3\).










DefDefinition 4 (Diagonalizable matrix)






Definition 4 (Diagonalizable matrix) A \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) is diagonalizable [4] if \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}\) for some invertible \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{X}\) and diagonal \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{\Lambda}\) (Definition 4 in Special Matrices and Decompositions); by Remark 1, that is the same as \(\mathbf{\Lambda} = \mathbf{X}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{X}\), so a diagonalizable matrix is one similar to a diagonal matrix (Definition 3).










ExmExample 11 (Diagonalizable and not)






Example 11 (Diagonalizable and not)  

\(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) is diagonalizable: with \(\mathbf{P}\) as in Example 8, \(\operatorname{diag}(3, 1) = \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}\).
\(\mathbf{J} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\) is not. Suppose \(\mathbf{J} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}\), so \(\mathbf{\Lambda} = \mathbf{X}^{-1} \mathbf{J} \mathbf{X}\) (Remark 1). The eigenvalues of \(\mathbf{\Lambda}\) are its diagonal entries (Theorem 4, since a diagonal matrix is upper triangular) and are the eigenvalues of \(\mathbf{J}\) (Theorem 6), of which \(0\) is the only one (Example 2). So \(\mathbf{\Lambda} = \mathbf{0}_{2 \times 2}\), and then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{J} &= \mathbf{X}\,\mathbf{0}_{2 \times 2}\,\mathbf{X}^{-1} \\
&= \mathbf{0}_{2 \times 2}
\end{aligned}
\]
(1), which it is not.











LemTheorem 8 (Diagonalizable means a basis of eigenvectors)






Theorem 8 (Diagonalizable means a basis of eigenvectors) A \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) is diagonalizable (Definition 4) exactly when it has \(p\) linearly independent eigenvectors. Then \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}\) with the eigenvectors as the columns of \(\mathbf{X}\) and their eigenvalues, in the same order, on the diagonal of \(\mathbf{\Lambda}\).










ProofProof






Proof. Let \(\mathbf{X}\) have columns \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_p\) and \(\mathbf{\Lambda} = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_p)\). Column \(j\) of \(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{X}\) is \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_j\), and column \(j\) of \(\mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}\) is \(\mathbf{X}\) times column \(j\) of \(\mathbf{\Lambda}\) (1), which is
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{X}\,(\lambda_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{e}}\vphantom{e}}_j) &= \lambda_j\,(\mathbf{X} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{e}}\vphantom{e}}_j) \\
&= \lambda_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_j
\end{aligned}
\]
(Theorem 7 in Projections and Linear Systems, Theorem 3 in Subspaces and Rank), so
\[
\mathbf{A} \mathbf{X} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}
\iff \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_j = \lambda_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_j \text{ for every } j.
\tag{1}\]
Also, \(\mathbf{X}\) has rank \(p\) exactly when its \(p\) columns are linearly independent (Definition 2 in Subspaces and Rank), which by Theorem 1 is exactly when \(\mathbf{X}\) is invertible.
Eigenvectors give a diagonalization. If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_p\) are linearly independent eigenvectors, then \(\mathbf{X}\) is invertible and Equation 1 gives \(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{X} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}\), so
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}
&= \mathbf{A}\,\mathbf{I}_p
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}\,(\mathbf{X} \mathbf{X}^{-1})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-inverse}{\text{Definition~5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\\
&= (\mathbf{A} \mathbf{X})\,\mathbf{X}^{-1}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}.
&& \text{(} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{X} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
A diagonalization gives eigenvectors. If \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}\), then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A} \mathbf{X}
&= (\mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1})\,\mathbf{X}
&& \text{(substitute } \mathbf{A} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}\,(\mathbf{X}^{-1} \mathbf{X})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}\,\mathbf{I}_p
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-inverse}{\text{Definition~5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda},
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so each column satisfies \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_j = \lambda_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_j\) (Equation 1). \(\mathbf{X}\) is invertible, so its columns are linearly independent, and in particular nonzero; they are \(p\) linearly independent eigenvectors.










ExmExample 12 (Building the diagonalization from eigenvectors)






Example 12 (Building the diagonalization from eigenvectors) The eigenvectors \((1, 1)\) and \((1, -1)\) of \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) are linearly independent (Example 10), so with \(\mathbf{X} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{bmatrix}\) and \(\mathbf{\Lambda} = \operatorname{diag}(3, 1)\), \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}\); by Remark 1 this is the similarity of Example 8, with \(\mathbf{X} = \mathbf{P}\). Because \(\mathbf{A}\) is symmetric, Theorem 12 in Special Matrices and Decompositions even gives such a factorization with orthonormal eigenvectors (Definition 18 in Special Matrices and Decompositions): dividing each column of \(\mathbf{X}\) by its length \(\sqrt{2}\) gives one. By contrast, every eigenvector of \(\mathbf{J}\) in Example 2 has the form \((t, 0)\), so any two of them are multiples of each other, and \(\mathbf{J}\) has no two linearly independent eigenvectors: another way to see that it is not diagonalizable.










CorCorollary 1 (Distinct eigenvalues make a matrix diagonalizable)






Corollary 1 (Distinct eigenvalues make a matrix diagonalizable) A \(p \times p\) matrix with \(p\) different eigenvalues is diagonalizable.










ProofProof






Proof. Choose one eigenvector for each eigenvalue. These \(p\) eigenvectors are linearly independent (Theorem 7), so the matrix is diagonalizable (Theorem 8).










ExmExample 13 (A triangular matrix with distinct diagonal entries)






Example 13 (A triangular matrix with distinct diagonal entries) \(\mathbf{U}\) of Example 6 has the three different eigenvalues \(2\), \(\tfrac{1}{2}\) and \(-1\) (Theorem 4), so it is diagonalizable, even though it is not symmetric, so Theorem 12 in Special Matrices and Decompositions does not apply to it. The converse fails: \(\mathbf{I}_2\) is diagonal, hence diagonalizable (\(\mathbf{I}_2 = \mathbf{I}_2\,\mathbf{I}_2\,\mathbf{I}_2^{-1}\)), but has only the eigenvalue \(1\).










LemTheorem 9 (Powers of a diagonalizable matrix)






Theorem 9 (Powers of a diagonalizable matrix) If \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}\) (Definition 4) with \(\mathbf{\Lambda} = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_p)\), then for every positive integer \(k\), \(\mathbf{A}^k = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}^k \mathbf{X}^{-1}\) and \(\mathbf{\Lambda}^k = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1^k, \ldots, \lambda_p^k)\).










ProofProof






Proof. Powers of \(\mathbf{\Lambda}\). If \(\mathbf{D} = \operatorname{diag}(d_1, \ldots, d_p)\) and \(\mathbf{E} = \operatorname{diag}(e_1, \ldots, e_p)\), with entries \(d_{il}\) and \(e_{lj}\) (so \(d_{ii} = d_i\) and \(e_{jj} = e_j\)), entry \((i, j)\) of \(\mathbf{D} \mathbf{E}\) is \(\sum_l d_{il}\,e_{lj}\) (1), where \(d_{il} = 0\) unless \(l = i\) and \(e_{lj} = 0\) unless \(l = j\). For \(i \ne j\) no term survives, so the entry is \(0\); for \(i = j\) only \(l = i\) survives, giving \(d_i\,e_i\). So \(\mathbf{D} \mathbf{E} = \operatorname{diag}(d_1 e_1, \ldots, d_p e_p)\), and induction on \(k\) gives \(\mathbf{\Lambda}^k = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1^k, \ldots, \lambda_p^k)\).
Powers of \(\mathbf{A}\), by induction on \(k\). \(k = 1\) is the assumption. If it holds for \(k - 1\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}^k
&= \mathbf{A}^{k-1} \mathbf{A}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-power}{\text{Definition~2 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}^{k-1} \mathbf{X}^{-1}\,\mathbf{A}
&& \text{(induction hypothesis)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}^{k-1} \mathbf{X}^{-1}\,\mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}
&& \text{(the assumption)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}^{k-1}\,(\mathbf{X}^{-1} \mathbf{X})\,\mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}^{k-1}\,\mathbf{I}_p\,\mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-inverse}{\text{Definition~5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}^{k-1} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}^{k} \mathbf{X}^{-1}.
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-power}{\text{Definition~2 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 14 (A closed form for the powers)






Example 14 (A closed form for the powers) For \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\), with \(\mathbf{X}\) as in Example 12 and \(\mathbf{X}^{-1} = \tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{X}\) (Example 8),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}^k
&= \mathbf{X}\,\operatorname{diag}(3^k, 1)\,\mathbf{X}^{-1}
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-diagonalizable-powers}{\text{Theorem~9}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X}\,\operatorname{diag}(3^k, 1)\,(\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{X})
&& \text{(substitute } \mathbf{X}^{-1} \text{)}
\\
&= \tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{X}\,\operatorname{diag}(3^k, 1)\,\mathbf{X}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{)}
\\
&= \tfrac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix} 3^k & 1 \\ 3^k & -1 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-mult}{\text{Definition~7 in Matrices}}\text{, for } \mathbf{X}\,\operatorname{diag}(3^k, 1) \text{)}
\\
&= \tfrac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix} 3^k + 1 & 3^k - 1 \\ 3^k - 1 & 3^k + 1 \end{bmatrix}.
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-mult}{\text{Definition~7 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
For \(k = 2\) this gives \(\tfrac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix} 10 & 8 \\ 8 & 10 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 5 & 4 \\ 4 & 5 \end{bmatrix}\), which matches \(\mathbf{A}^2\) in Example 5.

