This section is adapted from Zhou [1], used under the MIT License (see the license text in Section 2 in Subspaces and Rank). These notes take eigenvalues to be real numbers (Definition 16 in Special Matrices and Decompositions), so the source’s complex eigenvalues are left out, and eigenvalues are characterized through null spaces rather than through the characteristic polynomial. Only the source’s material on similarity, diagonalization and the basic eigenvalue properties is adapted; its characteristic polynomial and algebraic multiplicity, its trace and determinant identities, and the part of its section on symmetric matrices that builds bases of the column space and null space from eigenvectors are left out. The rest of that section is covered by Theorem 12 in Special Matrices and Decompositions and Theorem 13 in Special Matrices and Decompositions. Its result that eigenvalues of orthogonal matrices have modulus \(1\) appears here in the real form \(\pm 1\). Three other parts of the source are not part of this section:

its statement that the eigenvalues of a lower triangular matrix are also its diagonal entries (Theorem 4 states the upper triangular case)
its three further examples of matrices with geometric multiplicity smaller than algebraic multiplicity (Example 2 gives one nilpotent example)
its code cells The source’s two-vector argument that eigenvectors for distinct eigenvalues are independent is extended here to any number of eigenvectors.












LemTheorem 1 (A square matrix is invertible exactly when it has full column rank)






Theorem 1 (A square matrix is invertible exactly when it has full column rank) An \(n \times n\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) is invertible (Definition 6 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) exactly when \(\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{A}) = n\) (full column rank, Definition 3 in Subspaces and Rank), that is, exactly when \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A}) = \mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_n\right\}\mathclose{}\).










ProofProof






Proof. Invertible implies rank \(n\). If \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_n\), then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&= \mathbf{I}_n \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= (\mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{A})\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-inverse}{\text{Definition~5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}^{-1}\,(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}^{-1}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_n
&& \text{(} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_n \text{)}
\\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_n,
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matvec-mult}{\text{Definition~13 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A}) = \mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_n\right\}\mathclose{}\), the nullity is \(0\), and \(\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{A}) = n\) (Theorem 7 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity).
Rank \(n\) implies invertible. Every system \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\) has exactly one solution (Corollary 1 in Projections and Linear Systems, both parts, with \(m = n\)). Let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}_j\) solve \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}_j = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{e}}\vphantom{e}}_j\) (Definition 18 in Vectors), and let \(\mathbf{B}\) have columns \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}_n\). Column \(j\) of \(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B}\) is \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}_j = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{e}}\vphantom{e}}_j\) (1), so \(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B} = \mathbf{I}_n\). For the other order,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}\,(\mathbf{B} \mathbf{A} - \mathbf{I}_n)
&= \mathbf{A} \mathbf{B} \mathbf{A} - \mathbf{A} \mathbf{I}_n
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{I}_n \mathbf{A} - \mathbf{A} \mathbf{I}_n
&& \text{(} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{B} = \mathbf{I}_n \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A} - \mathbf{A}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{0}_{n \times n},
&& \text{(arithmetic)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so every column of \(\mathbf{B} \mathbf{A} - \mathbf{I}_n\) is in \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A}) = \mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_n\right\}\mathclose{}\) (1), and \(\mathbf{B} \mathbf{A} = \mathbf{I}_n\). So \(\mathbf{B}\) satisfies Definition 6 in Special Matrices and Decompositions. The two conditions in the statement are equivalent by Theorem 7 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity: the nullity is \(0\) exactly when \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A}) = \mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_n\right\}\mathclose{}\), because a subspace of dimension \(0\) has the empty list as a basis, whose span is \(\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\right\}\mathclose{}\) (Definition 9 in Subspaces and Rank, Definition 5 in Subspaces and Rank), and \(\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\right\}\mathclose{}\) has dimension \(0\) (Example 16 in Subspaces and Rank).










ExmExample 1 (Rank decides invertibility)






Example 1 (Rank decides invertibility)  

\(\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) of Example 5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions has rank \(2\): \(c_1 (2, 0) + c_2 (1, 1) = (2c_1 + c_2, c_2)\) is \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) only if \(c_2 = 0\) and then \(c_1 = 0\). So it is invertible, as that example found by exhibiting the inverse.
\(\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) has rank less than \(2\): \((1, -1)\) is a nonzero vector in its null space. So it is singular, as Example 5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions found.











DefDefinition 1 (Eigenspace)






Definition 1 (Eigenspace) Let \(\lambda\) be an eigenvalue of a \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) (Definition 16 in Special Matrices and Decompositions). The eigenspace [2] of \(\lambda\) is \(\mathcal{E}_\lambda\stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A} - \lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p)\). It is a subspace of \(\mathbb{R}^p\) (Theorem 2 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity).










DefDefinition 2 (Geometric multiplicity)






Definition 2 (Geometric multiplicity) The dimension (Definition 9 in Subspaces and Rank) of the eigenspace \(\mathcal{E}_\lambda\) (Definition 1) of an eigenvalue \(\lambda\) is the geometric multiplicity of \(\lambda\).










ExmExample 2 (Eigenspaces of two matrices)






Example 2 (Eigenspaces of two matrices)  

For \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) of Example 24 in Special Matrices and Decompositions, \(\mathbf{A} - 3\,\mathbf{I}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} -1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{bmatrix}\) sends \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) to \((v_2 - v_1)\,(1, -1)\), so \(\mathcal{E}_3 = \operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{(1, 1)\right\}\mathclose{}\), with geometric multiplicity \(1\).
For \(\mathbf{J} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\), \(\mathbf{J} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = (v_2, 0)\).
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{J}\,(1, 0) &= (0, 0) \\
&= 0\,(1, 0),
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(0\) is an eigenvalue. If \(\mathbf{J} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) with \(\lambda\ne 0\), the second entry gives \(\lambda v_2 = 0\), so \(v_2 = 0\), and then the first gives \(\lambda v_1 = 0\), so \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\). So \(0\) is the only eigenvalue, and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{E}_0 &= \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{J}) \\
&= \mathopen{}\left\{(t, 0) : t \in \mathbb{R}\right\}\mathclose{},
\end{aligned}
\]
with geometric multiplicity \(1\).











ExmExample 3 (A number that is not an eigenvalue has no eigenspace)






Example 3 (A number that is not an eigenvalue has no eigenspace) For \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) and \(\lambda= 2\), \(\mathbf{A} - 2\,\mathbf{I}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\) sends \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) to \((v_2, v_1)\), which is \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) only for \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), so \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A} - 2\,\mathbf{I}_2) = \mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\right\}\mathclose{}\). And \(2\) is not an eigenvalue (Definition 16 in Special Matrices and Decompositions): \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = 2\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) reads \((2v_1 + v_2, v_1 + 2v_2) = (2v_1, 2v_2)\), so \(v_2 = 0\) and \(v_1 = 0\). So Definition 1 does not apply to \(2\).










LemTheorem 2 (Eigenvalues are where \(\mathbf{A} - \lambda\mathbf{I}\) is singular)






Theorem 2 (Eigenvalues are where \(\mathbf{A} - \lambda\mathbf{I}\) is singular) Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be a \(p \times p\) matrix and \(\lambda\) a real number. Then \(\lambda\) is an eigenvalue of \(\mathbf{A}\) (Definition 16 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) exactly when \(\mathbf{A} - \lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p\) is singular (Definition 6 in Special Matrices and Decompositions). In particular, \(\mathbf{A}\) is singular exactly when \(0\) is one of its eigenvalues; and each eigenspace \(\mathcal{E}_\lambda\) (Definition 1) is a subspace of \(\mathbb{R}^p\) consisting of \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) and the eigenvectors for \(\lambda\).










ProofProof






Proof. For any \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \in \mathbb{R}^p\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
(\mathbf{A} - \lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&= \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} - (\lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{, the difference law)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} - \lambda\,(\mathbf{I}_p \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{, the scalar factor)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} - \lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}},
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) exactly when \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \in \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A} - \lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p)\); the nonzero such \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) are the eigenvectors for \(\lambda\). So \(\lambda\) is an eigenvalue exactly when that null space contains a nonzero vector, which by Theorem 1 is exactly when \(\mathbf{A} - \lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p\) is singular. With \(\lambda= 0\), \(\mathbf{A} - 0\,\mathbf{I}_p = \mathbf{A}\). An eigenspace is a null space, so it is a subspace (Theorem 2 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity).










ExmExample 4 (Eigenvalues and singular matrices)






Example 4 (Eigenvalues and singular matrices) For \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\), \(\mathbf{A} - 3\,\mathbf{I}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} -1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{bmatrix}\) is singular (its columns are negatives of each other, so its rank is \(1\)), matching the eigenvalue \(3\) of Example 24 in Special Matrices and Decompositions. \(\mathbf{A}\) itself has rank \(2\), since \(c_1 (2, 1) + c_2 (1, 2) = (2c_1 + c_2, c_1 + 2c_2)\) is \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) only if
\[
\begin{aligned}
c_1 &= c_2 \\
&= 0
\end{aligned}
\]
(subtract twice the second entry from the first: \(-3c_2 = 0\), and then the second entry gives \(c_1 = 0\)); so it is invertible (Theorem 1), and \(0\) is not an eigenvalue. The singular matrix \(\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) has eigenvalue \(0\), with eigenvector \((1, -1)\).










LemTheorem 3 (Eigenvalues of shifts and powers)






Theorem 3 (Eigenvalues of shifts and powers) Let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) be an eigenvector of a \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) for the eigenvalue \(\lambda\) (Definition 16 in Special Matrices and Decompositions). Then

\(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is an eigenvector of \(\mathbf{A} + s\,\mathbf{I}_p\) for \(\lambda+ s\), for every number \(s\);
\(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is an eigenvector of \(\mathbf{A}^k\) (Definition 2 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) for \(\lambda^k\), for every positive integer \(k\).











ProofProof






Proof. Part 1.
\[
\begin{aligned}
(\mathbf{A} + s\,\mathbf{I}_p)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&= \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} + (s\,\mathbf{I}_p)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-distrib}{\text{Theorem~6 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} + s\,(\mathbf{I}_p \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{)}
\\
&= \lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} + s\,(\mathbf{I}_p \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(eigenvector)}
\\
&= \lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} + s \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= (\lambda+ s)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}.
&& \text{(add entrywise)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Part 2, by induction on \(k\). For \(k = 1\) it is the assumption. If \(\mathbf{A}^{k-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \lambda^{k-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\), then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}^k \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&= \mathbf{A}\,(\mathbf{A}^{k-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-power}{\text{Definition~2 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}\,(\lambda^{k-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(induction hypothesis)}
\\
&= \lambda^{k-1}\,(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{)}
\\
&= \lambda^{k-1}\,(\lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(eigenvector)}
\\
&= \lambda^k \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}.
&& \text{(multiply the numbers)}
\end{aligned}
\]
In both parts \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), so \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is an eigenvector.










ExmExample 5 (Shifting and squaring)






Example 5 (Shifting and squaring) For \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) with eigenvector \((1, 1)\) for \(3\) (Example 24 in Special Matrices and Decompositions):

\(\mathbf{A} - 2\,\mathbf{I}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\) sends \((1, 1)\) to \((1, 1) = (3 - 2)\,(1, 1)\);
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}^2 &= \begin{bmatrix} 2 \cdot 2 + 1 \cdot 1 & 2 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 2 \\ 1 \cdot 2 + 2 \cdot 1 & 1 \cdot 1 + 2 \cdot 2 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 5 & 4 \\ 4 & 5 \end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
\]

sends \((1, 1)\) to \((9, 9) = 3^2\,(1, 1)\).












LemTheorem 4 (The eigenvalues of an upper triangular matrix are its diagonal entries)






Theorem 4 (The eigenvalues of an upper triangular matrix are its diagonal entries) The eigenvalues (Definition 16 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) of an upper triangular \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{U}\) (Definition 7 in Projections and Linear Systems) are exactly its diagonal entries \(u_{11}, \ldots, u_{pp}\).










ProofProof






Proof. \(\mathbf{U} - \lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p\) is upper triangular, with diagonal entries \(u_{ii} - \lambda\) (Definition 12 in Matrices, Definition 5 in Matrices).
If \(\lambda\) is not a diagonal entry, every \(u_{ii} - \lambda\ne 0\), so \((\mathbf{U} - \lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) has exactly one solution, \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) (Theorem 17 in Projections and Linear Systems), and \(\lambda\) is not an eigenvalue (Theorem 2).
If \(\lambda\) is a diagonal entry, let \(k\) be the smallest index with \(u_{kk} = \lambda\), and write \(\mathbf{M} = \mathbf{U} - \lambda\,\mathbf{I}_p\), so \(m_{kk} = 0\) and \(m_{ii} \ne 0\) for \(i &lt; k\). Look for \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) with \(v_k = 1\) and \(v_j = 0\) for \(j &gt; k\). Entry \(i\) of \(\mathbf{M} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is
\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{j=1}^pm_{ij}\,v_j
&= \sum_{j=i}^{p} m_{ij}\,v_j
&& \text{(} m_{ij} = 0 \text{ for } j &lt; i \text{)}
\\
&= \sum_{j=i}^{k} m_{ij}\,v_j,
&& \text{(} v_j = 0 \text{ for } j &gt; k \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
where the last sum is empty, and so \(0\), when \(i &gt; k\). So:

for \(i &gt; k\) entry \(i\) is \(0\);
for \(i = k\) it is \(m_{kk}\,v_k = 0\);
for \(i &lt; k\) it is \(0\) exactly when \(v_i = -\frac{1}{m_{ii}} \sum_{j=i+1}^{k} m_{ij}\,v_j\), and these equations fix \(v_{k-1}, \ldots, v_1\) in turn, as in Theorem 17 in Projections and Linear Systems.

So \(\mathbf{M} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) with \(v_k = 1\), hence \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), and \(\lambda\) is an eigenvalue.










ExmExample 6 (Eigenvalues read off the diagonal)






Example 6 (Eigenvalues read off the diagonal) \(\mathbf{U} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 & -1 \\ 0 & \frac{1}{2} & \frac{1}{2} \\ 0 & 0 & -1 \end{bmatrix}\) of Example 24 in Projections and Linear Systems has eigenvalues \(2\), \(\tfrac{1}{2}\) and \(-1\). For \(\lambda= \tfrac{1}{2}\) (so \(k = 2\)), take \(v_2 = 1\), \(v_3 = 0\); row 1 of \((\mathbf{U} - \tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{I}_3)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is \(\tfrac{3}{2}\,v_1 + 1 = 0\), so \(v_1 = -\tfrac{2}{3}\). Check:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{U}\,(-\tfrac{2}{3}, 1, 0) &= (-\tfrac{4}{3} + 1, \tfrac{1}{2}, 0) \\
&= \tfrac{1}{2}\,(-\tfrac{2}{3}, 1, 0).
\end{aligned}
\]
The matrix \(\mathbf{J}\) of Example 2 is upper triangular with both diagonal entries \(0\), and \(0\) is its only eigenvalue.










LemTheorem 5 (Eigenvalues of idempotent and orthogonal matrices)






Theorem 5 (Eigenvalues of idempotent and orthogonal matrices)  

Every eigenvalue of an idempotent matrix (Definition 7 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) is \(0\) or \(1\).
Every eigenvalue of an orthogonal matrix (Definition 10 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) is \(1\) or \(-1\).











ProofProof






Proof. Let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) with \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\).
Part 1. If \(\mathbf{A}^2 = \mathbf{A}\), then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&= \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(eigenvector)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}^2 \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(idempotent)}
\\
&= \lambda^2 \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}},
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-eigen-shift-power}{\text{Theorem~3}}\text{, part 2)}
\end{aligned}
\]
and subtracting \(\lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) from both sides gives \((\lambda^2 - \lambda)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\). Some entry of \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is nonzero, so
\[
\begin{aligned}
\lambda^2 - \lambda&= \lambda\,(\lambda- 1) \\
&= 0,
\end{aligned}
\]
and a product of two numbers is \(0\) only when one of them is: \(\lambda= 0\) or \(\lambda= 1\).
Part 2. If \(\mathbf{A}\) is orthogonal, then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#thm-orthogonal-norm}{\text{Theorem~6 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
&& \text{(eigenvector)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left|\lambda\right|\mathclose{}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\right\rVert\mathclose{},
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-inner-products.qmd#thm-norm-properties}{\text{Theorem~1 in Inner Products and Orthogonality}}\text{, part 2)}
\end{aligned}
\]
and \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\right\rVert\mathclose{} &gt; 0\) (Theorem 1 in Inner Products and Orthogonality, part 1), so dividing by \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}\) gives \(\mathopen{}\left|\lambda\right|\mathclose{} = 1\); since \(\lambda\) is real (Definition 16 in Special Matrices and Decompositions), \(\lambda= 1\) or \(\lambda= -1\).










ExmExample 7 (Projections and reflections)






Example 7 (Projections and reflections)  

The idempotent matrix \(\begin{bmatrix} 0.5 & 0.5 \\ 0.5 & 0.5 \end{bmatrix}\) of Example 3 in Projections and Linear Systems sends \((1, 1)\) to \((1, 1)\) and \((1, -1)\) to \((0, 0)\): eigenvalues \(1\) and \(0\).
The matrix \(\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\), which swaps the two entries, is orthogonal: its columns \((0, 1)\) and \((1, 0)\) are orthonormal (Remark 6 in Special Matrices and Decompositions). It sends \((1, 1)\) to \((1, 1)\) and \((1, -1)\) to \((-1, 1)\): eigenvalues \(1\) and \(-1\).
The rotation \(\mathbf{Q}\) of Example 13 in Special Matrices and Decompositions has no real eigenvalue. By part 2 the only candidates are \(\pm 1\). \(\mathbf{Q} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) reads \(-0.4\,v_1 - 0.8\,v_2 = 0\) and \(0.8\,v_1 - 0.4\,v_2 = 0\), so \(v_1 = -2 v_2\) and
\[
\begin{aligned}
v_2 &= 2 v_1 \\
&= -4 v_2,
\end{aligned}
\]
forcing \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\); \(\mathbf{Q} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = -\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) reads \(1.6\,v_1 - 0.8\,v_2 = 0\) and \(0.8\,v_1 + 1.6\,v_2 = 0\), so \(v_2 = 2 v_1\) and \(0.8\,v_1 + 3.2\,v_1 = 0\), forcing \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) again.











DefDefinition 3 (Similar matrices)






Definition 3 (Similar matrices) Two \(p \times p\) matrices \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{B}\) are similar [3] if \(\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}\) for some invertible \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{P}\) (Definition 6 in Special Matrices and Decompositions).










RemarkRemark 1 (Similarity read both ways)






Remark 1 (Similarity read both ways). If \(\mathbf{P}\) is invertible, then \(\mathbf{P}^{-1}\) is invertible with inverse \(\mathbf{P}\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{P} &= \mathbf{P} \mathbf{P}^{-1} \\
&= \mathbf{I}_p
\end{aligned}
\]

is the condition of Definition 6 in Special Matrices and Decompositions for \(\mathbf{P}^{-1}\) with \(\mathbf{P}\) as the other factor, and that factor is unique (Remark 3 in Special Matrices and Decompositions). If \(\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}\), then

\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{P} \mathbf{B} \mathbf{P}^{-1}
&= \mathbf{P}\,(\mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P})\,\mathbf{P}^{-1}
&& \text{(substitute } \mathbf{B} \text{)}
\\
&= (\mathbf{P} \mathbf{P}^{-1})\,\mathbf{A}\,(\mathbf{P} \mathbf{P}^{-1})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{I}_p\,\mathbf{A}\,\mathbf{I}_p
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-inverse}{\text{Definition~5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A},
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
and the same steps with \(\mathbf{P}\) and \(\mathbf{P}^{-1}\) exchanged turn \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{P} \mathbf{B} \mathbf{P}^{-1}\) back into \(\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}\). So \(\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}\) exactly when \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{P} \mathbf{B} \mathbf{P}^{-1}\), and similarity goes both ways: \(\mathbf{A}\) is similar to \(\mathbf{B}\) through \(\mathbf{P}^{-1}\), whose inverse is \(\mathbf{P}\).










ExmExample 8 (A matrix similar to a diagonal one)






Example 8 (A matrix similar to a diagonal one) Let \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) and \(\mathbf{P} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{bmatrix}\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{P}^2
&= \begin{bmatrix} 1 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 1 & 1 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot(-1) \\ 1 \cdot 1 + (-1) \cdot 1 & 1 \cdot 1 + (-1)(-1) \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-mult}{\text{Definition~7 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\ 0 & 2 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(arithmetic)}
\\
&= 2\,\mathbf{I}_2,
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-scalar-mult}{\text{Definition~6 in Matrices}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-identity-matrix}{\text{Definition~12 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{P}\,(\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{P}) &= \tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{P}^2 \\
&= \mathbf{I}_2
\end{aligned}
\]
and likewise \((\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{P})\,\mathbf{P} = \mathbf{I}_2\) (Theorem 7 in Projections and Linear Systems): \(\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{P}\) (2). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}
&= (\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{P})\,\mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}
&& \text{(substitute } \mathbf{P}^{-1} \text{)}
\\
&= \tfrac{1}{2}\,(\mathbf{P} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{)}
\\
&= \tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{P}\,(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{P})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \tfrac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 1 \\ 3 & -1 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-mult}{\text{Definition~7 in Matrices}}\text{, for } \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P} \text{)}
\\
&= \tfrac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix} 6 & 0 \\ 0 & 2 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-mult}{\text{Definition~7 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix},
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-scalar-mult}{\text{Definition~6 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\mathbf{A}\) is similar to \(\operatorname{diag}(3, 1)\). At the other extreme, the identity matrix is similar only to itself:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{I}_p \mathbf{P} &= \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{P} \\
&= \mathbf{I}_p
\end{aligned}
\]
for every invertible \(\mathbf{P}\) (Theorem 8 in Matrices, 2).










LemTheorem 6 (Similar matrices have the same eigenvalues)






Theorem 6 (Similar matrices have the same eigenvalues) If \(\mathbf{B} = \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}\) (Definition 3), then \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is an eigenvector of \(\mathbf{A}\) for \(\lambda\) exactly when \(\mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is an eigenvector of \(\mathbf{B}\) for \(\lambda\). So \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{B}\) have the same eigenvalues.










ProofProof






Proof. From \(\mathbf{A}\) to \(\mathbf{B}\). Let \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = \lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) with \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} = \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}
&= (\mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P})\,(\mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(substitute } \mathbf{B} \text{ and } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A}\,(\mathbf{P} \mathbf{P}^{-1})\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A}\,\mathbf{I}_p\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-inverse}{\text{Definition~5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{P}^{-1}\,(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{P}^{-1}\,(\lambda\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})
&& \text{(eigenvector)}
\\
&= \lambda\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}.
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
And \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\): if \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} &= \mathbf{I}_p \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \\
&= \mathbf{P}\,(\mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}) \\
&= \mathbf{P}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}
\end{aligned}
\]
(Theorem 8 in Matrices, 2, Theorem 5 in Matrices, Definition 13 in Matrices), which is false.
From \(\mathbf{B}\) to \(\mathbf{A}\). By Remark 1,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A} &= \mathbf{P} \mathbf{B} \mathbf{P}^{-1} \\
&= (\mathbf{P}^{-1})^{-1} \mathbf{B}\,\mathbf{P}^{-1},
\end{aligned}
\]
so the first part, with \(\mathbf{P}^{-1}\) in place of \(\mathbf{P}\) and the roles of \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{B}\) exchanged, shows that if \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} = \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) is an eigenvector of \(\mathbf{B}\) for \(\lambda\), then \(\mathbf{P} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}\) is an eigenvector of \(\mathbf{A}\) for \(\lambda\); and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{P} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} &= \mathbf{P} \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
\end{aligned}
\]
(Theorem 5 in Matrices, 2, Theorem 8 in Matrices).










ExmExample 9 (Eigenvectors carried across a similarity)






Example 9 (Eigenvectors carried across a similarity) In Example 8, \(\mathbf{B} = \operatorname{diag}(3, 1)\) has eigenvectors \((1, 0)\) for \(3\) and \((0, 1)\) for \(1\): \(\operatorname{diag}(3, 1)\,(1, 0) = (3, 0)\) and \(\operatorname{diag}(3, 1)\,(0, 1) = (0, 1)\). \(\mathbf{P}\,(1, 0) = (1, 1)\) and \(\mathbf{P}\,(0, 1) = (1, -1)\) are the eigenvectors of \(\mathbf{A}\) for \(3\) and \(1\) found in Example 24 in Special Matrices and Decompositions.










LemTheorem 7 (Eigenvectors for distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent)






Theorem 7 (Eigenvectors for distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent) If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_k\) are eigenvectors of a \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) for eigenvalues \(\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_k\) that are all different, then \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_k\) are linearly independent (Definition 1 in Subspaces and Rank).










ProofProof






Proof. By induction on \(k\). For \(k = 1\), \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_1 \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) is linearly independent on its own. Suppose the result holds for \(k - 1\) eigenvectors, and \(\sum_{i=1}^{k} c_i \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_i = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}
&= (\mathbf{A} - \lambda_k \mathbf{I}_p) \sum_{i=1}^{k} c_i \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_i
&& \text{(multiply the supposed equation by } \mathbf{A} - \lambda_k \mathbf{I}_p \text{)}
\\
&= \sum_{i=1}^{k} c_i\,(\mathbf{A} - \lambda_k \mathbf{I}_p)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_i
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-subspaces.qmd#thm-matvec-linear}{\text{Theorem~4 in Subspaces and Rank}}\text{)}
\\
&= \sum_{i=1}^{k} c_i\,(\lambda_i - \lambda_k)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_i
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-eigen-shift-power}{\text{Theorem~3}}\text{, part 1, with } s = -\lambda_k \text{)}
\\
&= \sum_{i=1}^{k-1} c_i\,(\lambda_i - \lambda_k)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_i.
&& \text{(the } i = k \text{ term is } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
By the induction hypothesis \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_{k-1}\) are linearly independent, so every \(c_i\,(\lambda_i - \lambda_k) = 0\) for \(i &lt; k\), and since \(\lambda_i \ne \lambda_k\), every \(c_i = 0\) for \(i &lt; k\). The supposed equation then reads \(c_k \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_k = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) with \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}_k \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), so \(c_k = 0\) too.










ExmExample 10 (Independent eigenvectors)






Example 10 (Independent eigenvectors) The eigenvectors \((1, 1)\) for \(3\) and \((1, -1)\) for \(1\) of Example 24 in Special Matrices and Decompositions are linearly independent, as Theorem 7 says they must be: \(c_1 (1, 1) + c_2 (1, -1) = (c_1 + c_2, c_1 - c_2)\) is \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) only if
\[
\begin{aligned}
c_1 &= c_2 \\
&= 0.
\end{aligned}
\]
Two eigenvectors for the same eigenvalue need not be independent: \((1, 1)\) and \((2, 2)\) are both eigenvectors for \(3\).










DefDefinition 4 (Diagonalizable matrix)






Definition 4 (Diagonalizable matrix) A \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) is diagonalizable [4] if \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}\) for some invertible \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{X}\) and diagonal \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{\Lambda}\) (Definition 4 in Special Matrices and Decompositions); by Remark 1, that is the same as \(\mathbf{\Lambda} = \mathbf{X}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{X}\), so a diagonalizable matrix is one similar to a diagonal matrix (Definition 3).










ExmExample 11 (Diagonalizable and not)






Example 11 (Diagonalizable and not)  

\(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) is diagonalizable: with \(\mathbf{P}\) as in Example 8, \(\operatorname{diag}(3, 1) = \mathbf{P}^{-1} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}\).
\(\mathbf{J} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\) is not. Suppose \(\mathbf{J} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}\), so \(\mathbf{\Lambda} = \mathbf{X}^{-1} \mathbf{J} \mathbf{X}\) (Remark 1). The eigenvalues of \(\mathbf{\Lambda}\) are its diagonal entries (Theorem 4, since a diagonal matrix is upper triangular) and are the eigenvalues of \(\mathbf{J}\) (Theorem 6), of which \(0\) is the only one (Example 2). So \(\mathbf{\Lambda} = \mathbf{0}_{2 \times 2}\), and then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{J} &= \mathbf{X}\,\mathbf{0}_{2 \times 2}\,\mathbf{X}^{-1} \\
&= \mathbf{0}_{2 \times 2}
\end{aligned}
\]
(1), which it is not.











LemTheorem 8 (Diagonalizable means a basis of eigenvectors)






Theorem 8 (Diagonalizable means a basis of eigenvectors) A \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) is diagonalizable (Definition 4) exactly when it has \(p\) linearly independent eigenvectors. Then \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}\) with the eigenvectors as the columns of \(\mathbf{X}\) and their eigenvalues, in the same order, on the diagonal of \(\mathbf{\Lambda}\).










ProofProof






Proof. Let \(\mathbf{X}\) have columns \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_p\) and \(\mathbf{\Lambda} = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_p)\). Column \(j\) of \(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{X}\) is \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_j\), and column \(j\) of \(\mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}\) is \(\mathbf{X}\) times column \(j\) of \(\mathbf{\Lambda}\) (1), which is
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{X}\,(\lambda_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{e}}\vphantom{e}}_j) &= \lambda_j\,(\mathbf{X} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{e}}\vphantom{e}}_j) \\
&= \lambda_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_j
\end{aligned}
\]
(Theorem 7 in Projections and Linear Systems, Theorem 3 in Subspaces and Rank), so
\[
\mathbf{A} \mathbf{X} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}
\iff \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_j = \lambda_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_j \text{ for every } j.
\tag{1}\]
Also, \(\mathbf{X}\) has rank \(p\) exactly when its \(p\) columns are linearly independent (Definition 2 in Subspaces and Rank), which by Theorem 1 is exactly when \(\mathbf{X}\) is invertible.
Eigenvectors give a diagonalization. If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_1, \ldots, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_p\) are linearly independent eigenvectors, then \(\mathbf{X}\) is invertible and Equation 1 gives \(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{X} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}\), so
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}
&= \mathbf{A}\,\mathbf{I}_p
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}\,(\mathbf{X} \mathbf{X}^{-1})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-inverse}{\text{Definition~5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\\
&= (\mathbf{A} \mathbf{X})\,\mathbf{X}^{-1}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}.
&& \text{(} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{X} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
A diagonalization gives eigenvectors. If \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}\), then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A} \mathbf{X}
&= (\mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1})\,\mathbf{X}
&& \text{(substitute } \mathbf{A} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}\,(\mathbf{X}^{-1} \mathbf{X})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}\,\mathbf{I}_p
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-inverse}{\text{Definition~5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda},
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so each column satisfies \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_j = \lambda_j \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}_j\) (Equation 1). \(\mathbf{X}\) is invertible, so its columns are linearly independent, and in particular nonzero; they are \(p\) linearly independent eigenvectors.










ExmExample 12 (Building the diagonalization from eigenvectors)






Example 12 (Building the diagonalization from eigenvectors) The eigenvectors \((1, 1)\) and \((1, -1)\) of \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) are linearly independent (Example 10), so with \(\mathbf{X} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{bmatrix}\) and \(\mathbf{\Lambda} = \operatorname{diag}(3, 1)\), \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}\); by Remark 1 this is the similarity of Example 8, with \(\mathbf{X} = \mathbf{P}\). Because \(\mathbf{A}\) is symmetric, Theorem 12 in Special Matrices and Decompositions even gives such a factorization with orthonormal eigenvectors (Definition 18 in Special Matrices and Decompositions): dividing each column of \(\mathbf{X}\) by its length \(\sqrt{2}\) gives one. By contrast, every eigenvector of \(\mathbf{J}\) in Example 2 has the form \((t, 0)\), so any two of them are multiples of each other, and \(\mathbf{J}\) has no two linearly independent eigenvectors: another way to see that it is not diagonalizable.










CorCorollary 1 (Distinct eigenvalues make a matrix diagonalizable)






Corollary 1 (Distinct eigenvalues make a matrix diagonalizable) A \(p \times p\) matrix with \(p\) different eigenvalues is diagonalizable.










ProofProof






Proof. Choose one eigenvector for each eigenvalue. These \(p\) eigenvectors are linearly independent (Theorem 7), so the matrix is diagonalizable (Theorem 8).










ExmExample 13 (A triangular matrix with distinct diagonal entries)






Example 13 (A triangular matrix with distinct diagonal entries) \(\mathbf{U}\) of Example 6 has the three different eigenvalues \(2\), \(\tfrac{1}{2}\) and \(-1\) (Theorem 4), so it is diagonalizable, even though it is not symmetric, so Theorem 12 in Special Matrices and Decompositions does not apply to it. The converse fails: \(\mathbf{I}_2\) is diagonal, hence diagonalizable (\(\mathbf{I}_2 = \mathbf{I}_2\,\mathbf{I}_2\,\mathbf{I}_2^{-1}\)), but has only the eigenvalue \(1\).










LemTheorem 9 (Powers of a diagonalizable matrix)






Theorem 9 (Powers of a diagonalizable matrix) If \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}\) (Definition 4) with \(\mathbf{\Lambda} = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_p)\), then for every positive integer \(k\), \(\mathbf{A}^k = \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}^k \mathbf{X}^{-1}\) and \(\mathbf{\Lambda}^k = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1^k, \ldots, \lambda_p^k)\).










ProofProof






Proof. Powers of \(\mathbf{\Lambda}\). If \(\mathbf{D} = \operatorname{diag}(d_1, \ldots, d_p)\) and \(\mathbf{E} = \operatorname{diag}(e_1, \ldots, e_p)\), with entries \(d_{il}\) and \(e_{lj}\) (so \(d_{ii} = d_i\) and \(e_{jj} = e_j\)), entry \((i, j)\) of \(\mathbf{D} \mathbf{E}\) is \(\sum_l d_{il}\,e_{lj}\) (1), where \(d_{il} = 0\) unless \(l = i\) and \(e_{lj} = 0\) unless \(l = j\). For \(i \ne j\) no term survives, so the entry is \(0\); for \(i = j\) only \(l = i\) survives, giving \(d_i\,e_i\). So \(\mathbf{D} \mathbf{E} = \operatorname{diag}(d_1 e_1, \ldots, d_p e_p)\), and induction on \(k\) gives \(\mathbf{\Lambda}^k = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1^k, \ldots, \lambda_p^k)\).
Powers of \(\mathbf{A}\), by induction on \(k\). \(k = 1\) is the assumption. If it holds for \(k - 1\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}^k
&= \mathbf{A}^{k-1} \mathbf{A}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-power}{\text{Definition~2 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}^{k-1} \mathbf{X}^{-1}\,\mathbf{A}
&& \text{(induction hypothesis)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}^{k-1} \mathbf{X}^{-1}\,\mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}
&& \text{(the assumption)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}^{k-1}\,(\mathbf{X}^{-1} \mathbf{X})\,\mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}^{k-1}\,\mathbf{I}_p\,\mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-inverse}{\text{Definition~5 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}^{k-1} \mathbf{\Lambda} \mathbf{X}^{-1}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X} \mathbf{\Lambda}^{k} \mathbf{X}^{-1}.
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-power}{\text{Definition~2 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 14 (A closed form for the powers)






Example 14 (A closed form for the powers) For \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\), with \(\mathbf{X}\) as in Example 12 and \(\mathbf{X}^{-1} = \tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{X}\) (Example 8),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}^k
&= \mathbf{X}\,\operatorname{diag}(3^k, 1)\,\mathbf{X}^{-1}
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-diagonalizable-powers}{\text{Theorem~9}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{X}\,\operatorname{diag}(3^k, 1)\,(\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{X})
&& \text{(substitute } \mathbf{X}^{-1} \text{)}
\\
&= \tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbf{X}\,\operatorname{diag}(3^k, 1)\,\mathbf{X}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{)}
\\
&= \tfrac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix} 3^k & 1 \\ 3^k & -1 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & -1 \end{bmatrix}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-mult}{\text{Definition~7 in Matrices}}\text{, for } \mathbf{X}\,\operatorname{diag}(3^k, 1) \text{)}
\\
&= \tfrac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix} 3^k + 1 & 3^k - 1 \\ 3^k - 1 & 3^k + 1 \end{bmatrix}.
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-mult}{\text{Definition~7 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
For \(k = 2\) this gives \(\tfrac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix} 10 & 8 \\ 8 & 10 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 5 & 4 \\ 4 & 5 \end{bmatrix}\), which matches \(\mathbf{A}^2\) in Example 5.

2 Gram matrices, Schur complements and Cholesky






NoteSource





This section is adapted from Zhou [5], used under the MIT License (see the license text in Section 2 in Subspaces and Rank). The source’s eigenvalue and quadratic-form characterizations of definiteness are covered in these notes already (Theorem 17 in Special Matrices and Decompositions). These parts of the source are left out:

its covariance test, which needs probability
its material on minimization
its positive semidefinite version of the Schur complement test
its pivot test
its Hadamard-product “Schur lemma”
its notes on the LU factorization and computational cost
its statement that a nonnegative combination of positive semidefinite matrices is positive semidefinite (Theorem 11 states the positive definite version)
its section on the ellipse \(a x^2 + 2 b x y + c y^2 = 1\), with its worked example of a positive definite and an indefinite matrix
its numerical example of the Cholesky factorization

The source leaves the Schur complement test unproved; it is proved here from a block expansion of the quadratic form, and the Cholesky factorization is derived from it.











LemTheorem 10 (Definite matrices are Gram matrices)






Theorem 10 (Definite matrices are Gram matrices) Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be a \(p \times p\) matrix.

\(\mathbf{A}\) is positive semidefinite (Definition 21 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) exactly when \(\mathbf{A} = {\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{B}\) for some matrix \(\mathbf{B}\) with \(p\) columns.
\(\mathbf{A}\) is positive definite (Definition 22 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) exactly when \(\mathbf{A} = {\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{B}\) for some matrix \(\mathbf{B}\) with \(p\) linearly independent columns.











ProofProof






Proof. If \(\mathbf{A} = {\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{B}\). \(\mathbf{A}\) is symmetric, since
\[
\begin{aligned}
{({\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{B})}^{\top} &= {\mathbf{B}}^{\top}\,{({\mathbf{B}}^{\top})}^{\top} \\
&= {\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{B}
\end{aligned}
\]
(Theorem 17 in Matrices, Definition 3 in Matrices). For any \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{B}}^{\top}\,\mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(substitute } \mathbf{A} \text{)}
\\
&= {(\mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-transpose-product}{\text{Theorem~17 in Matrices}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#eq-l2-norm}{\text{Equation~2 in Vectors}}\text{, squared)}
\\
&\ge 0.
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-inner-products.qmd#thm-norm-properties}{\text{Theorem~1 in Inner Products and Orthogonality}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
If the columns of \(\mathbf{B}\) are linearly independent, then \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{B}) = \mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\right\}\mathclose{}\) (\(\mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) is the combination of the columns with coefficients \(x_j\), Theorem 3 in Subspaces and Rank, and only the zero combination is \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), Definition 1 in Subspaces and Rank), so for \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), \(\mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) and \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 &gt; 0\) (Theorem 1 in Inner Products and Orthogonality, part 1).
Only if. Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be positive semidefinite. It is symmetric, so \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{Q} \mathbf{\Lambda} {\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\) (Theorem 12 in Special Matrices and Decompositions), and every \(\lambda_i \ge 0\) (Theorem 17 in Special Matrices and Decompositions). Let \(\mathbf{\Lambda}^{1/2} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\operatorname{diag}(\sqrt{\lambda_1}, \ldots, \sqrt{\lambda_p})\), which is symmetric, with \(\mathbf{\Lambda}^{1/2} \mathbf{\Lambda}^{1/2} = \mathbf{\Lambda}\) (the product of diagonal matrices multiplies their diagonals, as in the proof of Theorem 9), and let \(\mathbf{B} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathbf{\Lambda}^{1/2} {\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{B}
&= {({\mathbf{Q}}^{\top})}^{\top}\,{(\mathbf{\Lambda}^{1/2})}^{\top}\,\mathbf{\Lambda}^{1/2} {\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-transpose-product}{\text{Theorem~17 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{Q}\,\mathbf{\Lambda}^{1/2} \mathbf{\Lambda}^{1/2}\,{\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-transpose}{\text{Definition~3 in Matrices}}\text{; } \mathbf{\Lambda}^{1/2} \text{ is symmetric)}
\\
&= \mathbf{Q} \mathbf{\Lambda} {\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}
&& \text{(} \mathbf{\Lambda}^{1/2} \mathbf{\Lambda}^{1/2} = \mathbf{\Lambda} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}.
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#thm-spectral}{\text{Theorem~12 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
If \(\mathbf{A}\) is positive definite, it is positive semidefinite (Remark 19 in Special Matrices and Decompositions), so the same \(\mathbf{B}\) gives \(\mathbf{A} = {\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{B}\); and every \(\lambda_i &gt; 0\) (Theorem 17 in Special Matrices and Decompositions), so \(\mathbf{\Lambda}^{1/2}\) is diagonal with nonzero diagonal, and invertible (its inverse is \(\operatorname{diag}(1/\sqrt{\lambda_1}, \ldots, 1/\sqrt{\lambda_p})\)); \({\mathbf{Q}}^{\top}\) is invertible with inverse \(\mathbf{Q}\) (Remark 6 in Special Matrices and Decompositions). So \(\mathbf{B}\) is invertible (Theorem 1 in Special Matrices and Decompositions), and its \(p\) columns are linearly independent (Theorem 1, Definition 2 in Subspaces and Rank).










ExmExample 15 (Gram factorizations)






Example 15 (Gram factorizations)  

With \(\mathbf{R} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) (\(1 \times 2\)), \({\mathbf{R}}^{\top} \mathbf{R} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\), the positive semidefinite matrix of Example 30 in Special Matrices and Decompositions; the two columns of \(\mathbf{R}\), \(1\) and \(1\), are dependent, so part 2 does not apply to \(\mathbf{R}\); \({\mathbf{R}}^{\top} \mathbf{R}\) is in fact not positive definite (Example 32 in Special Matrices and Decompositions).
With \(\mathbf{X}= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \\ 1 & 3 \end{bmatrix}\), whose columns are independent (Example 2 in Subspaces and Rank), \({\mathbf{X}}^{\top} \mathbf{X}= \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 6 \\ 6 & 14 \end{bmatrix}\) (Example 1 in Projections and Linear Systems) is positive definite.
\(\mathbf{D} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 2 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) of Example 32 in Special Matrices and Decompositions is symmetric but not positive semidefinite, so by part 1 it is not \({\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{B}\) for any \(\mathbf{B}\).











LemTheorem 11 (Operations that preserve definiteness)






Theorem 11 (Operations that preserve definiteness)  

If \(\mathbf{C}\) is a \(p \times p\) positive definite matrix (Definition 22 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) and \(\mathbf{A}\) is a \(p \times k\) matrix with \(k\) linearly independent columns, then the \(k \times k\) matrix \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathbf{C} \mathbf{A}\) is positive definite.
If \(\mathbf{A}_1\) and \(\mathbf{A}_2\) are positive definite \(p \times p\) matrices and \(\alpha_1, \alpha_2 &gt; 0\), then \(\alpha_1 \mathbf{A}_1 + \alpha_2 \mathbf{A}_2\) is positive definite.











ProofProof






Proof. Part 1. \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathbf{C} \mathbf{A}\) is symmetric:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{({\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathbf{C} \mathbf{A})}^{\top} &= {\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{C}}^{\top}\,{({\mathbf{A}}^{\top})}^{\top} \\
&= {\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathbf{C} \mathbf{A}
\end{aligned}
\]
(Theorem 17 in Matrices, twice; \(\mathbf{C}\) symmetric; Definition 3 in Matrices). For \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_k\), \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_p\), since the columns of \(\mathbf{A}\) are independent (Theorem 3 in Subspaces and Rank, Definition 1 in Subspaces and Rank), and
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,({\mathbf{A}}^{\top} \mathbf{C} \mathbf{A})\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&= {(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})}^{\top}\,\mathbf{C}\,(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-transpose-product}{\text{Theorem~17 in Matrices}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-assoc}{\text{Theorem~5 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&&gt; 0.
&& \text{(} \mathbf{C} \text{ positive definite, } \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Part 2. \(\alpha_1 \mathbf{A}_1 + \alpha_2 \mathbf{A}_2\) is symmetric: its transpose is \(\alpha_1 {\mathbf{A}_1}^{\top} + \alpha_2 {\mathbf{A}_2}^{\top} = \alpha_1 \mathbf{A}_1 + \alpha_2 \mathbf{A}_2\) (Theorem 16 in Matrices, Definition 6 in Matrices, Definition 3 in Matrices). For \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,(\alpha_1 \mathbf{A}_1 + \alpha_2 \mathbf{A}_2)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,(\alpha_1 \mathbf{A}_1)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}+ {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,(\alpha_2 \mathbf{A}_2)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-distrib}{\text{Theorem~6 in Matrices}}\text{, twice)}
\\
&= \alpha_1\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A}_1 \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \alpha_2\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A}_2 \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{)}
\\
&&gt; 0.
&& \text{(each term is positive)}
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 16 (Building positive definite matrices)






Example 16 (Building positive definite matrices)  

With \(\mathbf{C} = \mathbf{I}_3\) and \(\mathbf{X}\) of Example 15, part 1 gives again that \({\mathbf{X}}^{\top} \mathbf{X}\) is positive definite.
With \(\alpha_1 = 2\), \(\mathbf{A}_1 = \mathbf{I}_2\) (Example 32 in Special Matrices and Decompositions), \(\alpha_2 = 1\) and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A}_2 &= {\mathbf{X}}^{\top} \mathbf{X}\\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 6 \\ 6 & 14 \end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
\]
(Example 15), part 2 gives that \(2 \mathbf{I}_2 + {\mathbf{X}}^{\top} \mathbf{X}= \begin{bmatrix} 5 & 6 \\ 6 & 16 \end{bmatrix}\) is positive definite.
An aside beyond part 2: \(\mathbf{I}_2 + \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) is positive definite plus positive semidefinite; its quadratic form is \(x_1^2 + x_2^2 + (x_1 + x_2)^2 &gt; 0\) for \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), so it is positive definite, in line with its eigenvalues \(3\) and \(1\) (Example 24 in Special Matrices and Decompositions).
Independence is needed in part 1: with \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\), whose columns are equal, \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\,\mathbf{I}_2\,\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 2 \\ 2 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\), and \((1, -1)\) gives quadratic form \(0\).











LemTheorem 12 (A positive definite matrix is invertible)






Theorem 12 (A positive definite matrix is invertible) Every positive definite matrix (Definition 22 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) is invertible (Definition 6 in Special Matrices and Decompositions).










ProofProof






Proof. Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be a \(p \times p\) positive definite matrix, and let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) be a vector of length \(p\) with \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_p\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#def-quadratic-form}{\text{Definition~11 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_p
&& \text{(} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_p \text{)}
\\
&= 0.
&& \text{(every term of the inner product is } 0 \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_p\), then \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} &gt; 0\) because \(\mathbf{A}\) is positive definite, which contradicts \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} = 0\). So \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_p\), the null space \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A})\) is \(\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_p\right\}\mathclose{}\), and \(\mathbf{A}\) is invertible by Theorem 1.










ExmExample 17 (Positive definite matrices are invertible; the converse fails)






Example 17 (Positive definite matrices are invertible; the converse fails) \(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) is positive definite, since
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&= 2 x_1^2 + 2 x_1 x_2 + 2 x_2^2
&& \text{(multiply out)}
\\
&= x_1^2 + x_2^2 + (x_1 + x_2)^2,
&& \text{(expand the square)}
\end{aligned}
\]
which is \(0\) only when \(x_1 = x_2 = 0\). It is invertible, with \({\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{A}\right)\mathclose{}}^{-1} = \frac{1}{3}\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 \\ -1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\).
The converse fails. \(\mathbf{D} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 2 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) of Example 32 in Special Matrices and Decompositions is not positive definite, yet it is invertible: \({\mathopen{}\left(\mathbf{D}\right)\mathclose{}}^{-1} = \frac{1}{3}\begin{bmatrix} -1 & 2 \\ 2 & -1 \end{bmatrix}\).










LemTheorem 13 (Adding a positive multiple of the identity makes a matrix positive definite)






Theorem 13 (Adding a positive multiple of the identity makes a matrix positive definite) Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be a \(p \times p\) positive semidefinite matrix (Definition 21 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) and let \(\alpha&gt; 0\). Then \(\mathbf{A} + \alpha\mathbf{I}_p\) is positive definite (Definition 22 in Special Matrices and Decompositions), so it is invertible (Theorem 12).










ProofProof






Proof. \(\mathbf{A} + \alpha\mathbf{I}_p\) is symmetric: its transpose is \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top} + \alpha\mathbf{I}_p = \mathbf{A} + \alpha\mathbf{I}_p\) (Theorem 16 in Matrices, Definition 6 in Matrices, Definition 3 in Matrices). For \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_p\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{A} + \alpha\mathbf{I}_p)\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \alpha\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top}\,\mathbf{I}_p\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-distrib}{\text{Theorem~6 in Matrices}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} + \alpha\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-identity}{\text{Theorem~8 in Matrices}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#eq-l2-norm}{\text{Equation~2 in Vectors}}\text{, squared)}
\\
&&gt; 0.
&& \text{(} {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} \ge 0, \; \alpha&gt; 0, \; \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_p \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 18 (Repairing a singular matrix)






Example 18 (Repairing a singular matrix) \(\mathbf{B} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) of Example 30 in Special Matrices and Decompositions is positive semidefinite and singular: \(\mathbf{B} (1, -1) = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_2\). With \(\alpha= 1\),
\[
\mathbf{B} + \mathbf{I}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix},
\]
which is positive definite and invertible by Example 17.










CorCorollary 2 (\({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\) is invertible exactly when \(\mathbf{X}\) has full column rank)






Corollary 2 (\({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\) is invertible exactly when \(\mathbf{X}\) has full column rank) For an \(n \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{X}\), the \(p \times p\) matrix \({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\) is invertible (Definition 6 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) exactly when \(\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{X}) = p\) (Definition 3 in Subspaces and Rank).










ProofProof






Proof. The following statements are equivalent:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\text{ is invertible}
&\iff \mathcal{N}({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}) = \mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_p\right\}\mathclose{}
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-invertible-rank}{\text{Theorem~1}}\text{)}
\\
&\iff \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{X}) = \mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_p\right\}\mathclose{}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-rank-nullity.qmd#thm-null-gram}{\text{Theorem~5 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity}}\text{)}
\\
&\iff \mathbf{X}\text{ has full column rank.}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-rank-nullity.qmd#thm-independent-null-space}{\text{Theorem~3 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 19 (A design matrix with a repeated column)






Example 19 (A design matrix with a repeated column) \(\mathbf{X}= \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 1 & 2 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) of Example 2 in Subspaces and Rank has rank \(1 &lt; 2\). Its cross-product matrix is
\[
{\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}= \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 6 \\ 6 & 12 \end{bmatrix},
\]
which is singular: \((2, -1)\) is in its null space, because \(\mathbf{X}(2, -1) = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_3\) (Example 5 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity). For the full-column-rank matrix of Example 1 in Projections and Linear Systems, \({\mathbf{X}}^{\top}\mathbf{X}\) is invertible.










DefDefinition 5 (Block matrix)






Definition 5 (Block matrix) Split the rows of an \(m \times n\) matrix \(\mathbf{X}\) into the first \(k\) and the last \(m - k\), and its columns into the first \(l\) and the last \(n - l\), with \(1 \le k &lt; m\) and \(1 \le l &lt; n\). This cuts \(\mathbf{X}\) into four smaller matrices, its blocks:
\[
\mathbf{X} = \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{A} & \mathbf{B} \\ \mathbf{C} & \mathbf{D} \end{bmatrix},
\]
where \(\mathbf{A}\) (\(k \times l\)) holds the entries \(x_{ij}\) with \(i \le k\) and \(j \le l\), \(\mathbf{B}\) (\(k \times (n - l)\)) those with \(i \le k\) and \(j &gt; l\), \(\mathbf{C}\) (\((m - k) \times l\)) those with \(i &gt; k\) and \(j \le l\), and \(\mathbf{D}\) (\((m - k) \times (n - l)\)) those with \(i &gt; k\) and \(j &gt; l\), each in its original order. A matrix written this way is a block matrix [6].










ExmExample 20 (Cutting a \(3 \times 3\) matrix into blocks)






Example 20 (Cutting a \(3 \times 3\) matrix into blocks) With
\[
\begin{aligned}
k &= l \\
&= 1,
\end{aligned}
\]
\[
\mathbf{X} = \begin{bmatrix} 4 & 2 & 1 \\ 2 & 5 & 3 \\ 1 & 3 & 6 \end{bmatrix}
= \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{A} & \mathbf{B} \\ \mathbf{C} & \mathbf{D} \end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \end{bmatrix},
\quad
\mathbf{B} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \end{bmatrix},
\quad
\mathbf{C} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
\quad
\mathbf{D} = \begin{bmatrix} 5 & 3 \\ 3 & 6 \end{bmatrix}.
\]
For instance, entry \((1, 2)\) of \(\mathbf{D}\) is
\[
\begin{aligned}
x_{1 + 1, 2 + 1} &= x_{23} \\
&= 3.
\end{aligned}
\]
With
\[
\begin{aligned}
k &= l \\
&= 2
\end{aligned}
\]
instead, the leading \(2 \times 2\) block is \(\begin{bmatrix} 4 & 2 \\ 2 & 5 \end{bmatrix}\).










DefDefinition 6 (Leading block of a square matrix, top-left block)






Definition 6 (Leading block of a square matrix, top-left block) When \(\mathbf{X}\) is \(n \times n\) and \(1 \le k \le n\), the \(k \times k\) matrix of the entries \(x_{ij}\) with \(i, j \le k\) is the leading (or top-left) \(k \times k\) block of \(\mathbf{X}\); for \(k &lt; n\) it is the block \(\mathbf{A}\) of Definition 5 with \(l = k\), and for \(k = n\) it is \(\mathbf{X}\) itself.










LemTheorem 14 (Leading blocks of a positive definite matrix are positive definite)






Theorem 14 (Leading blocks of a positive definite matrix are positive definite) Let \(\mathbf{X}\) be a \(p \times p\) positive definite matrix (Definition 22 in Special Matrices and Decompositions), and for \(1 \le k \le p\) let \(\mathbf{X}_k\) be its leading \(k \times k\) block (Definition 5), with entries \(x_{ij}\) for \(i, j \le k\). Then \(\mathbf{X}_k\) is positive definite. Also, every diagonal entry of a positive definite matrix is positive.










ProofProof






Proof. \(\mathbf{X}_k\) is symmetric, because \(\mathbf{X}\) is. For \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} \in \mathbb{R}^k\) with \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = (u_1, \ldots, u_k, 0, \ldots, 0) \in \mathbb{R}^p\), which is nonzero, with entries \(y_i = u_i\) for \(i \le k\) and \(y_i = 0\) for \(i &gt; k\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{X}_k \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}
&= \sum_{i=1}^{k} \sum_{j=1}^{k} u_i\,x_{ij}\,u_j
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matvec-mult}{\text{Definition~13 in Matrices}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#def-dot-product}{\text{Definition~8 in Vectors}}\text{)}
\\
&= \sum_{i=1}^{k} \sum_{j=1}^{k} y_i\,x_{ij}\,y_j
&& \text{(} y_i = u_i \text{ for } i \le k \text{)}
\\
&= \sum_{i=1}^p\sum_{j=1}^py_i\,x_{ij}\,y_j
&& \text{(each added term has a factor } y_i = 0 \text{ or } y_j = 0 \text{)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}}^{\top} \mathbf{X} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matvec-mult}{\text{Definition~13 in Matrices}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#def-dot-product}{\text{Definition~8 in Vectors}}\text{)}
\\
&&gt; 0.
&& \text{(} \mathbf{X} \text{ positive definite, } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
For the diagonal entry \(x_{ii}\), take \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{e}}\vphantom{e}}_i\) (Definition 18 in Vectors), which is nonzero:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{e}}\vphantom{e}}_i}^{\top} \mathbf{X} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{e}}\vphantom{e}}_i
&= \sum_{r=1}^{p} \sum_{s=1}^{p} (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{e}}\vphantom{e}}_i)_r\,x_{rs}\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{e}}\vphantom{e}}_i)_s
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matvec-mult}{\text{Definition~13 in Matrices}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#def-dot-product}{\text{Definition~8 in Vectors}}\text{)}
\\
&= x_{ii}
&& \text{(only the term with } r \text{ and } s \text{ both equal to } i \text{ is nonzero)}
\\
&&gt; 0.
&& \text{(} \mathbf{X} \text{ positive definite)}
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 21 (A quick necessary test)






Example 21 (A quick necessary test) \(\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\) is positive definite (Example 16), and its diagonal entries \(2, 2\) and its \(1 \times 1\) leading block \([2]\) are positive. The test can only rule matrices out: \(-\mathbf{I}_2\) has negative diagonal entries, so it is not positive definite, even though
\[
\begin{aligned}
\det(-\mathbf{I}_2) &= (-1)(-1) - 0 \\
&= 1 \\
&&gt; 0
\end{aligned}
\]
(Definition 26 in Special Matrices and Decompositions); a positive determinant alone does not make a matrix positive definite. And positive diagonal entries are not enough: \(\mathbf{D} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 2 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) of Example 32 in Special Matrices and Decompositions has them but is not positive definite.










LemTheorem 15 (The quadratic form of a block matrix)






Theorem 15 (The quadratic form of a block matrix) Let \(k, m \ge 1\), let \(\mathbf{A}\) be a symmetric \(k \times k\) matrix, \(\mathbf{B}\) a \(k \times m\) matrix and \(\mathbf{C}\) a symmetric \(m \times m\) matrix, and let \(\mathbf{X}\) be the \((k + m) \times (k + m)\) block matrix (Definition 5)
\[
\mathbf{X} = \begin{bmatrix} \underbrace{\mathbf{A}}_{k \times k} & \underbrace{\mathbf{B}}_{k \times m} \\ \underbrace{{\mathbf{B}}^{\top}}_{m \times k} & \underbrace{\mathbf{C}}_{m \times m} \end{bmatrix},
\]
that is, \(x_{ij} = a_{ij}\), \(x_{i, k+j} = b_{ij}\), \(x_{k+i, j} = b_{ji}\) and \(x_{k+i, k+j} = c_{ij}\) for indices in range. \(\mathbf{X}\) is symmetric (Definition 3 in Special Matrices and Decompositions):
\[
\begin{aligned}
x_{k+i, j} &= b_{ji} \\
&= x_{j, k+i},
\end{aligned}
\]
and \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{C}\) are symmetric. Split \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \in \mathbb{R}^{k + m}\) as \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}})\), with \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} \in \mathbb{R}^k\) its first \(k\) entries and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} \in \mathbb{R}^m\) the rest. Then
\[
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top} \mathbf{X} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + 2\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top} \mathbf{C} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}},
\tag{2}\]
and if \(\mathbf{A}\) is invertible, with \(\mathbf{S} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathbf{C} - {\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{B}\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{z}}\vphantom{z}} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + \mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}\),
\[
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top} \mathbf{X} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{z}}\vphantom{z}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{z}}\vphantom{z}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top} \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}.
\]










ProofProof






Proof. \(\mathbf{X} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) in blocks. For \(i \le k\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
(\mathbf{X} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})_i
&= \sum_{j=1}^{k+m} x_{ij}\,v_j
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matvec-mult}{\text{Definition~13 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= \sum_{j=1}^{k} x_{ij}\,v_j + \sum_{j=1}^{m} x_{i, k+j}\,v_{k+j}
&& \text{(split the sum at } j = k \text{)}
\\
&= \sum_{j=1}^{k} a_{ij}\,u_j + \sum_{j=1}^{m} b_{ij}\,w_j
&& \text{(the blocks of } \mathbf{X} \text{ and of } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \text{)}
\\
&= (\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}})_i + (\mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}})_i,
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matvec-mult}{\text{Definition~13 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
and in the same way \((\mathbf{X} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})_{k+i} = ({\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}})_i + (\mathbf{C} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}})_i\) for \(i \le m\).
The expansion.
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top} \mathbf{X} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}
&= \sum_{i=1}^{k} u_i\,(\mathbf{X} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})_i + \sum_{i=1}^{m} w_i\,(\mathbf{X} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}})_{k+i}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#def-dot-product}{\text{Definition~8 in Vectors}}\text{, split the sum at } k \text{)}
\\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} \cdot (\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}) + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} \cdot ({\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + \mathbf{C} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}})
&& \text{(the blocks of } \mathbf{X} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} \text{)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top} {\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top} \mathbf{C} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-direct-sums.qmd#thm-dot-linear}{\text{Theorem~5 in Direct Sums and Orthogonal Complements}}\text{)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top} \mathbf{C} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}
&& \text{(} {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top} {\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} = {\mathopen{}\left({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top} {\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top} \text{, which equals } {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} \text{; } 1 \times 1 \text{, }\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-transpose}{\text{Definition~3 in Matrices}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-transpose-product}{\text{Theorem~17 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + 2\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top} \mathbf{C} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}.
&& \text{(combine like terms)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Completing the square. Write \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}\), so \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{z}}\vphantom{z}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}\) and \(\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} = \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}\) (Theorem 5 in Matrices, 2, Theorem 8 in Matrices); \(\mathbf{A}^{-1}\) is symmetric (Corollary 1 in Special Matrices and Decompositions). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{z}}\vphantom{z}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{z}}\vphantom{z}}
&= (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}) \cdot \mathbf{A}\,(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}})
&& \text{(substitute } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{z}}\vphantom{z}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} \text{)}
\\
&= (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}) \cdot (\mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-matmul-distrib}{\text{Theorem~6 in Matrices}}\text{)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-direct-sums.qmd#thm-dot-linear}{\text{Theorem~5 in Direct Sums and Orthogonal Complements}}\text{, both slots)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}
&& \text{(as in the expansion, with } {\mathbf{A}}^{\top} = \mathbf{A} \text{)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + 2\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}
&& \text{(combine like terms)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + 2\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}}^{\top} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}
&& \text{(} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} = \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} \text{, twice)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + 2\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}},
&& \text{(} {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}}^{\top} = {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top} {\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{A}^{-1} \text{, }\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#thm-transpose-product}{\text{Theorem~17 in Matrices}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-special-matrices.qmd#cor-inverse-symmetric}{\text{Corollary~1 in Special Matrices and Decompositions}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
and
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top} \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{C} - {\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{B})\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}
&& \text{(substitute } \mathbf{S} \text{)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top}\,(\mathbf{C} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} - {\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}})
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{, difference rule on the right)}
\\
&= {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top} \mathbf{C} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} - {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}.
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{, difference rule on the left)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Adding the two displays, the \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}\) terms cancel, leaving \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + 2\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}}^{\top} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top} \mathbf{C} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}\), which is \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top} \mathbf{X} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}\) by Equation 2.










ExmExample 22 (The \(2 \times 2\) case)






Example 22 (The \(2 \times 2\) case) For \(\mathbf{X} = \begin{bmatrix} a & b \\ b & c \end{bmatrix}\) (\(k = 1\) and \(m = 1\)) and \(a \ne 0\), the theorem reads
\[
a u^2 + 2buw + cw^2 = a\,\mathopen{}\left(u + \tfrac{b}{a}\,w\right)\mathclose{}^2 + \mathopen{}\left(c - \tfrac{b^2}{a}\right)\mathclose{}\,w^2,
\]
with \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = (u, w)\): the familiar completing of the square. With
\[
\begin{aligned}
a &= c \\
&= 5
\end{aligned}
\]
and \(b = 4\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
5\,(u + 0.8\,w)^2 + 1.8\,w^2 &= 5\,(u^2 + 1.6\,uw + 0.64\,w^2) + 1.8\,w^2 \\
&= 5u^2 + 8uw + 3.2\,w^2 + 1.8\,w^2 \\
&= 5u^2 + 8uw + 5w^2.
\end{aligned}
\]










DefDefinition 7 (Schur complement)






Definition 7 (Schur complement) For a block matrix \(\mathbf{X} = \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{A} & \mathbf{B} \\ {\mathbf{B}}^{\top} & \mathbf{C} \end{bmatrix}\) as in Theorem 15 with \(\mathbf{A}\) invertible, the Schur complement [7] of \(\mathbf{A}\) in \(\mathbf{X}\) is the \(m \times m\) matrix \(\mathbf{S} = \mathbf{C} - {\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{B}\) defined in Theorem 15.










ExmExample 23 (Schur complements of \(2 \times 2\) matrices)






Example 23 (Schur complements of \(2 \times 2\) matrices)  

For \(\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 4 \\ 4 & 5 \end{bmatrix}\) with \(\mathbf{A} = [5]\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{S} &= 5 - 4 \cdot\tfrac{1}{5} \cdot 4 \\
&= \tfrac{25}{5} - \tfrac{16}{5} \\
&= \tfrac{9}{5},
\end{aligned}
\]
that is, \(\mathbf{S} = [\tfrac{9}{5}]\).
For \(\mathbf{D} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 2 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) with \(\mathbf{A} = [1]\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{S} &= [1 - 2 \cdot 1 \cdot 2] \\
&= [-3].
\end{aligned}
\]
For \(\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{bmatrix}\) the top-left block \([0]\) is not invertible, so there is no Schur complement of it.











LemTheorem 16 (Schur complement test)






Theorem 16 (Schur complement test) Let \(\mathbf{X} = \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{A} & \mathbf{B} \\ {\mathbf{B}}^{\top} & \mathbf{C} \end{bmatrix}\) be as in Theorem 15. Then \(\mathbf{X}\) is positive definite (Definition 22 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) exactly when \(\mathbf{A}\) is positive definite and its Schur complement \(\mathbf{S}\) (Definition 7) is positive definite.










ProofProof






Proof. If. \(\mathbf{A}\) is positive definite, so invertible (Theorem 18 in Special Matrices and Decompositions), and \(\mathbf{S}\) is defined. \(\mathbf{X}\) is symmetric. Let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}) \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{z}}\vphantom{z}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} + \mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}\). By Theorem 15, \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top} \mathbf{X} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{z}}\vphantom{z}}}^{\top} \mathbf{A} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{z}}\vphantom{z}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top} \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}\), and both terms are at least \(0\). If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), the second term is positive. If \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), then \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{z}}\vphantom{z}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}\), so the first term is positive. Either way \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top} \mathbf{X} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} &gt; 0\).
Only if. \(\mathbf{A}\) is the top-left \(k \times k\) block of \(\mathbf{X}\), so it is positive definite (Theorem 14), and invertible (Theorem 18 in Special Matrices and Decompositions). \(\mathbf{S}\) is symmetric:
\[
\begin{aligned}
{({\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{B})}^{\top} &= {\mathbf{B}}^{\top}\,{(\mathbf{A}^{-1})}^{\top}\,\mathbf{B} \\
&= {\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{B}
\end{aligned}
\]
(Theorem 17 in Matrices, Definition 3 in Matrices for \({({\mathbf{B}}^{\top})}^{\top} = \mathbf{B}\), Corollary 1 in Special Matrices and Decompositions), and \(\mathbf{C}\) is symmetric, so \(\mathbf{S} = \mathbf{C} - {\mathbf{B}}^{\top} \mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{B}\) is symmetric (the transpose of a difference is the difference of the transposes, entry by entry, Definition 3 in Matrices). For \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), take \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}} = -\mathbf{A}^{-1} \mathbf{B} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}\), so \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{z}}\vphantom{z}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} = (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{u}}\vphantom{u}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}) \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\); then \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}}}^{\top} \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{w}}\vphantom{w}} = {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}}}^{\top} \mathbf{X} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{v}}\vphantom{v}} &gt; 0\) (Theorem 15).










ExmExample 24 (Testing \(2 \times 2\) matrices)






Example 24 (Testing \(2 \times 2\) matrices) A symmetric \(\begin{bmatrix} a & b \\ b & c \end{bmatrix}\) is positive definite exactly when \(a &gt; 0\) and \(c - \tfrac{b^2}{a} &gt; 0\), that is, \(a &gt; 0\) and \(ac &gt; b^2\) (a \(1 \times 1\) matrix \([s]\) is positive definite exactly when \(s &gt; 0\), since \({x}^{\top} s x = s x^2\)).

\(\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 4 \\ 4 & 5 \end{bmatrix}\): \(5 &gt; 0\) and \(\tfrac{9}{5} &gt; 0\) (Example 23), so it is positive definite.
\(\mathbf{D} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 2 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\): \(1 &gt; 0\) but \(\mathbf{S} = -3\), so it is not, as Example 32 in Special Matrices and Decompositions found directly.











LemTheorem 17 (Cholesky factorization)






Theorem 17 (Cholesky factorization) A \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{A}\) is positive definite (Definition 22 in Special Matrices and Decompositions) exactly when \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{L} {\mathbf{L}}^{\top}\) for some lower triangular \(\mathbf{L}\) (Definition 8 in Projections and Linear Systems) with positive diagonal entries. For a positive definite \(\mathbf{A}\) that \(\mathbf{L}\) is unique; it is the Cholesky factor [8] of \(\mathbf{A}\).










ProofProof






Proof. If. \({\mathbf{L}}^{\top}\) is upper triangular with positive diagonal (Definition 3 in Matrices), so \({\mathbf{L}}^{\top} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) only for \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\) (Theorem 17 in Projections and Linear Systems), and the columns of \({\mathbf{L}}^{\top}\) are linearly independent (Theorem 3 in Subspaces and Rank, Definition 1 in Subspaces and Rank). So \(\mathbf{A} = {({\mathbf{L}}^{\top})}^{\top}\,{\mathbf{L}}^{\top}\) (Definition 3 in Matrices) is positive definite (Theorem 10, part 2).
Only if, with uniqueness, by induction on \(p\). For \(p = 1\), \(\mathbf{A} = [a]\) with \(a &gt; 0\) (Theorem 14), and \([c]\,[c] = [a]\) with \(c &gt; 0\) holds exactly for \(c = \sqrt{a}\). For \(p &gt; 1\), assume every \((p - 1) \times (p - 1)\) positive definite matrix has exactly one such factor, and split off the first row and column. \(\mathbf{A}\) is symmetric, so its first row is the transpose of its first column:
\[
\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} a_{11} & {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}}^{\top} \\ \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} & \mathbf{A}_{22} \end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\mathbf{L} = \begin{bmatrix} \ell_{11} & \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_{1 \times (p-1)} \\ \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} & \mathbf{L}_{22} \end{bmatrix},
\qquad
{\mathbf{L}}^{\top} = \begin{bmatrix} \ell_{11} & {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}}^{\top} \\ \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_{(p-1) \times 1} & {\mathbf{L}_{22}}^{\top} \end{bmatrix},
\]
with \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \in \mathbb{R}^{p-1}\), where \(b_i\) and \(g_i\) are entries \((i + 1, 1)\) of \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\mathbf{L}\), and \((\mathbf{A}_{22})_{ij}\) and \((\mathbf{L}_{22})_{ij}\) are their entries \((i + 1, j + 1)\); \({\mathbf{L}}^{\top}\) has this form by Definition 3 in Matrices. A \(p \times p\) matrix \(\mathbf{L}\) is lower triangular with positive diagonal exactly when \(\ell_{11} &gt; 0\), its first row is otherwise zero, and \(\mathbf{L}_{22}\) is lower triangular with positive diagonal; \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}\) can be anything. So choosing \(\mathbf{L}\) means choosing \(\ell_{11} &gt; 0\), \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}\) and such an \(\mathbf{L}_{22}\).
Entry \((i, j)\) of \(\mathbf{L} {\mathbf{L}}^{\top}\) is \(\sum_{r=1}^{p} \ell_{ir}\,\ell_{jr}\), with \(\ell_{ir}\) entry \((i, r)\) of \(\mathbf{L}\) (1, Definition 3 in Matrices). Row \(1\) of \(\mathbf{L}\) is \((\ell_{11}, 0, \ldots, 0)\), and row \(i + 1\) is \((g_i, (\mathbf{L}_{22})_{i1}, \ldots, (\mathbf{L}_{22})_{i,p-1})\). So, for \(i, j = 1, \ldots, p - 1\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
(\mathbf{L} {\mathbf{L}}^{\top})_{11}
&= \ell_{11}\,\ell_{11} + \sum_{r=2}^{p} 0 \cdot 0 \\
&= \ell_{11}^2,
&& \text{(rows 1 and 1)}
\\
(\mathbf{L} {\mathbf{L}}^{\top})_{i+1,1}
&= g_i\,\ell_{11} + \sum_{r=2}^{p} (\mathbf{L}_{22})_{i,r-1} \cdot 0 \\
&= \ell_{11}\,g_i,
&& \text{(rows } i + 1 \text{ and } 1 \text{)}
\\
(\mathbf{L} {\mathbf{L}}^{\top})_{i+1,j+1}
&= g_i\,g_j + \sum_{r=2}^{p} (\mathbf{L}_{22})_{i,r-1}\,(\mathbf{L}_{22})_{j,r-1}
&& \text{(rows } i + 1 \text{ and } j + 1 \text{)}
\\
&= (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}}^{\top})_{ij} + (\mathbf{L}_{22} {\mathbf{L}_{22}}^{\top})_{ij}.
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-mult}{\text{Definition~7 in Matrices}}\text{, with } s = r - 1 \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Entry \((1, j + 1)\) is \(\ell_{11}\,g_j\) by the same computation with the roles swapped, the transpose of the \((2, 1)\) block; it matches \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}}^{\top}\) exactly when the \((2, 1)\) block matches \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\), which is where the symmetry of \(\mathbf{A}\) is used. So \(\mathbf{L} {\mathbf{L}}^{\top} = \mathbf{A}\) says exactly
\[
\ell_{11}^2 = a_{11}, \qquad
\ell_{11}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}, \qquad
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}}^{\top} + \mathbf{L}_{22} {\mathbf{L}_{22}}^{\top} = \mathbf{A}_{22}.
\]
\(a_{11} &gt; 0\) (Theorem 14), so the first equation with \(\ell_{11} &gt; 0\) forces \(\ell_{11} = \sqrt{a_{11}}\); the second then forces \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} = \tfrac{1}{\ell_{11}}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\); and the third becomes
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{L}_{22} {\mathbf{L}_{22}}^{\top}
&= \mathbf{A}_{22} - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}}^{\top}
&& \text{(subtract } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}}^{\top} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}_{22} - \mathopen{}\left(\tfrac{1}{\ell_{11}}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\right)\mathclose{} {\mathopen{}\left(\tfrac{1}{\ell_{11}}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}
&& \text{(substitute } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}_{22} - \tfrac{1}{\ell_{11}^2}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}}^{\top}
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-matrices.qmd#def-matrix-transpose}{\text{Definition~3 in Matrices}}\text{, }\href{linear-algebra-projections.qmd#thm-scalar-matmul}{\text{Theorem~7 in Projections and Linear Systems}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{A}_{22} - \tfrac{1}{a_{11}}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}}^{\top}.
&& \text{(} \ell_{11}^2 = a_{11} \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Here \([a_{11}]\) is invertible with inverse \([1 / a_{11}]\), so with \(\mathbf{B} = {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}}^{\top}\) in Definition 7,
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathbf{B}}^{\top}\,[a_{11}]^{-1} \mathbf{B} &= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}\,\tfrac{1}{a_{11}}\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}}^{\top} \\
&= \tfrac{1}{a_{11}}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}} {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{b}}\vphantom{b}}}^{\top}
\end{aligned}
\]
(Theorem 7 in Projections and Linear Systems), and the right side is the Schur complement of \([a_{11}]\) in \(\mathbf{A}\). It is positive definite by Theorem 16 (with \(k = 1\); \(\mathbf{A}_{22}\) is symmetric because \(\mathbf{A}\) is). By the induction hypothesis it has exactly one factor \(\mathbf{L}_{22}\) of the required kind. So \(\mathbf{L}\) exists and is unique.










ExmExample 25 (Two Cholesky factors)






Example 25 (Two Cholesky factors)  

\(\mathbf{A} = \begin{bmatrix} 4 & 2 \\ 2 & 5 \end{bmatrix}\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\ell_{11} &= \sqrt{4} \\
&= 2,
\end{aligned}
\]
\[
\begin{aligned}
\ell_{21} &= 2/2 \\
&= 1,
\end{aligned}
\]
and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\ell_{22}^2 &= 5 - 1^2 \\
&= 4,
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(\ell_{22} = 2\): \(\mathbf{L} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\ 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}\), and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{L} {\mathbf{L}}^{\top} &= \begin{bmatrix} 2 \cdot 2 & 2 \cdot 1 \\ 1 \cdot 2 & 1 \cdot 1 + 2 \cdot 2 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \mathbf{A}.
\end{aligned}
\]
\(\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 4 \\ 4 & 5 \end{bmatrix}\): \(\ell_{11} = \sqrt{5}\), \(\ell_{21} = 4/\sqrt{5}\), and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\ell_{22}^2 &= 5 - \tfrac{16}{5} \\
&= \tfrac{9}{5},
\end{aligned}
\]
the Schur complement of Example 23, so \(\ell_{22} = \tfrac{3}{\sqrt{5}}\).
For \(\mathbf{D} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 2 & 1 \end{bmatrix}\) the method breaks down:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\ell_{22}^2 &= 1 - 4 \\
&= -3
\end{aligned}
\]
has no real solution, matching the failure of the Schur complement test (Example 24).

2 Strong induction






DefDefinition 3 (Proof by strong induction)






Definition 3 (Proof by strong induction) Let \(n_0\) be an integer, and let \(P(n)\) be a predicate about the integers \(n \ge n_0\). A proof by strong induction [3] on \(n\) shows that \(P(n)\) holds for every integer \(n \ge n_0\) by showing one thing: for every integer \(n \ge n_0\), if \(P(k)\) holds for every integer \(k\) with \(n_0 \le k &lt; n\), then \(P(n)\) holds.
The assumption that \(P(k)\) holds for every integer \(k\) with \(n_0 \le k &lt; n\) is the strong induction hypothesis.
For \(n = n_0\) no integer \(k\) satisfies \(n_0 \le k &lt; n\), so the strong induction hypothesis is vacuously true, and the proof for \(n = n_0\) is a base case. When the proof for \(n\) needs particular earlier cases that do not exist for the first few \(n\), those few cases are shown separately as further base cases.










ExmExample 3 (Every term of the Fibonacci sequence is less than a power of 2)






Example 3 (Every term of the Fibonacci sequence is less than a power of 2) Let \(F_1, F_2, \ldots\) be the Fibonacci sequence. To prove \(F_n &lt; 2^n\) for every \(n \in \mathbb{N}\), let \(P(n)\) be the inequality \(F_n &lt; 2^n\) and use Definition 3 with \(n_0 = 1\).
Base cases, \(n = 1\) and \(n = 2\). The step below uses the two entries \(F_{n-1}\) and \(F_{n-2}\) before \(F_n\), which do not exist for \(n = 1\) and \(n = 2\), so these two cases are shown directly:
\[
\begin{aligned}
F_1 &= 1 &lt; 2 = 2^1
&& \text{(} 1 &lt; 2 \text{)}
\\
F_2 &= 1 &lt; 4 = 2^2.
&& \text{(} 1 &lt; 4 \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Step of the strong induction, \(n \ge 3\). Let \(n \ge 3\), and suppose \(P(k)\) holds for every integer \(k\) with \(1 \le k &lt; n\). Then \(k = n - 1\) and \(k = n - 2\) both satisfy \(1 \le k &lt; n\), so \(F_{n-1} &lt; 2^{n-1}\) and \(F_{n-2} &lt; 2^{n-2}\). Therefore
\[
\begin{aligned}
F_n
&= F_{n-1} + F_{n-2}
&& \text{(definition of } F_n \text{, since } n \ge 3 \text{)}
\\
&&lt; 2^{n-1} + 2^{n-2}
&& \text{(strong induction hypothesis, twice)}
\\
&&lt; 2^{n-1} + 2^{n-1}
&& \text{(} 2^{n-2} &lt; 2^{n-1} \text{)}
\\
&= 2 \cdot 2^{n-1}
&& \text{(} a + a = 2a \text{)}
\\
&= 2^n,
&& \text{(} 2 \cdot 2^{n-1} = 2^n \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
which is \(P(n)\). By Definition 3, \(F_n &lt; 2^n\) for every \(n \in \mathbb{N}\).

4 Percent
The word “percent” comes from the Latin “per centum”; “centum” is Latin for 100, so “percent” means “per hundred” [7].






DefDefinition 12 (Percent)






Definition 12 (Percent) For a real number \(x\), “\(x\) percent”, written \(x\%\), means \(x / 100\) [7]:
\[
x\% \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\frac{x}{100}
\]
The percent sign “%” is a shorthand for “\(/100\)”.










ExmExample 7 (Ten percent is one tenth)






Example 7 (Ten percent is one tenth) Contrary to what you may have learned previously, the equality \(10\% = 0.1\) is true and correct, just as \(10 \text{kg} = 10,000 \text{g}\) is true and correct:
\[
\begin{aligned}
10\% &= \frac{10}{100}
&& \text{(definition of percent)}
\\ &= 0.1
&& \text{(divide)}
\end{aligned}
\]




You are welcome to switch between decimal and percent notation freely; just make sure you execute it correctly.

2 Affine and linear functions of one variable






DefDefinition 11 (Affine function of one variable)






Definition 11 (Affine function of one variable) A function \(f: \mathbb{R}\to \mathbb{R}\) is affine [5] if there are numbers \(m\) and \(b\) such that
\[f(x) = m x + b \quad \text{for all } x \in \mathbb{R}\]










DefDefinition 12 (Slope)






Definition 12 (Slope) In the formula \(f(x) = m x + b\) for an affine function \(f\) (Definition 11), \(m\) is called the slope of \(f\) [6].










DefDefinition 13 (Intercept)






Definition 13 (Intercept) In the formula \(f(x) = m x + b\) for an affine function \(f\) (Definition 11), \(b\) is called the intercept of \(f\).










DefDefinition 14 (Linear function of one variable)






Definition 14 (Linear function of one variable) A function \(f: \mathbb{R}\to \mathbb{R}\) is linear [7] if it is affine (Definition 11) with intercept \(0\); that is, if there is a number \(m\) such that
\[f(x) = m x \quad \text{for all } x \in \mathbb{R}\]










LemTheorem 1 (Intercept and slope of an affine function)






Theorem 1 (Intercept and slope of an affine function) Let \(f(x) = m x + b\) be an affine function (Definition 11).

The intercept is the value at zero: \(f(0) = b\).
The slope is the change in \(f\) per unit change in \(x\): for any \(x_1 \neq x_2\), \[\frac{f(x_2) - f(x_1)}{x_2 - x_1} = m\]

So \(f\) determines its slope and intercept: if also \(f(x) = m' x + b'\) for all \(x\), then \(m' = m\) and \(b' = b\).











ProofProof






Proof. Part 1.
\[
\begin{aligned}
f(0)
&= m \cdot 0 + b
&& \text{(}\href{#def-affine-function}{\text{Definition~11}}\text{)}
\\&= 0 + b
&& \text{(any number times } 0 \text{ is } 0 \text{)}
\\&= b + 0
&& \text{(}\href{algebra-sums.qmd#thm-sum-symmetric}{\text{Theorem~5 in Convexity, Infimum and Sums}}\text{)}
\\&= b
&& \text{(}\href{algebra-sums.qmd#thm-add-ident}{\text{Theorem~4 in Convexity, Infimum and Sums}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Part 2. First the numerator:
\[
\begin{aligned}
f(x_2) - f(x_1)
&= (m x_2 + b) - (m x_1 + b)
&& \text{(}\href{#def-affine-function}{\text{Definition~11}}\text{)}
\\&= m x_2 + b - m x_1 - b
&& \text{(subtracting a sum subtracts each term)}
\\&= m x_2 - m x_1 + b - b
&& \text{(}\href{algebra-sums.qmd#thm-sum-symmetric}{\text{Theorem~5 in Convexity, Infimum and Sums}}\text{, swapping } b \text{ and } {-m x_1} \text{)}
\\&= (m x_2 - m x_1) + (b - b)
&& \text{(}\href{algebra-sums.qmd#thm-sum-assoc}{\text{Theorem~6 in Convexity, Infimum and Sums}}\text{)}
\\&= (m x_2 - m x_1) + 0
&& (b - b = 0)
\\&= m x_2 - m x_1
&& \text{(}\href{algebra-sums.qmd#thm-add-ident}{\text{Theorem~4 in Convexity, Infimum and Sums}}\text{)}
\\&= m x_2 + m (-x_1)
&& (-(m x_1) = m (-x_1) \text{, by }\href{algebra-basics.qmd#thm-negative-one}{\text{Theorem~6 in Equalities, Inequalities and Minimizers}}\text{ and }\href{algebra-sums.qmd#thm-prod-assoc}{\text{Theorem~9 in Convexity, Infimum and Sums}}\text{)}
\\&= m (x_2 + (-x_1))
&& \text{(}\href{algebra-sums.qmd#thm-mult-distr}{\text{Theorem~11 in Convexity, Infimum and Sums}}\text{, read right to left)}
\\&= m (x_2 - x_1)
&& \text{(adding a negative is subtracting)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Then, writing \(d = x_2 - x_1\), which is not \(0\) because \(x_1 \neq x_2\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{f(x_2) - f(x_1)}{x_2 - x_1}
&= \frac{m d}{d}
&& \text{(the numerator above)}
\\&= (m d) \cdot\frac{1}{d}
&& \text{(}\href{algebra-sums.qmd#thm-prod-div}{\text{Theorem~10 in Convexity, Infimum and Sums}}\text{)}
\\&= m \cdot\mathopen{}\left(d \cdot\frac{1}{d}\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(}\href{algebra-sums.qmd#thm-prod-assoc}{\text{Theorem~9 in Convexity, Infimum and Sums}}\text{)}
\\&= m \cdot\frac{d}{d}
&& \text{(}\href{algebra-sums.qmd#thm-prod-div}{\text{Theorem~10 in Convexity, Infimum and Sums}}\text{)}
\\&= m \cdot 1
&& \text{(a nonzero number divided by itself is } 1 \text{)}
\\&= m
&& \text{(}\href{algebra-sums.qmd#thm-mult-one}{\text{Theorem~7 in Convexity, Infimum and Sums}}\text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Part 3. Part 1, applied to each formula, gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
b &= f(0) \\
&= b'.
\end{aligned}
\]
Part 2 with \(x_1 = 0\) and \(x_2 = 1\), applied to each formula, gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
m &= f(1) - f(0) \\
&= m'.
\end{aligned}
\]










ExmExample 8 (An affine function, and a linear one)






Example 8 (An affine function, and a linear one) \(f(x) = 2x + 3\) is affine with slope \(2\) and intercept \(3\). By Theorem 1, \(f(0) = 3\), and each unit step in \(x\) raises \(f\) by \(2\): for example, \(f(1) = 5\) and \(f(2) = 7\). Its intercept is not \(0\), so it is not linear.
\(g(x) = -\tfrac{1}{2} x\) is affine with intercept \(0\), so it is linear: \(g(0) = 0\), and each unit step in \(x\) lowers \(g\) by \(\tfrac{1}{2}\).










RemarkRemark 2 (Elementary algebra calls \(m x + b\) “linear”)






Remark 2 (Elementary algebra calls \(m x + b\) “linear”). Elementary algebra usually calls \(f(x) = m x + b\) a linear function, because its graph is a straight line. This site calls that an affine function and keeps “linear” for the case \(b = 0\), because that is the sense used in linear algebra, where a linear map must send \(0\) to \(0\) (theorem).

3 The fundamental theorem of linear algebra






LemTheorem 10 (Fundamental theorem of linear algebra)






Theorem 10 (Fundamental theorem of linear algebra) Let \(\mathbf{A}\) be an \(m \times n\) matrix with \(\operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{A}) = r\). Then

\(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})^\perp = \mathcal{N}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top})\), and \(\mathbb{R}^m = \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A}) \oplus \mathcal{N}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top})\);
\(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A}) = \mathcal{N}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top})^\perp\);
\(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A})^\perp = \mathcal{C}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top})\), and \(\mathbb{R}^n = \mathcal{C}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}) \oplus \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A})\);
\[
\begin{aligned}
\dim\mathopen{}\left(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})\right)\mathclose{} &= \dim\mathopen{}\left(\mathcal{C}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top})\right)\mathclose{} \\
&= r,
\end{aligned}
\]
\(\dim\mathopen{}\left(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A})\right)\mathclose{} = n - r\), and \(\dim\mathopen{}\left(\mathcal{N}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top})\right)\mathclose{} = m - r\).











ProofProof






Proof. Part 1. The first equation is Theorem 7. \(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})\) is a subspace of \(\mathbb{R}^m\) (Theorem 1 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity), so
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{R}^m
&= \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A}) \oplus \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})^\perp
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-orthogonal-direct-sum}{\text{Theorem~8}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A}) \oplus \mathcal{N}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}).
&& \text{(first equation)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Part 2.
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})
&= \mathopen{}\left(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})^\perp\right)\mathclose{}^\perp
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-double-complement}{\text{Theorem~9}}\text{)}
\\
&= \mathcal{N}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top})^\perp.
&& \text{(part 1)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Part 3. Apply parts 2 and 1 to the \(n \times m\) matrix \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\), using \({({\mathbf{A}}^{\top})}^{\top} = \mathbf{A}\): part 2 gives \(\mathcal{C}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}) = \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A})^\perp\), and part 1 gives \(\mathbb{R}^n = \mathcal{C}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}) \oplus \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A})\).
Part 4. \(\dim\mathopen{}\left(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A})\right)\mathclose{} = r\) by Theorem 6 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity. \(\dim\mathopen{}\left(\mathcal{C}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top})\right)\mathclose{} = \operatorname{rank}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top})\) by Theorem 6 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity, and that is \(r\) by Theorem 9 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity. \(\mathbf{A}\) has \(n\) columns, so \(\dim\mathopen{}\left(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A})\right)\mathclose{} = n - r\) (Definition 7 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity, Theorem 7 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity). \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\) has \(m\) columns and rank \(r\), so \(\dim\mathopen{}\left(\mathcal{N}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top})\right)\mathclose{} = m - r\) in the same way.










ExmExample 18 (The four subspaces of the matrix in Example 1 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity)






Example 18 (The four subspaces of the matrix in Example 1 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity) For \(\mathbf{A}\) in Example 1 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity, \(m = 2\), \(n = 3\) and \(r = 1\) (Example 10 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity).

In \(\mathbb{R}^3\): the row space \(\mathcal{C}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}) = \operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{(1, -2, -2)\right\}\mathclose{}\)

has dimension \(1 = r\), and \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A}) = \operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{(2, 1, 0), (2, 0, 1)\right\}\mathclose{}\) has dimension \(2 = n - r\) (Example 11 in Column Space, Null Space and Rank-Nullity). The spanning vectors are orthogonal:

\[
\begin{aligned}
(1, -2, -2) \cdot (2, 1, 0) &= 2 - 2 + 0 \\
&= 0
\end{aligned}
\]
and
\[
\begin{aligned}
(1, -2, -2) \cdot (2, 0, 1) &= 2 + 0 - 2 \\
&= 0.
\end{aligned}
\]
In \(\mathbb{R}^2\): \(\mathcal{C}(\mathbf{A}) = \operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{(1, 3)\right\}\mathclose{}\) has dimension \(1 = r\), and \(\mathcal{N}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}) = \operatorname{span}\mathopen{}\left\{(-3, 1)\right\}\mathclose{}\) has dimension \(1 = m - r\) (Example 14).

By part 3, \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} = (1, 0, 0)\) splits uniquely into a row-space part \(t\,(1, -2, -2)\) and a null-space part. The null-space part \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{y}}\vphantom{y}} - t\,(1, -2, -2)\) lies in \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A})\) (part 3), and \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{A}) = \mathcal{C}({\mathbf{A}}^{\top})^\perp\) (Theorem 7 applied to \({\mathbf{A}}^{\top}\), with \({({\mathbf{A}}^{\top})}^{\top} = \mathbf{A}\)), so it must be orthogonal to \((1, -2, -2)\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
0
&= (1, -2, -2) \cdot \mathopen{}\left((1, 0, 0) - t\,(1, -2, -2)\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(orthogonality)}
\\
&= (1, -2, -2) \cdot \mathopen{}\left(1 \cdot(1, 0, 0) + (-t)\,(1, -2, -2)\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(write the difference as a linear combination)}
\\
&= 1 \cdot\mathopen{}\left((1, -2, -2) \cdot (1, 0, 0)\right)\mathclose{} + (-t)\,\mathopen{}\left((1, -2, -2) \cdot (1, -2, -2)\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(}\href{#thm-dot-linear}{\text{Theorem~5}}\text{)}
\\
&= 1 \cdot 1 + (-t) \cdot 9
&& \text{(}\href{linear-algebra-vectors.qmd#def-dot-product}{\text{Definition~8 in Vectors}}\text{)}
\\
&= 1 - 9t,
&& \text{(arithmetic)}
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(t = \frac{1}{9}\). The row-space part is \(\mathopen{}\left(\frac{1}{9}, -\frac{2}{9}, -\frac{2}{9}\right)\mathclose{}\) and the null-space part is \(\mathopen{}\left(\frac{8}{9}, \frac{2}{9}, \frac{2}{9}\right)\mathclose{}\); as a check, row 1 of \(\mathbf{A}\) gives \(\frac{8}{9} - 2 \cdot\frac{2}{9} - 2 \cdot\frac{2}{9} = 0\), and row 2 is \(3\) times row 1, so \(\mathbf{A}\) sends the null-space part to \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_2\).
These four statements are the fundamental theorem of linear algebra [6].

5 Newton’s method and gradient descent






NoteSource





This section is adapted from the Newton-Raphson and gradient descent parts of Zhou [3], used under the MIT License (see the license text in Linear Algebra). The source motivates the Newton step by minimizing a quadratic approximation; here that step is proved to be the exact minimizer of the quadratic model. In the source’s zigzag example the starting value of the objective is \(\tfrac{1}{2}(b^2 + b)\), not \(\tfrac{1}{2}(1 + b^2)\) as the source writes; the ratio of successive values is unaffected. In the same example the source’s formula for \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(1)}\) has \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(1)}\) on its right side where \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)}\) is meant. These parts of the source are not part of this section:

the quadratic convergence theorem for Newton’s method in general, and its remedies for instability (a positive definite substitute for the Hessian, and shortening the Newton step by a search along its direction)
its remark that Hessians are expensive to compute
the convergence-rate theorem for strongly convex functions
gradient descent with momentum, and its optimal parameters
Nesterov acceleration, and stochastic gradient descent
its code and animations










DefDefinition 23 (Iterative method)






Definition 23 (Iterative method) An iterative method [14] starts from a point \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)}\) and computes \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t+1)}\) from \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)}\) by a fixed rule, for \(t = 0, 1, 2, \ldots\).










DefDefinition 24 (Iterate)






Definition 24 (Iterate) In an iterative method (Definition 23), the points \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)}\) are its iterates.










DefDefinition 25 (Error of an iterate)






Definition 25 (Error of an iterate) For a point \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^*\) that an iterative method (Definition 23) is meant to reach, \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)} - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^*\right\rVert\mathclose{}\) is the error of the iterate \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)}\) (Definition 24).










DefDefinition 26 (Convergence of a sequence of vectors)






Definition 26 (Convergence of a sequence of vectors) A sequence \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(1)}, \ldots\) in \(\mathbb{R}^p\) converges to \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^* \in \mathbb{R}^p\) if the sequence of distances \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)} - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^*\right\rVert\mathclose{}\) converges to \(0\). For \(p = 1\), \(\mathopen{}\left\lVert x^{(t)} - x^*\right\rVert\mathclose{} = \mathopen{}\left|x^{(t)} - x^*\right|\mathclose{}\), and this is the convergence of a sequence of numbers to \(x^*\).










ExmExample 38 (Halving a vector)






Example 38 (Halving a vector) The iterative method \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t+1)} = \tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)}\) from \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)} = {(4, 2)}^{\top}\) has iterates \({(4, 2)}^{\top}, {(2, 1)}^{\top}, {(1, 0.5)}^{\top}, \ldots\), that is, \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)} = 2^{-t}\,{(4, 2)}^{\top}\). With \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^* = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\), the error of \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)}\) is
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathopen{}\left\lVert 2^{-t}\,{(4, 2)}^{\top} - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
&= 2^{-t}\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert{(4, 2)}^{\top}\right\rVert\mathclose{}
&& \text{(norm of a multiple)}
\\
&= 2^{-t} \sqrt{20},
&& \text{(} 4^2 + 2^2 = 20 \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
which converges to \(0\), so the iterates converge to \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}\).










DefDefinition 27 (Newton’s method for minimization)






Definition 27 (Newton’s method for minimization) Let \(f : \mathbb{R}^p \to \mathbb{R}\) have first and second partial derivatives, and let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)}\) be a starting point. Newton’s method [15] computes, for \(t = 0, 1, 2, \ldots\), as long as \(\mathbf{H}_f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)})\) is invertible,
\[
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t+1)} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)} - \mathopen{}\left[\mathbf{H}_f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)})\right]\mathclose{}^{-1} \frac{\partial}{\partial \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)}).
\]










DefDefinition 28 (Newton step)






Definition 28 (Newton step) In Newton’s method (Definition 27), the vector \(-\mathopen{}\left[\mathbf{H}_f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)})\right]\mathclose{}^{-1} \frac{\partial}{\partial \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)})\) is the Newton step at \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)}\).










ExmExample 39 (Newton’s method for a cubic)






Example 39 (Newton’s method for a cubic) Let \(f(x) = \tfrac{1}{3} x^3 - 4x\), so \(f'(x) = x^2 - 4\) and \(f''(x) = 2x\). For \(x^{(t)} \ne 0\), one Newton step gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
x^{(t+1)}
&= x^{(t)} - \frac{(x^{(t)})^2 - 4}{2 x^{(t)}}
&& \text{(Newton's method with } p = 1 \text{)}
\\
&= \frac{2 (x^{(t)})^2 - (x^{(t)})^2 + 4}{2 x^{(t)}}
&& \text{(common denominator)}
\\
&= \frac{1}{2} \mathopen{}\left(x^{(t)} + \frac{4}{x^{(t)}}\right)\mathclose{}.
&& \text{(simplify)}
\end{aligned}
\]
From \(x^{(0)} = 2.5\):

\[
\begin{aligned}
x^{(1)} &= \tfrac{1}{2} (2.5 + 1.6) \\
&= 2.05;
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
\begin{aligned}
x^{(2)} &= \tfrac{1}{2} (2.05 + 1.951219\ldots) \\
&= 2.000610
\end{aligned}
\]
(to six decimals);

\(x^{(3)} = 2.0000001\) (to seven decimals).

The iterates (Definition 24) approach \(2\), the local minimizer of \(f\) (a stationary point, since \(f'(2) = 0\), with \(f''(2) = 4 &gt; 0\); Theorem 6).










DefDefinition 29 (Quadratic model, second-order Taylor polynomial)






Definition 29 (Quadratic model, second-order Taylor polynomial) Let \(f\) be as in Definition 27 and let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) be a point, with \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})\) and \(\mathbf{H} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathbf{H}_f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})\). The quadratic model of \(f\) at \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) is the function of a step \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}\in \mathbb{R}^p\)
\[
q(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}) \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}} + \tfrac{1}{2}\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}}^{\top} \mathbf{H} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}},
\]
also called the second-order Taylor polynomial of \(f\) at \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\).










ExmExample 40 (The model at \(x = 2.5\))






Example 40 (The model at \(x = 2.5\)) For \(f(x) = \tfrac{1}{3} x^3 - 4x\) at \(x = 2.5\) (Example 39),
\[
\begin{aligned}
g &= 2.5^2 - 4 \\
&= 2.25
\end{aligned}
\]
and
\[
\begin{aligned}
H &= [2 \cdot 2.5] \\
&= [5],
\end{aligned}
\]
so \(q(\delta) = f(2.5) + 2.25\,\delta+ 2.5\,\delta^2\). For \(f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) = \tfrac{1}{2} (x_1^2 + 3 x_2^2)\), whose Hessian is \(\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 3 \end{bmatrix}\) at every point, the model at any \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) equals \(f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}+ \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}})\) exactly (Example 9 with \(\mathbf{S} = \begin{bmatrix} 1/2 & 0 \\ 0 & 3/2 \end{bmatrix}\)).










RemarkRemark 3 (The quadratic model and Taylor’s theorem)






Remark 3 (The quadratic model and Taylor’s theorem). When the second partial derivatives of \(f\) are continuous, Theorem 5 says that \(f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}+ \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}})\) equals the expression for \(q(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}})\) in Definition 29 with the Hessian evaluated at a point between \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}+ \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}\).










LemTheorem 13 (The Newton step minimizes the quadratic model)






Theorem 13 (The Newton step minimizes the quadratic model) Let \(q\) be the quadratic model of \(f\) at \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) (Definition 29), with \(\mathbf{H} = \mathbf{H}_f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})\) positive definite. Its unique minimizer is the Newton step \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^* = -\mathbf{H}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}\).










ProofProof






Proof. \(\mathbf{H}\) is symmetric, by the definition of positive definite, and invertible (positive definite inverse), so \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^*\) exists and \(\mathbf{H} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^* = -\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}\). For any \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} \in \mathbb{R}^p\), first
\[
\begin{aligned}
{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}}^{\top} \mathbf{H} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^*
&= -\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}
&& \text{(} \mathbf{H} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^* = -\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \text{)}
\\
&= -\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}},
&& \text{(a } 1 \times 1 \text{ matrix equals its transpose)}
\end{aligned}
\]
and \({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^*{}}^{\top} \mathbf{H} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} = {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}}^{\top} \mathbf{H} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^*\) in the same way, using \({\mathbf{H}}^{\top} = \mathbf{H}\). Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
q(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^* + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}})
&= f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \cdot (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^* + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}) +\tfrac{1}{2}\,{(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^* + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}})}^{\top} \mathbf{H} (\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^* + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}})
&& \text{(definition of } q \text{)}
\\
&= f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^* + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}
+ \tfrac{1}{2} \mathopen{}\left({\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^*{}}^{\top} \mathbf{H} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^* + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^*{}}^{\top} \mathbf{H} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}}^{\top} \mathbf{H} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^* + {\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}}^{\top} \mathbf{H} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}\right)\mathclose{}
&& \text{(distribute)}
\\
&= f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^* + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}
+ \tfrac{1}{2}\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^*{}}^{\top} \mathbf{H} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^* - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} + \tfrac{1}{2}\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}}^{\top} \mathbf{H} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}
&& \text{(both cross terms equal } -\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} \text{)}
\\
&= f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) + \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^* + \tfrac{1}{2}\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^*{}}^{\top} \mathbf{H} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^* + \tfrac{1}{2}\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}}^{\top} \mathbf{H} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}
&& \text{(cancel } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} \text{)}
\\
&= q(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^*) + \tfrac{1}{2}\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}}^{\top} \mathbf{H} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}},
&& \text{(definition of } q \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
which is greater than \(q(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{\delta}}\vphantom{\delta}}^*)\) for every \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_{p \times 1}\), since \(\mathbf{H}\) is positive definite.










ExmExample 41 (The Newton step at \(x = 2.5\))






Example 41 (The Newton step at \(x = 2.5\)) For the model \(q(\delta) = f(2.5) + 2.25\,\delta+ 2.5\,\delta^2\) of Example 40, \(H = [5]\) is positive definite, so by Theorem 13 its minimizer is
\[
\begin{aligned}
\delta^* &= -H^{-1} g \\
&= -2.25 / 5 \\
&= -0.45.
\end{aligned}
\]
Setting the derivative \(2.25 + 5 \delta\) to \(0\) gives the same \(\delta^*\), and
\[
\begin{aligned}
2.5 - 0.45 &= 2.05 \\
&= x^{(1)}
\end{aligned}
\]
(Example 39).










DefDefinition 30 (Quadratic convergence)






Definition 30 (Quadratic convergence) A sequence \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)}, \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(1)}, \ldots\) in \(\mathbb{R}^p\) that converges (Definition 26) to \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^*\) converges quadratically [16] if there is a constant \(C\) with
\[
\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t+1)} - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^*\right\rVert\mathclose{} \le C\,\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)} - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^*\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
\qquad \text{for every } t.
\]










ExmExample 42 (Halving the error is not quadratic convergence)






Example 42 (Halving the error is not quadratic convergence) \(x^{(t)} = 2^{-t}\) converges to \(0\), but it does not converge quadratically: the bound would need \(2^{-(t+1)} \le C\,(2^{-t})^2 = C\,2^{-2t}\), that is, \(2^{t - 1} \le C\), which fails once \(t &gt; 1 + \log_2 C\). Each step only halves the error.










ExmExample 43 (The error squares at each step)






Example 43 (The error squares at each step) For the iteration \(x^{(t+1)} = \tfrac{1}{2} (x^{(t)} + 4 / x^{(t)})\) of Example 39,
\[
\begin{aligned}
x^{(t+1)} - 2
&= \frac{1}{2} \mathopen{}\left(x^{(t)} + \frac{4}{x^{(t)}}\right)\mathclose{} - 2
&& \text{(the iteration)}
\\
&= \frac{(x^{(t)})^2 + 4 - 4 x^{(t)}}{2 x^{(t)}}
&& \text{(common denominator)}
\\
&= \frac{(x^{(t)} - 2)^2}{2 x^{(t)}}.
&& \text{(the numerator is a perfect square)}
\end{aligned}
\]
So near \(2\) the error is roughly squared and divided by \(4\) at each step: the errors are \(0.05\), then \(0.00061\), then \(0.000000093\), and \(0.05^2 / (2 \cdot 2.05) \approx 0.00061\). The iterates converge quadratically (Definition 30): \(C = \tfrac{1}{2}\) works for every \(x^{(t)} \ge 1\), since then \(\tfrac{1}{2 x^{(t)}} \le \tfrac{1}{2}\), and every iterate from \(2.5\) is at least \(2\), since the identity makes \(x^{(t+1)} - 2 \ge 0\) whenever \(x^{(t)} &gt; 0\). Zhou [3] states such a bound for Newton’s method in general; it holds from starting points close enough to a minimizer where the Hessian is positive definite, when near that minimizer the Hessian changes by at most a constant times the distance moved, which these notes do not prove.










ExmExample 44 (Newton’s method can find a maximum)






Example 44 (Newton’s method can find a maximum) From \(x^{(0)} = -2.5\) the same iteration gives \(-2.05\), \(-2.000610\), \(-2.0000001\), approaching \(-2\). But \(-2\) is not a minimizer of \(f\): it is a stationary point of \(-f\), whose second derivative there is \(-f''(-2) = 4 &gt; 0\), so it is a strict local minimizer of \(-f\) (Theorem 6), that is, \(f\) is strictly larger at \(-2\) than at all nearby points. Newton’s method looks only for a stationary point, and here the Hessian \(f''(x) = 2x\) is negative along the way, so the quadratic model has no minimizer (with \(H &lt; 0\), \(q(\delta) = f + g\,\delta+ \tfrac{1}{2} H \delta^2\) decreases without bound) and Theorem 13 does not apply: the positive definite hypothesis there is needed.










LemTheorem 14 (Newton’s method minimizes a quadratic in one step)






Theorem 14 (Newton’s method minimizes a quadratic in one step) Let \(\mathbf{S}\) be a \(p \times p\) positive definite matrix, \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}} \in \mathbb{R}^p\), and \(f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) = \tfrac{1}{2}\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}} - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\). From any \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)}\), Newton’s method (Definition 27) gives \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(1)} = \mathbf{S}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}}\), the unique minimizer of \(f\).










ProofProof






Proof. Partial derivatives of a sum or of a constant multiple are the sum or multiple of the partial derivatives, so by derivative of a quadratic form for \(\tfrac{1}{2}\,{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}}^{\top} \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) and derivative of a linear combination for \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) &= \tfrac{1}{2} (2 \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}} \\
&= \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}- \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}};
\end{aligned}
\]
by Hessian of a quadratic form with \(\tfrac{1}{2} \mathbf{S}\) in place of \(\mathbf{S}\), and because the gradient \(-\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}}\) of the linear term is constant (constant with respect to a vector), \(\mathbf{H}_f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) = \mathbf{S}\), which is invertible (positive definite inverse). So
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(1)}
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)} - \mathbf{S}^{-1} (\mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)} - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}})
&& \text{(definition of Newton's method)}
\\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)} - \mathbf{S}^{-1} \mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)} + \mathbf{S}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}}
&& \text{(distribute)}
\\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)} - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)} + \mathbf{S}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}}
&& \text{(} \mathbf{S}^{-1} \mathbf{S} = \mathbf{I}_p \text{)}
\\
&= \mathbf{S}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}}.
&& \text{(cancel)}
\end{aligned}
\]
The gradient at \(\mathbf{S}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}}\) is \(\mathbf{S} \mathbf{S}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}} - \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_{p \times 1}\), and \(f\) is strictly convex (Theorem 11, part 2), so \(\mathbf{S}^{-1} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}}\) is a global minimizer (Corollary 1). It is the only one: a strictly convex function has at most one global minimizer, since the midpoint of two distinct global minimizers, which have equal values, would have a strictly smaller value (Definition 9 with \(t = \tfrac{1}{2}\)).










ExmExample 45 (One step to the minimizer)






Example 45 (One step to the minimizer) Let \(f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) = \tfrac{1}{2} (x_1^2 + 3 x_2^2)\), so \(\mathbf{S} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 3 \end{bmatrix}\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{c}}\vphantom{c}} = \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_{2 \times 1}\). From \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)} = {(3, 1)}^{\top}\) the gradient is \(\mathbf{S} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)} = {(3, 3)}^{\top}\), and \(\mathbf{S}^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1/3 \end{bmatrix}\), so the Newton step is \(-\mathbf{S}^{-1} {(3, 3)}^{\top} = -{(3, 1)}^{\top}\) and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(1)} &= {(3, 1)}^{\top} - {(3, 1)}^{\top} \\
&= \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_{2 \times 1},
\end{aligned}
\]
the minimizer.










DefDefinition 31 (Gradient descent)






Definition 31 (Gradient descent) Let \(f : \mathbb{R}^p \to \mathbb{R}\) have first partial derivatives, let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)}\) be a starting point, and let \(s^{(0)}, s^{(1)}, \ldots\) be positive step lengths. Gradient descent [17] computes, for \(t = 0, 1, 2, \ldots\),
\[
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t+1)} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)} - s^{(t)}\,\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)}).
\]










ExmExample 46 (Fixed steps on a parabola)






Example 46 (Fixed steps on a parabola) For \(f(x) = x^2\), \(f'(x) = 2x\), so gradient descent with fixed step length \(s\) is
\[
\begin{aligned}
x^{(t+1)} &= x^{(t)} - 2 s\,x^{(t)} \\
&= (1 - 2s)\,x^{(t)},
\end{aligned}
\]
and \(x^{(t)} = (1 - 2s)^t x^{(0)}\).

With \(s = 0.25\), \(x^{(t)} = 0.5^t x^{(0)}\), which tends to the minimizer \(0\).
With \(s = 0.5\), the first step lands on \(0\), since \(1 - 2s = 0\).
With \(s = 1.5\), \(x^{(t)} = (-2)^t x^{(0)}\), which moves away from \(0\) for any \(x^{(0)} \ne 0\): too long a step can make gradient descent fail.











RemarkRemark 4 (Gradient descent as Newton’s method with a simpler Hessian)






Remark 4 (Gradient descent as Newton’s method with a simpler Hessian). Gradient descent (Definition 31) is Newton’s method (Definition 27) with the Hessian replaced by \(\tfrac{1}{s^{(t)}} \mathbf{I}_p\), so it needs no second derivatives.










DefDefinition 32 (Gradient ascent)






Definition 32 (Gradient ascent) Let \(f : \mathbb{R}^p \to \mathbb{R}\) have first partial derivatives, let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)}\) be a starting point, and let \(s^{(0)}, s^{(1)}, \ldots\) be positive step lengths. Gradient ascent [17] repeats, for \(t = 0, 1, 2, \ldots\), the update
\[
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t+1)} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)} + s^{(t)}\,\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)}).
\]










ExmExample 47 (Gradient ascent is gradient descent on the negative function)






Example 47 (Gradient ascent is gradient descent on the negative function) Gradient ascent on \(f\) takes the same steps as gradient descent on \(-f\), since \(\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} (-f) = -\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} f\). In one variable, for \(f(x) = -x^2\) the derivative is \(f'(x) = -2x\), so gradient ascent with fixed step length \(s\) is \(x^{(t+1)} = (1 - 2s)\,x^{(t)}\), the iteration of Example 46, which tends to the maximizer \(0\) when \(0 &lt; s &lt; 1\).










DefDefinition 33 (Line search)






Definition 33 (Line search) In gradient descent (Definition 31), a line search [18] is a rule that chooses the step length \(s^{(t)}\) by examining the values of \(f\) along the line \(\mathopen{}\left\{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)} - s\,\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)}) : s &gt; 0\right\}\mathclose{}\).










DefDefinition 34 (Exact line search)






Definition 34 (Exact line search) Exact line search is the line search (Definition 33) that chooses \(s^{(t)}\) to minimize \(f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)} - s\,\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)}))\) over \(s &gt; 0\).










ExmExample 48 (Exact line search on a parabola)






Example 48 (Exact line search on a parabola) For \(f(x) = x^2\) and \(x^{(t)} = 2\), the derivative is \(f'(2) = 4\), so the line is \(\mathopen{}\left\{2 - 4s : s &gt; 0\right\}\mathclose{}\) and the values along it are
\[
\begin{aligned}
\phi(s)
&= f(2 - 4s)
&& \text{(the value at step length } s \text{)}
\\&= (2 - 4s)^2
&& (f(x) = x^2)
\end{aligned}
\]
\(\phi(s) \ge 0\) for every \(s\), and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\phi(0.5) &= (2 - 2)^2 \\
&= 0,
\end{aligned}
\]
so exact line search chooses \(s^{(t)} = 0.5\), which lands on the minimizer \(x^{(t+1)} = 0\) in one step.










DefDefinition 35 (Descent direction)






Definition 35 (Descent direction) Let \(f : \mathbb{R}^p \to \mathbb{R}\) have a gradient \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})\) at \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\). A direction \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}}\) (direction) is a descent direction for \(f\) at \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) if \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} &lt; 0\). When \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_{p \times 1}\), \(-\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}\) is a descent direction, since \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \cdot (-\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}) =-\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2 &lt; 0\).










ExmExample 49 (Descent directions for \(x_1^2 + x_2^2\) at \({(1, 0)}^{\top}\))






Example 49 (Descent directions for \(x_1^2 + x_2^2\) at \({(1, 0)}^{\top}\)) Let \(f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) = x_1^2 + x_2^2\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= {(1, 0)}^{\top}\), so
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} &= {(2 x_1,\ 2 x_2)}^{\top} \\
&= {(2, 0)}^{\top}.
\end{aligned}
\]


\(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} = {(-1, 1)}^{\top}\) is a descent direction:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} &= -2 + 0 \\
&= -2 \\
&&lt; 0.
\end{aligned}
\]

\(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} = {(0, 1)}^{\top}\) is not: \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{d}}\vphantom{d}} = 0\).
\(-\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} = {(-2, 0)}^{\top}\) is: \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \cdot (-\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}) = -4 &lt; 0\).











LemTheorem 15 (A short enough step downhill decreases the function)






Theorem 15 (A short enough step downhill decreases the function) Let \(f : \mathbb{R}^p \to \mathbb{R}\) have first partial derivatives that are continuous on \(\mathbb{R}^p\) (continuity), and let \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\in \mathbb{R}^p\) be a point with \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_{p \times 1}\). Then there is an \(\bar{s} &gt; 0\) with \(f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}- s \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}) &lt; f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})\) for every \(s \in (0, \bar{s})\): a short enough step in the descent direction \(-\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}\) (Definition 35) decreases \(f\).










ProofProof






Proof. Let \(h(s) \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}- s \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}})\). The inner function \(s \mapsto \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}- s \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}\) has derivative \(-{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}}^{\top}\) (vector-valued derivative), so by the vector chain rule (vector chain rule)
\[
\begin{aligned}
h'(s)
&= -{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}}^{\top}\,\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}- s \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}),
&& \text{(chain rule)}
\\
h'(0)
&= -{\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}}^{\top}\,\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})
&& \text{(set } s = 0 \text{)}
\\
&= -\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \cdot \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}
&& \text{(definition of } \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \text{)}
\\
&= -\mathopen{}\left\lVert\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}\right\rVert\mathclose{}^2
&& \text{(squared length)}
\\
&&lt; 0.
&& \text{(} \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}} \ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_{p \times 1} \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
Since \(h'(0) = \lim_{s \to 0} (h(s) - h(0)) / s\) is negative, taking \(\varepsilon= \mathopen{}\left|h'(0)\right|\mathclose{}\) in the definition of the limit gives an \(\bar{s} &gt; 0\) such that \((h(s) - h(0)) / s &lt; h'(0) + \mathopen{}\left|h'(0)\right|\mathclose{} = 0\) for \(0 &lt; s &lt; \bar{s}\). Multiplying by \(s &gt; 0\), \(h(s) &lt; h(0)\), that is, \(f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}- s \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{g}}\vphantom{g}}) &lt; f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})\).










ExmExample 50 (How short is short enough)






Example 50 (How short is short enough) In Example 46, \(f(x) = x^2\) at \(x \ne 0\) has \(g = 2x\), and \(f(x - 2 s x) = (1 - 2s)^2 x^2 &lt; x^2\) exactly when \(\mathopen{}\left|1 - 2s\right|\mathclose{} &lt; 1\), that is, when \(0 &lt; s &lt; 1\). So Theorem 15 holds with \(\bar{s} = 1\), and the step \(s = 1.5\) of that example was too long.










ExmExample 51 (Gradient descent zigzags)






Example 51 (Gradient descent zigzags) Let \(f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) = \tfrac{1}{2} (x_1^2 + b\,x_2^2)\) with \(b &gt; 0\), so \(\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) = {(x_1,\ b x_2)}^{\top}\) and one gradient step of length \(s\) from \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) gives \({((1 - s) x_1,\ (1 - b s) x_2)}^{\top}\). For exact line search (Definition 34), let \(\phi(s) \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}f\) at that point:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\phi(s)
&= \tfrac{1}{2} \mathopen{}\left((1 - s)^2 x_1^2 + b (1 - b s)^2 x_2^2\right)\mathclose{},
&& \text{(definition of } f \text{)}
\\
\phi'(s)
&= -(1 - s) x_1^2 - b^2 (1 - b s) x_2^2
&& \text{(chain rule, term by term)}
\\
&= -x_1^2 + s\,x_1^2 - b^2 x_2^2 + b^3 s\,x_2^2
&& \text{(expand the products)}
\\
&= s\,(x_1^2 + b^3 x_2^2) - (x_1^2 + b^2 x_2^2).
&& \text{(collect the terms in } s \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
For \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\ne \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_{2 \times 1}\), \(x_1^2 + b^3 x_2^2 &gt; 0\), so \(\phi'\) is increasing in \(s\) with a single zero, and \(\phi\) is minimized at \(s^* = \dfrac{x_1^2 + b^2 x_2^2}{x_1^2 + b^3 x_2^2}\). From \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)} = {(b, 1)}^{\top}\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
s^{(0)}
&= \frac{b^2 + b^2}{b^2 + b^3}
&& \text{(substitute } x_1 = b, x_2 = 1 \text{)}
\\
&= \frac{2}{1 + b},
&& \text{(cancel } b^2 \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
and, with \(r \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}\tfrac{b - 1}{b + 1}\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
1 - s^{(0)}
&= \frac{1 + b - 2}{1 + b} \\
&= r,
&& \text{(common denominator)}
\\
1 - b\,s^{(0)}
&= \frac{1 + b - 2b}{1 + b} \\
&= -r,
&& \text{(common denominator)}
\\
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(1)}
&= {\mathopen{}\left(r\,b,\ -r \cdot 1\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top} \\
&= r\,{(b, -1)}^{\top}.
&& \text{(the gradient step from } {(b, 1)}^{\top} \text{)}
\end{aligned}
\]
For \(b \ne 1\) the same holds at every step, by induction (when \(b = 1\), \(r = 0\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(1)}\) is already the minimizer): if \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)} = {(c\,b,\ \pm c)}^{\top}\) for a number \(c \ne 0\), then \(s^*\), which depends only on \(x_1^2 = c^2 b^2\) and \(x_2^2 = c^2\), is again \(\tfrac{2}{1 + b}\) (the factor \(c^2\) cancels), and the step multiplies the first entry by \(r\) and the second by \(-r\). So
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)}
&= {\mathopen{}\left(b\,r^t,\ (-r)^t\right)\mathclose{}}^{\top}, \\
f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(t)})
&= \tfrac{1}{2} (b^2 + b)\,r^{2t} \\
&= r^{2t} f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(0)}).
\end{aligned}
\]
For \(b \ne 1\), exactly one entry changes sign at every step: the second when \(b &gt; 1\) (then \(r &gt; 0\)), and the first when \(b &lt; 1\) (then \(r &lt; 0\)). So the iterates zigzag toward the minimizer \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{0}}\vphantom{0}}_{2 \times 1}\).

With \(b = 3\), \(r = \tfrac{1}{2}\): \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(1)} = {(1.5, -0.5)}^{\top}\) and \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}^{(2)} = {(0.75, 0.25)}^{\top}\), and \(f\) falls from \(6\) to \(1.5\) to \(0.375\), a quarter at each step.
With \(b = 1\), \(r = 0\) and one step reaches the minimizer.
With \(b = 0.01\), \(r^2 = (0.99 / 1.01)^2 \approx 0.96\), so \(f\) shrinks by only about \(4\%\) per step. Newton’s method reaches the minimizer of any such \(f\) in one step (Theorem 14).











DefDefinition 36 (Rosenbrock function)






Definition 36 (Rosenbrock function) The Rosenbrock function on \(\mathbb{R}^2\) is
\[
f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}) \stackrel{\text{def}}{=}100\mathopen{}\left(x_2 - x_1^2\right)\mathclose{}^2 + \mathopen{}\left(1 - x_1\right)\mathclose{}^2,
\qquad \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}= {(x_1, x_2)}^{\top}.
\]
It is a standard test problem for optimizers [19].










ExmExample 52 (Values of the Rosenbrock function)






Example 52 (Values of the Rosenbrock function) We evaluate the Rosenbrock function at the minimizer \((1, 1)\), at the origin, and at the standard starting point \((-1.2, 1)\). Then we evaluate it along the curve \(x_2 = x_1^2\), which is the floor of a narrow, curved valley.


R
Python




rosenbrock_rf &lt;- function(x1, x2) 100 * (x2 - x1^2)^2 + (1 - x1)^2

points_rf &lt;- list(minimizer = c(1, 1), origin = c(0, 0), start = c(-1.2, 1))
sapply(points_rf, function(p) round(rosenbrock_rf(p[1], p[2]), 3))
#&gt; minimizer    origin     start 
#&gt;       0.0       1.0      24.2

# On the curve x2 = x1^2 the first term vanishes, leaving (1 - x1)^2.
x_curve_rf &lt;- c(-1, 0, 0.5, 1, 2)
rbind(
  on_curve = rosenbrock_rf(x_curve_rf, x_curve_rf^2),
  formula = (1 - x_curve_rf)^2
)
#&gt;          [,1] [,2] [,3] [,4] [,5]
#&gt; on_curve    4    1 0.25    0    1
#&gt; formula     4    1 0.25    0    1




import numpy as np

def rosenbrock_rf(x1, x2):
    return 100 * (x2 - x1 ** 2) ** 2 + (1 - x1) ** 2

points_rf = {"minimizer": (1, 1), "origin": (0, 0), "start": (-1.2, 1)}
print({name: round(rosenbrock_rf(*p), 3) for name, p in points_rf.items()})
#&gt; {'minimizer': 0, 'origin': 1, 'start': 24.2}

# On the curve x2 = x1^2 the first term vanishes, leaving (1 - x1)^2.
x_curve_rf = np.array([-1.0, 0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0])
print("on_curve:", rosenbrock_rf(x_curve_rf, x_curve_rf ** 2))
#&gt; on_curve: [4.   1.   0.25 0.   1.  ]
print("formula: ", (1 - x_curve_rf) ** 2)
#&gt; formula:  [4.   1.   0.25 0.   1.  ]




The function is \(0\) at \((1, 1)\) and positive everywhere else, because both of its parts are non-negative and vanish together only when \(x_1 = 1\) and \(x_2 = 1\). So \((1, 1)\) is the global minimizer. At the starting point the value is 24.2, mostly from the first term, because \((-1.2, 1)\) is off the curve \(x_2 = x_1^2\). On the curve the function is \((1 - x_1)^2\), which varies slowly compared with the steep walls.










