DefDefinition 38 (Plateau)






Definition 38 (Plateau) Let \(f : \mathbb{R}^p \to \mathbb{R}\) be a function with a gradient at every point of a region, and let \(\epsilon &gt; 0\) be a tolerance. The region is a plateau of \(f\) if the Euclidean norm of the gradient satisfies
\[
0 &lt; \mathopen{}\left\lVert\frac{\partial}{\partial \mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}} f(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}})\right\rVert\mathclose{} &lt; \epsilon
\]
at every point \(\mathord{\smash[b]{\underset{\raise.3ex\hbox{$\scriptstyle\sim$}}{x}}\vphantom{x}}\) of the region. The function is then nearly flat on the region, and the region contains no stationary point.










ExmExample 54 (A cost with no stationary point)






Example 54 (A cost with no stationary point)  

Let \(f(x) = \log\mathopen{}\left(1 + \text{e}^{-x}\right)\mathclose{}\). Its derivative is \(\frac{\partial}{\partial x} f(x) = -1 / (1 + \text{e}^{x})\), which is negative for every \(x\), so \(f\) has no stationary point at all.
At \(x = 0\) the gradient has size \(0.5\). At \(x = 10\) it has size \(4.54\times 10^{-5}\), which is small but not zero. A step of gradient descent subtracts the gradient times the step length. The gradient is negative, so the iterates keep moving to the right, more and more slowly. With tolerance \(\epsilon = 10^{-4}\), the region \(x \ge 10\) is a plateau (Definition 38). A run that enters this plateau has not converged; the run has only slowed down.










